{"id": "arxiv-sweep/reader18-fullreads/p2609_10615.txt", "corpus": "arxiv-sweep", "kind": "note", "chars": 341403, "equations": [], "text": "ACT, WAIT, or EXPERIMENT: A Causal Governance\nFramework for Retail Price Optimization Under\nAbstentions\n\narXiv:2609.10615v1 [econ.EM] 8 Sep 2026\n\nPedro Cadahia Delgado∗\nPhD, Universidad de Huelva, Spain\nMethodological study · Causal Pricing and Governance in Retail · 2026\n\nAbstract\nIn intermediated retail channels, estimating consumer price elasticity from wholesale list prices is\nconfounded by retail pass-through, promotions, competitor movements, and local market frictions.\nRather than focusing solely on point estimation, this paper introduces a unified causal governance\nframework under which decision abstention (wait) is treated as a primary, diagnostic output rather than\nan estimation failure. Grounded in the companion theory of design-based uncertainty (Delgado, 2026 ),\nwe show that conventional within-panel bootstrap intervals suffer severe empirical subcoverage on this\npanel and that a hierarchical variance-component construction using Paule-Mandel estimation closes most\nof the gap; that construction is reported as validated rather than adopted, and every figure in this paper\nis produced under the bootstrap the implementation currently runs.\nWe present an act/wait decision system that combines Double Machine Learning, cost-shock\ninstrumental contrasts, and conformal prediction to decouple weekly panel estimation from monthly\ndecisions. The system evaluates recommendations through an admissibility gate and eight guards, jointly\npartitioning ten abstention causes, with the structural-break test (Guard 5) operating as a passive temporal\ndrift sensor rather than an active veto, and the asymmetric Dual Beta estimator retired due to physical\nnoise floor limits. The decision layer partitions all exit reasons exhaustively into three mutually exclusive\nverdict types under a fixed precedence: wait-contamination (GCONT ), wait-transient (GTRANS ), and\nwait-identification (GID ). The third is the operationally consequential one, as it marks a product\npresentation as a candidate for an actively designed pricing experiment rather than a terminal rejection.\nThis paper makes three main contributions: (1) an operational framework converting selective\nprediction into an active pricing diagnostic, leaving no presentation in an unclassified terminal state; (2)\ntransforming seven tacit econometric assumptions into active system diagnostics; and (3) showing that\naggregating unidentifiable presentation-level items to brand or category levels restores usable elasticity\nestimates (reducing RMSE from 0.571 to 0.159 against a true magnitude of 1.1).\nThe system is tested against synthetic data-generating processes with known ground truth under\nthirty pre-registered engineering rules (11 pass, 18 fail, 1 not applicable); it has not been run against a\ncommercial panel, and no result reported here is an observation of a real product category. Informative\nfailures show that component discipline matters more than algorithmic complexity. On identifiable panels,\nthe family-wise false-veto rate of the whole harness is estimated at 0.053, one veto among nineteen\nindependently seeded replications. Given the thin data typical of retail channels, reporting when evidence\nsupports—or fails to support—a decision offers a practical and secure alternative to uncritical estimation.\nKeywords: Price optimization; causal inference; double machine learning; decision under abstention;\ndemand systems; intermediated retail channels.\n\n∗ Corresponding author.\n\n1\n\n\f1\n\nIntroduction\n\nA manufacturer selling through decentralized retail sets the list price and does not set the price the consumer\npays. The shopkeeper does, subject to local margin targets, menu costs and rigid psychological price points\n[Levy et al., 2011]. Any elasticity estimated from list-price variation in this channel is therefore a composite:\nthe consumer’s response to the shelf price convolved with the shopkeeper’s pass-through of the list price\nto the shelf [Besanko et al., 2005]. The operational problem is nonetheless posed at the list price, since\nthat is the only lever the manufacturer holds: set monthly list prices, bounded by ±20%, so as to maximize\ncontribution without sacrificing volume or share.\nNaive regressions of volume on historical prices fail here because they confound the causal slope with\npromotions, competitor moves, seasonality and nominal inflationary drift [Villas-Boas and Winer, 1999].\nSince an optimizer is a causal intervention machine [Pearl, 2009], the binding problem in this channel is\nnot optimization but the isolation of the slope the optimizer consumes. The revenue-management literature\nand the enterprise deployments it documents [Talluri and van Ryzin, 2006, Hormby et al., 2010, Deng et al.,\n2023, Llenas et al., 2026] largely take the observational price–demand relation as sufficient for prescription.\nDecentralized Retail violates that premise through three structural conditions: scarce list-price variation\nconfounded with promotion, indirect and heterogeneous pass-through, and shelf prices captured by audit\nrather than by transaction. Section 2 situates that gap against six bodies of literature this work draws on.\nFrom estimation to decision. Most of that literature, and most practice built on it, treats the object of\ninterest as a number: an elasticity to be estimated as precisely as possible and handed to an optimizer. This\npaper treats it instead as an input to a decision that is not always safe to make. Three questions organize\nwhat follows, and they replace “what is the elasticity” as the paper’s central question.\n1. When can a price change be executed on the estimate, and when should the system\nwithhold a recommendation instead? This is the ACT/WAIT question. Treating WAIT as a\nfirst-class, reportable output, rather than as evidence of a system that failed to produce a number, is\nthe paper’s first contribution (Section 4.4); Section 8.3 shows that a WAIT verdict is also diagnostic of\nthe remedy it calls for, not only a report that one is needed.\n2. What conditions, on the data and on the estimator, have to be made explicit and checked\nbefore an ACT verdict is trusted? Answering this converts assumptions such systems ordinarily\nleave tacit into diagnostics the system executes rather than presumes. That conversion, together with a\ndeclared account of which diagnostics adapt an existing econometric tool and which are original to this\nsetting, is the paper’s second contribution (Section 8.1).\n3. At what level of commercial aggregation, presentation, brand or category, is a decision\nlegitimate when the evidence does not support it at the level the business would prefer to\nact on? Showing when and how far the unit of decision should move is the paper’s third contribution\n(Section 6.4).\nThis paper reports a system built to answer those three questions in one specific setting and, more consequentially, the validation run that measured whether it does. The system decouples weekly panel estimation\nfrom a monthly decision cycle and chains Double Machine Learning [Chernozhukov et al., 2018], a cost-shock\ninstrumental contrast, hierarchical pooling, conformal predictive intervals, and a decision layer that abstains\nwhen identification is unviable. It is validated against two synthetic generators with known ground truth\nunder thirty engineering-verification rules registered before the run. These thirty rules are a quality-assurance\ndevice internal to the validation run, each pairing a claim the manuscript makes with a threshold that would\nsettle it (Section 5); they are not the paper’s scientific hypotheses, which are the three contributions listed\nabove. Eleven of the thirty rules pass, eighteen fail, and one does not apply, and several of the failures are\ninformative findings in their own right rather than simple defects.\nTwo further results support the third contribution and the abstention rule together. Aggregating the estimate\nfrom the presentation to the brand or the category level substantially improves its reliability, because designspecific bias among independently priced presentations partly averages out: a category-level estimate reaches\na root mean squared error of 0.159 against a true elasticity of magnitude 1.1, where a presentation-level one\n2\n\n\freaches 0.571 (Section 6.4). And on a panel this thin, a resampled interval computed within one realized\ndesign cannot always be certified as honest, because it cannot see the dispersion that would arise from having\nobserved a different set of price movements altogether; that limitation, not a deficiency of estimation, is why\nsome presentations, at some levels of aggregation, are correctly answered with WAIT rather than with a\nnumber (Section 6.5). This paper motivates that limitation only to the extent needed to justify the abstention\nrule; its full econometric treatment, the decomposition of the shortfall, how it scales with aggregation, and\nthe case for closing it experimentally, is developed in companion methodological work [Delgado, 2026] rather\nthan here.\nThat evidentiary shape is not peculiar to this system or this sponsor. A hundred and twenty weekly periods, a\nhandful of presentations, list prices that move a few times a year and a shelf price captured by audit describe\nthe evidence available to most manufacturers in this channel. Reporting when such evidence does, and does\nnot, support a decision is more useful than reporting a system that always appears to clear it.\nThe methodological claim the paper defends is broader than pricing. The failure modes that survive a\ncompetent build are rarely errors of estimation; they are unstated assumptions about the environment, and\neach can be converted into a quantity that costs less to measure than its consequence costs to absorb. Four\nsuch assumptions were found by auditing the design and three more by executing the validation. Table 4\nstates all seven with the contrast each became and, for each, whether the test adapts an existing tool or is\noriginal to this work.\nThe remainder is organized as follows. Section 2 situates the three contributions against the literature and\nstates the gap. Section 3 describes the institutional setting and the panel. Section 4 gives a global view of\nthe system that answers the first two questions above, with formal specifications deferred to Appendix A and\nthe extended exposition to Appendix B. Section 5 states the validation design. Section 6 reports what the\nrun measured, with extended contrasts in Appendix C. Section 7 reports robustness checks and ablations.\nSection 8 draws the governance and deployment consequences, and Section 9 closes.\n\n2\n\nRelated Work and the Gap\n\nThis work sits at the intersection of six literatures, and states plainly, at the outset, what none of them by\nitself supplies.\nStructural demand estimation in industrial organization [Berry, 1994, Berry et al., 1995, Nevo, 2001]\nidentifies substitution patterns and market power from aggregate market shares under strong assumptions\nabout functional form and instrument availability; it targets the estimate itself and is agnostic about when a\nfirm should act on it. Vertical pass-through [Villas-Boas, 2007, Nakamura and Zerom, 2010] documents\nthat retailers rarely transmit a wholesale price change to the shelf one-for-one, which motivates this paper’s\ntreatment of pass-through as a process rather than a constant (Section 4.3), but does not address how\na manufacturer without transaction-level shelf data should decide whether to move a price at all. Nonexchangeable conformal inference [Gibbs and Candès, 2021, Barber et al., 2023] supplies the machinery\nthis paper uses to bound the volume response under a chronological, non-stationary split (Section 4.2); it is\na tool the framework adopts, not a decision rule in itself. Selective prediction and learning-to-defer\n[El-Yaniv and Wiener, 2010, Mozannar and Sontag, 2020] formalize the general principle that a system can\ndecline to answer rather than answer badly, and the ACT/WAIT gate is an instance of that principle applied\nto a pricing decision; the contribution here is the instantiation, the declared diagnostics that trigger abstention\nin this setting, not the general principle. Multiway panel clustering [Chiang et al., 2022] and the broader\ninference literature it belongs to inform the resampling and clustering choices audited in Section 4.1. And\nperformative prediction [Perdomo et al., 2020], alongside the general critique that a relation estimated\nunder one policy regime need not survive a new one [Lucas, 1976, MacKenzie, 2006], names the risk that\nonce this system moves prices, the observational identification it relies on will erode; Section B.12 treats that\nrisk as a governance problem this paper flags rather than solves.\nA seventh, more practically oriented literature sits alongside these six and is worth separating from them:\nenterprise deployments of pricing and revenue-management systems [Talluri and van Ryzin, 2006, Marn and\nRosiello, 1992, Hormby et al., 2010, Deng et al., 2023, Llenas et al., 2026]. These report what a deployed\n3\n\n\fsystem achieved, typically a revenue or margin gain, and are the closest thing to a track record this literature\nhas for whether firms use such systems at all and what they get from them. They are also, for the purpose\nof this paper, the sharpest illustration of the gap: none of them reports which of the system’s identifying\nassumptions were checked, whether the reported gain would survive an audit of confounding, or at what\nlevel of aggregation the underlying estimates were trustworthy. The comparison is scoped to the three cited\nhere and is not offered as a survey finding, but it is a repeated pattern and not a coincidence: a literature\norganized around outcomes achieved has little occasion to report on evidence that was insufficient, because a\nsystem that only ever reports what it recommended has no channel through which to report having declined\nto recommend anything. The adjacent literature on algorithm aversion [Dietvorst et al., 2015, 2018] suggests\nwhy that omission is costly rather than harmless: trust in an automated recommendation is fragile once the\nsystem has been seen to err, and is rebuilt by giving the user visible, legible control over an imperfect system,\nof which a declared and costed abstention is the clearest form.\nThe gap. None of the seven literatures above treats the decision to abstain as a first-class object with its\nown diagnostics, its own measured cost, and its own conditions for reversal; the closest, selective prediction,\nformalizes the principle in a general classification setting and does not address what the relevant diagnostics\nare in an observational pricing panel with retail intermediation. None specifies the conditions under which a\ndecision illegitimate at one level of commercial aggregation becomes legitimate at a coarser one, or measures\nhow far aggregation actually closes that gap. And none converts the assumptions a causal pricing system\nordinarily leaves tacit, that controls are pre-treatment, that an instrument acts through one channel, that a\ncomparison group is inert, that no adjacent optimizer touches the same unit, into diagnostics the system\nitself executes and reports. Sections 4.4, 8.1 and 6.4 address the three gaps in that order, each corresponding\nto one of the questions posed in Section 1.\n\n3\n\nInstitutional Setting and Data\n\nThe institutional detail behind the identification problem stated in Section 1 is what makes this channel\nharder than the modern-trade setting most of the causal-pricing and revenue-management literature addresses:\nthe manufacturer’s price is not the consumer’s price, the intermediary between them is a liquidity-constrained\nsmall business rather than a chain with scanner data, and the panel available to measure any of it is short by\nthe standards of that literature. This section describes the panel this paper’s system runs on, before Section 4\ndescribes what the system does with it.\nThe valuation dataset is a weekly panel at the presentation × region × week level, integrating sell-in, marketaudit sell-out, distributor point-of-sale, competitor pricing, promotional schedules and variable costs, with\nweather and holiday covariates. Gap imputation and publication rules belong to an upstream data contract:\ngap imputation by forward fill, capped at two weeks and carrying an indicator flag, together with non-null\nconstraints, plausible price–volume bounds, price–cost coherence and weekly partitioning. The system neither\ngenerates nor repairs missing imputations, and validates only a diagnostic alert for week-over-week volume\ndrops above 80% within a cell, as a proxy for unmarked stockouts; that alert is specified here and executable\nexclusively against a production panel, so it is not exercised by any result in this paper. The boundary\nis methodologically load-bearing rather than administrative: if imputation flags concentrate at month-end\namong smaller retailers, forward fill is masking order refusals rather than random missingness, which is\nevidence for the extensive-margin mechanism of Appendix B.8 rather than a capture defect.\nThe atomic unit is the presentation (brand × format family × pack size), not the SKU: list prices are\nassigned per presentation, so intra-presentation SKU variation carries no identifying price variance. The\nbrand grounds the pooling hierarchy, the format family supplies the instrumental contrasts, and the pack\nsize governs consumption occasions and price stickiness. The framework organizes around the structural\nhierarchy Category > Brand > Presentation rather than around any category domain, and assumes a\nportfolio architecture with multiple brands, distinct pack sizes, uninstrumented competitor prices and retail\nintermediation. The generators of Section 5 instantiate one portfolio exhibiting that topology and the\nsystem is shown to run end to end on it; transfer to other portfolios sharing the structure remains a design\nexpectation rather than a verified property. This hierarchy is also what Section 6.4 later aggregates over\nwhen presentation-level evidence is not enough to decide.\n4\n\n\fThe week is the temporal unit of inference throughout: cross-fitting folds, block bootstrap, conformal splits,\nbreak tests and recency windows all operate on weekly boundaries, while the month governs only the decision\ncadence. That division is an identification decision and not a convenience. Under monthly aggregation\nthe quota-pressure guard loses its contrast identically, the calendar component of the cash-pressure index\ndisappears, and the break tests fall from a hundred and twenty observations to twenty-eight. The one genuine\nargument for the monthly grain, that it would cancel forward-buying bias within the observation, is already\nanswered by taking sell-out rather than sell-in as the outcome. Appendix B.2 develops the argument in full.\nFeasibility and admissibility. A presentation × region cell is modelable when five joint conditions hold\non deflated prices: a coefficient of variation above 0.03; at least four discrete price changes (|∆ log p| > 0.005);\nan absolute price–promotion correlation below 0.6; an absolute price–competitor correlation below 0.9; and\nnon-frozen shelf status, defined conjunctively as CV(P so ) ≥ 0.01 together with at most 5% zero-volume weeks.\nA minimum of forty usable observations applies. A presentation is then admissible when the diagnostic\npasses in at least half its regions, the treatment partial R2 after residualization exceeds 0.10, and the bootstrap\ninterval width for the raw elasticity falls below 0.6. Admissibility is evaluated before the guards, so a failing\npresentation reaches WAIT without a guard being evaluated, and it conditions conformal calibration and\nQA-Gate scoring. Non-modelable units inherit a brand-level pooled elasticity and are marked non-actionable.\nAppendix B.3 states the diagnostic in full, including the reduced-form competitor reaction function r̂ that\nmakes explicit which estimand the competitive bracket contrasts. These two thresholds, jointly with the\neight guards of Section 4.4 and the ten abstention causes they define, are the diagnostics referred to in the\nsecond question of Section 1: Table 4 and Section 8.1 state which of them adapt an existing tool and which\nare original to this setting.\n\n4\n\nMethodology\n\nThe system executes a sequential, artifact-driven process: diagnosis, causal identification, hierarchical pooling,\nconformal guarantees, and decision optimization. Table 1 states each layer in one line together with the\nverdict the validation run returned on it, and the subsections below develop the argument each layer rests on.\nFormal specifications and operating parameters are in Appendix A, and the extended exposition from which\nthis section is condensed is in Appendix B.\n\n5\n\n\fFigure 1: Schematic overview of the system. Solid arrows represent primary algorithmic flow, dashed arrows\ndenote control and feedback mechanisms, and the two terminal verdicts are distinguished by outline: solid for\nACT, dashed for WAIT.\n\n6\n\n\fTable 1: The system in one line per layer, with the verdict the validation run returned. The right-hand\ncolumn is the paper’s organizing discipline applied to its own architecture: a layer is reported as validated\nonly where an experiment measured its claim against a known truth.\nLayer\n\nWhat it does\n\nVerdict of the run\n\nVariation\ndiagnostic\nand admissibility gate\n\nFive cutoffs on observable variation\nplus three on the estimate; routes nonidentifiable cells to WAIT\nPartialling-out with contiguous block\ncross-fitting; estimand θ = ερ\n\nSeparates cleanly on the configuration it was\ncalibrated on; precision falls to 0.000 once evaluated outside it (§6.7)\nRecovers θ in the identifiable regime at a bias\nof 0.143; does not degrade gracefully elsewhere\n(§6.1)\nExclusion likely violated on this panel; remedy\nis data acquisition, not modeling\n\nCausal\n(DML)\n\nidentification\n\nInstrumental contrast\nand exclusion audit\nHierarchical pooling\n\nCost shocks as instrument; Hausmantype contrast with a residualcorrelation audit\nNormal–normal shrinkage over Category > Brand > Presentation\n\nConformal layer\n\nCQR, Mondrian by region, chronological split\n\nDemand system (LAAIDS on residuals)\nPass-through layer\n\nCross-elasticities identified through the\nsame residualization kernel\nρ as a process with states; deconvolution ε = θ/ρ\n\nOptimizer\n\nExact arg max of contribution over a\ndiscrete corridor\nFour-way verdict per presentation\n(ACT / wait-cont / wait-trans /\nwait-id), voting on the raw estimate\nHoldout, synthetic control, permutation inference, contamination test\n\nDecision layer (gate\n+ eight guards, ten\ncauses)\nExperimental layer\n\n4.1\n\nImproves the point estimate; its interval properties relative to the bootstrap are treated in\ncompanion work (§6.3)\nThe one uncertainty component whose measured behaviour matches its claim, verified\nout-of-time at rolling origins (§6.2)\nHomogeneity and symmetry each rejected in\n0.625 of replications (§7)\nEstimable and well recovered (ρ̂ bias −0.003);\nimmaterial to the decision, at ≤ 0.6% of band\nvariance\nNot the binding component; deterministic and\nglobal by construction\nFamily-wise false-veto rate 0.053 on an identifiable panel (N = 19, §6.8); Guard 5 withdrawn\nas not operative\nCalibrated between twelve and thirty independently assigned units (§7)\n\nCausal identification\n\nEstimation is the partialling-out estimator with cross-fitting from Double/Debiased Machine Learning\n[Chernozhukov et al., 2018] applied to a partially linear model [Robinson, 1988] and grounded in Frisch–\nWaugh–Lovell [Frisch and Waugh, 1933, Lovell, 1963]. Gradient-boosted learners [Friedman, 2001] model\nthe joint dependence of treatment T = log P si and outcome Y = log Qso on a confounder set W carrying\npromotional flags and intensity, log competitor prices, temperature, holidays, working days, week-of-year and\nlog inflation, extensible to distribution, out-of-stock, media and trade investment where the panel supplies\nthem.\nThe estimand requires one statement that is often left implicit. The coefficient of the residualized outcome on\nthe residualized treatment is not the structural consumer elasticity: it is the convolution θ = ε · ρ of consumer\nresponse with shopkeeper pass-through. Separating the two is the job of the pass-through layer, not of a\nbetter nuisance model, and comparing θ̂ against an injected consumer elasticity would report transmission as\nestimation bias.\nFour properties of the construction carry consequences the run measures. (i) Cross-fitting uses K = 5\ncontiguous temporal blocks [Bergmeir and Benítez, 2012]; this is not forward-chaining and relies on stationarity\nand weak dependence [Chiang et al., 2022] rather than on Neyman orthogonality, which concerns moment\nconditions and not information sets. An embargoed variant of that partition is implemented and its coverage\nmeasured, and it is not the one this run uses: adopting it would change the residualizer that two of the\nablations compare, so those contrasts would stop testing what the text says they test. The consequence is\ndeclared rather than absorbed, because it is a leak and not a preference: without the embargo the partition\n\n7\n\n\fcan place a single week on both sides of the split through a different region. (ii) Nuisance learners are shallow\nboosted trees, which are piecewise constant and represent a monotone trend as a staircase, so low-frequency\ncomponents can survive into the residuals. (iii) Inference is a moving-block bootstrap clustered by week\n[Künsch, 1989] with block length L = clip(round(1.5 W 1/3 ), 2, ⌊W/3⌋), giving L = 7 at W = 120. (iv) The\npoint estimate is the median across folds rather than the pooled DML2 aggregate. Each of the four is\ncontrasted against its alternative in Section 7, and two of them do not survive as general recommendations:\nthe choice of nuisance learner reverses between the two generators, and the choice of point estimator reverses\nbetween learners.\nTemporal status and the mediator bracket. Every column of W carries a declared status, pre or\npost, relative to the pricing decision, and estimation aborts on an undeclared control. Conditioning on\npost-treatment realizations blocks causal pathways and changes the estimand [Rosenbaum, 1984, VanderWeele,\n2015], so elasticities are reported with and without post-treatment commercial controls and the differential is\npublished as a diagnostic rather than used to prune automatically. Under the sign structure assumed for this\nchannel, trade activity rising with list price, the indirect path carries the opposite sign to the direct effect, so\nover-conditioning inflates |θ̂|; since the optimizer’s interior optimum carries a markup θ/(1 + θ) falling toward\none as |θ| grows, the failure mode is a systematically conservative recommendation that stays plausible. The\ndirection depends on the assumed sign and not on the estimator, and is declared as conditional for that\nreason.\nTwo structural contrasts. Residualization addresses observed confounders only, so two contrasts probe\nwhat it cannot. The instrumental contrast uses log variable costs as an instrument for supply-side\nshocks, and the divergence between observational and instrumental estimates is an operational Hausman test\n[Hausman, 1978]. Category-wide cost shocks move competitor prices too, violating the exclusion restriction\nand displacing both estimators together, which understates the contrast [Angrist et al., 1996]; the framework\ntherefore audits the restriction directly through the residual correlation between the residualized instrument\nand the residualized competitor price, with an explicit time index added because a boosted tree partials a\nmonotone drift poorly. The competitive bracket decomposes price response into three declared estimands,\nconditional, historical-equilibrium and cross, and admits the internal identity θtotal − θcond ≈ θcross · r̂. The\nidentity binds only where the competitor moves and reacts, and Section 6.1 establishes on which panel it is\ncontrastable before reporting it. Both are specified formally in Appendices A.5 and A.4.\n\n4.2\n\nRegularization, uncertainty and the demand system\n\nThe causal layer returns one slope per presentation with an interval, and three problems remain that no\nsingle-unit estimator solves: thin panels borrow strength or return noise, a point estimate is not a bound on\nthe volume a committee will observe, and an own-price elasticity says nothing about what the rest of the\nportfolio does.\nPartial pooling. The hierarchy Category > Brand > Presentation shrinks each estimate toward its branch\nthrough a conjugate normal–normal update, with three origins for the prior: a leave-one-out brand mean with\nbetween-presentation heterogeneity τ̂ 2 estimated by Paule–Mandel [Paule and Mandel, 1982]; the previous\ncycle, through a local-level dynamic linear model at discount δ = 0.90 [West and Harrison, 1997], whose\ninformation weight halves in roughly seven cycles; and a cold-start branch prior. Two pitfalls are avoided by\nconstruction, using the sample variance in place of τ̂ 2 and summing raw precisions rather than incorporating\nτ̂ 2 into branch precisions [Efron and Morris, 1975]. The output is a decision-grade elasticity θdec carrying its\nposterior variance, own-data weight and declared origin. Its role is bounded deliberately: the ACT/WAIT\ngate votes solely with the raw estimate θ̂, so pooling can never unlock a movement, while θdec sets the level of\nthe published price and elasticity curves. Appendix A.6 gives the update and two declared approximations.\nThe heterogeneity component and design variance. The between-presentation heterogeneity τ̂ 2 that\nthis layer estimates in order to set the shrinkage weight also measures something a within-panel bootstrap\ncannot: dispersion that comes from the realized design itself, this set of price movements falling in these\n\n8\n\n\fweeks, rather than from sampling noise around it (Section\n√ 6.3 sketches why, and treats the question at the\nlevel this paper needs). An interval of the form θ̂ ± 1.96 s2 + τ̂ 2 is one way to fold that source back into\nthe reported uncertainty; whether, and by how much, it improves on the production bootstrap interval is a\nquestion of its own, developed in companion methodological work [Delgado, 2026] rather than here. The\nchange is identified and not yet adopted in the production configuration, and every figure in this paper is\nproduced under the interval the implementation currently runs.\nOne consequence for this layer is unfavourable and is stated with the rest. Where the raw estimate is\nmis-centred, shrinkage can move the interval toward a displaced branch mean while narrowing it, so the\npooling posterior’s coverage is not guaranteed to improve on the bootstrap it shrinks. Partial pooling is not\nassumed to help the interval merely because it helps the point estimate, and a system that publishes both\nshould say so.\nThe conformal layer. Predictive intervals on log volume are built by Conformalized Quantile Regression\n[Romano et al., 2019], Mondrian-stratified by region [Vovk et al., 2003]. Where a stratum is too sparse for\nlocal quantile estimation the estimator falls back to global calibration, which relaxes the guarantee from\ngroup-conditional to marginal coverage, and each fallback is recorded by a unit-level indicator. Because\nrandom splits in a weekly panel mix past and future and produce optimistic bands, the partition is strictly\nchronological (50/30/20) and evaluated across rolling origins. Non-exchangeability forfeits the finite-sample\nguarantee [Vovk et al., 2005, Lei et al., 2018], so the construction rests on the adaptive conformal argument\nfor non-stationary environments [Gibbs and Candès, 2021, Barber et al., 2023] and on measured out-of-time\ncoverage that clears the nominal 0.90 level at the rolling origins tested (Section 6.2). The band over-covers,\nwhich is the conservative direction and costs width rather than validity, and it bounds the volume response\nonly: price bounds are enforced independently by the competitive corridor, and intersecting the two would\nmix an interval on a response with a bound on a decision variable.\nCross-elasticities. Portfolio decisions require knowing how much presentations cannibalize one another.\nThe system estimates an AIDS demand system [Deaton and Muellbauer, 1980] in its linear approximation\nover revenue shares deflated by the Stone index [Stone, 1954], and the identification correction is the layer’s\ncontribution: standard practice regresses shares on raw prices, which confounds the price effect with everything\nthat moved at the same time and invents spurious substitution. Here the system runs exclusively on the\nresiduals of the same causal model, reusing the single cross-fitting kernel, so by Frisch–Waugh–Lovell the\ncoefficients coincide with those of the full system without inheriting the endogeneity of the observational price.\nThe theoretical restrictions are tested rather than imposed. Section 7 reports the test and it is unfavourable.\nThree declared simplifications, the observed-share Stone deflator [Moschini, 1995], date-level collapse of W ,\nand the amplification of noise by division through small revenue shares, are stated in Appendix A.9.\n\n4.3\n\nIntermediated-Retail extensions\n\nTwo extensions describe a single agent, the shopkeeper who sets the shelf price and who buys under a liquidity\nconstraint.\nPass-through as a process, not a constant. Treating ρ as an implicit constant is the standard error\nin this channel: measured pass-through is neither unitary nor homogeneous [Besanko et al., 2005], and\ntwo presentations with identical observed elasticities under different transmission regimes call for different\ndecisions. The system estimates ρ per presentation from a residualized regression of the log shelf price on\nthe log list price and classifies the regime against two cutoffs, sticky below 0.30 where the shelf is anchored\nat a round point and the list lever loses traction, and margin-defending at or above 0.80. To that level\nit adds a Markov chain over the shopkeeper’s margin state, conditioned on the sign of the list shock, and\nthe deconvolution ε̂ = θ̂/ρ̂, which is reported as an interpretive diagnostic only: decoupling introduces a\nnegative structural covariance, so the optimizer and every band operate directly on θ̂, and ε̂ is undefined\nbelow ρ̂ = 0.30.\nWhat the region × week aggregate does not identify is the discrete rigidity of repricing, the (S, s) behaviour\n[Sheshinski and Weiss, 1977, Nakamura and Steinsson, 2008] in which the shelf jump depends on untransferred\n9\n\n\fcost accumulated since the last change; aggregation smooths it into a falsely gradual pass-through [Caballero\nand Engel, 2007]. A three-layer structural architecture for store-level panels is specified in Appendix A.10\nand not estimated here. One asymmetry in the estimation is declared because a reader will otherwise find it:\nρ is residualized against a reduced confounder set. Excluding the competitor’s list price is defensible, since it\nis not a confounder of the shopkeeper’s own margin decision; excluding the inflation index is not, since it is a\ncommon factor of both prices, and the run measures what the conflation costs (Appendix B.7).\nThe budget constraint of the intermediate buyer. A store may decline or truncate an order for\nreasons unrelated to how well the product sells. The intuitive measurement, the store’s observed outlay with\nthe manufacturer, is built from the treatment and the outcome simultaneously: admitted as a control it\nconverts the elasticity into within-basket substitution at constant expenditure, and recommends a larger\nincrease precisely where substitution room exists. The construct is therefore decomposed into three objects\nwith three entry points: a cash-pressure index built only from sources exogenous to the firm’s own sales,\nadmitted to W with pre status and published with its variance decomposition; total store expenditure,\nwhich belongs in the demand system as the instrumented allocation variable [Blundell and Robin, 1999];\nand the binding state of the constraint, which is an effect modifier and not a control. Order refusal is an\nextensive-margin event requiring a two-part treatment [Cragg, 1971] under selection on the constraint state\n[Heckman, 1979].\nThe status of this frontier is stated exactly, because it is an instance of the paper’s governing principle rather\nthan a gap. One component is implemented, a cheap screening test that regresses portfolio breadth on the\ncash-pressure index and requires |t| ≥ 2 before any further construct is built. Three are specified and not\nestimated, because a region × week panel cannot support them and estimating them badly at this grain\nwould smooth a lumpy discrete process into a mild and false elasticity. What the screen returns on this\ngenerator, and why, is reported in Section 7.\n\n4.4\n\nOptimization and the ACT/WAIT decision layer\n\nThe optimizer maximizes contribution (P − C)Q̂(P ) subject to minimum margin, volume, share and ladder\nconstraints, the operating limit of ±20% on the base list price, and the competitive corridor [P comp (1 −\nκ), P comp (1 + κ)] at κ = 0.10. The feasible set is the intersection of these, the tighter bound governs, and the\nartifact records which one bound. For a single presentation that set is one-dimensional and discrete, since\nprices are set in whole units of the smallest circulating denomination, so exact enumeration delivers the global\noptimum deterministically and the grid is the feasible set rather than a discretization of it; a metaheuristic\nwould add stochastic dependence and buy nothing. Genuine non-convexity appears only in the joint portfolio\nvector, where cross-cannibalization couples decisions, and differential evolution [Storn and Price, 1997] with\nL-BFGS-B polishing [Byrd et al., 1995] is reserved for that scale-up. A Score combining normalized margin\nand volume ranks scenarios for the committee and never defines the optimum, since maximizing a normalized\nscore is not maximizing contribution.\nEvery recommendation travels with three quantities that a committee can adjudicate: the tolerable volume\nloss L = ∆p/(m + ∆p), which is algebraically exact and free of functional-form assumptions; the critical\nelasticity at which moving and holding are indifferent, exact conditional on the log-log specification the\noptimizer itself uses; and a flag stating whether the whole uncertainty interval falls on one side of that\nthreshold. The motivation is empirical: the canonical meta-analysis reports a mean elasticity of −1.76 with a\ndispersion as wide as the mean across 367 estimates [Tellis, 1988], and a later synthesis over 1,851 estimates\ndoes not agree with it in the first digit [Bijmolt et al., 2005]. Publishing an elasticity to two decimals promises\na precision the method cannot deliver; reporting which side of the break-even the whole range falls on delivers\nwhat the decision requires.\nThe decision layer. The decision layer adds no analysis. It converts what has been estimated into one\nverdict per presentation and makes the optimizer act only where identification holds. It is the least common\ncomponent in enterprise pricing systems and the one on which organizational trust turns: reluctance to use\na model intensifies once it has been seen to err [Dietvorst et al., 2015], and is recovered by giving the user\nvisible control over an imperfect system rather than by improving the model [Dietvorst et al., 2018].\n10\n\n\fACT requires passing every operative guard simultaneously. The qualifier is load-bearing: a guard that cannot\nbe evaluated is reported as such rather than counted as passed, so a presentation reaching ACT on seven\nevaluable guards is distinguishable from one reaching ACT on eight. A verdict of WAIT, however, is not a\nsingle state. The decision layer partitions the abstention causes into three mutually exclusive types, and the\npartition is what makes the abstention diagnostic rather than terminal: it states what would have to change\nfor the presentation to become actionable, and in one of the three cases that change is an experiment this\nsystem can specify.\nDefinition 1 (Three-way classification of guard-level abstention causes). The admissibility gate and the\neight guards define ten guard-level abstention causes, since Guard 1 evaluates two conditions separately: a\nregime shift in θ on excluding broken weeks (1a) and an excessive share of weeks so flagged (1b). Let S be\nthe vector of diagnostic outcomes for a presentation in a cycle. The ten causes partition into three disjoint\nsubsets:\n• GID (identification, five causes): failing admissibility; Guard 1a; Guard 1b; Guard 2; Guard 3.\nEach is a precision shortfall, or natural variation contaminated by reactive pricing, that independently\nrandomized perturbation directly resolves.\n• GCONT (contamination, four causes): Guard 4; Guard 6; Guard 7; Guard 8. Guard 4 belongs\nhere rather than with precision because a strong-instrument divergence is direct evidence of residual\nendogeneity in the observational variation itself, by the same logic already applied to Guard 7. Each\nsignals a specification or validity problem that an experiment does not, by itself, repair.\n• GTRANS (transient, one cause): Guard 5. Neither more identifying variation nor a specification fix\naddresses a regime that has just ended; the estimate becomes usable again once the new regime has\naccumulated its own history.\nThe decision layer is the mapping D(S) → {ACT, wait-contamination, wait-transient, wait-identification}\ndefined by\n\nACT,\nGCONT = ∅ ∧ GTRANS = ∅ ∧ GID = ∅,\n\n\n\n\n\nwait-contamination, GCONT ̸= ∅,\nD(S) =\n\n\nwait-transient,\nGCONT = ∅ ∧ GTRANS ̸= ∅,\n\n\n\n\nwait-identification, GCONT = ∅ ∧ GTRANS = ∅ ∧ GID ̸= ∅.\nThe precedence is not a tie-breaking convenience. A single contamination-type cause blocks the experimental\nroute regardless of what else bound, since it signals an assumption violation that independent randomization\nalone does not repair; a recent structural break next makes the current estimate untrustworthy independent\nof its precision; and a presentation whose only binding causes are identification-type is the one for which\nan actively designed price experiment is the correct remedy. The definition adds no guard, no gate and no\nthreshold: it only completes an assignment the framework already applied informally to some causes and left\nunstated for the rest, and it names a third category for the one cause, a structural break, that fits neither of\nthe first two.\nFigure 2 depicts the same partition graphically, together with the precedence rule that resolves a presentation\nwhose diagnostics trip causes from more than one subset at once; Table 2 below restates it as the four verdicts\nin order of precedence.\n\n11\n\n\fFigure 2: The ten guard-level abstention causes of Definition 1, grouped by the subset each belongs to (GID ,\nGCONT , GTRANS ) and resolved by the decision layer’s fixed precedence: contamination first, then transient,\nthen identification. Guard 5 is drawn with a dashed outline because it is computed and classified on every\ncycle but is not an active veto in the validated configuration (§C.2). A wait-identification verdict is the\nonly one that qualifies a presentation as an Experiment Candidate; whether it is actually assigned a designed\nexperiment in a given cycle depends further on the committee’s budget and the portfolio subset-selection\nsolution.\nTable 2: The four verdicts of the decision layer under Definition 1,\nin order of precedence. Every presentation in every cycle receives\nexactly one.\nVerdict\n\nNature of the block\n\nWhat would change it\n\nACT\n\nNone\n\nwait-contamination\n\nStructural: a validity\nassumption fails\n\nwait-transient\n\nTemporal: the regime just\nchanged\n\nIdentification and stability both hold; the\noptimizer emits P ∗\nControl of the offending channel, not more\nvariation; an experiment alone does not repair\nit\nAccumulated history under the new regime;\nthe estimate becomes usable again without\nany intervention\n\n12\n\n\fTable 2 (continued)\nVerdict\n\nNature of the block\n\nWhat would change it\n\nwait-identification\n(Experiment Candidate)\n\nObservational: the available\nvariation cannot resolve the\neffect\n\nIndependently assigned price variation. This\nis the verdict that qualifies a presentation as a\nformal candidate for portfolio experimental\ndesign\n\nTable 5 states the gate and the eight guards in compact form and Appendix B.11 develops them; several\nadapt an existing econometric tool to this decision and others have no close equivalent in that literature,\nand Section 8.1 states which is which for each of the diagnostics discussed there. Guards 1–5 address\nthe quality and the currency of the estimate: contaminated weeks, the Cinelli–Hazlett robustness value\n[Cinelli and Hazlett, 2020], the stability of the recommendation across the width of the uncertainty, the\ninstrumental contrast, and structural-break tests [Ploberger and Krämer, 1992, Chow, 1960, Andrews, 1993]\non weekly aggregates with a Bartlett long-run variance [Newey and West, 1987]. Guards 6–8, added after an\nexternal audit, address the ways an estimate can be well measured and still be measuring something else:\nover-conditioning on mediators, a failed exclusion restriction, and cross-system interference. Two mechanisms\ncomplement them: the elasticity is treated as a snapshot with an expiry date, re-estimated on expanding\nwindows with a drift alert; and every ACT-class recommendation is re-evaluated under three competitive\nscenarios drawn from the bracket, which is a reduced-form one-round device declared as such rather than\nstructural game theory.\nCross-system interference. No decision model in an enterprise operates alone, and treating adjacent\nsystems as inert is an unstated identifying assumption. The structural neighbour here is a commercial\norder-recommendation system that functions as a quota allocator: it predicts what the firm aims to place in\nthe channel, not what demand will absorb. Quota pressure is then an unobserved confounder with a temporal\nsignature, since a salesperson behind target discounts and pushes, and the incentive cycle concentrates\nthat contamination in the closing weeks where a weekly panel carries most of its price movement [Oyer,\n1998, Larkin, 2014]. Forward buying reverses after a month-end push [Hendel and Nevo, 2006], which is an\nindependent argument for taking sell-out rather than sell-in as the outcome. And over-inventoried stores\nare conjectured to discount on the shelf to rotate, which would enter the pass-through layer as consumer\nbehaviour; that third channel has no source behind it and is declared as an operational conjecture, unlike the\nfirst two. The correction is to admit the incentive cycle into W and test its influence, which is Guard 8, and\nto adopt a reporting convention under which every alternative reaching the committee declares its expected\ncost to the adjacent system. One risk no econometrics resolves: performativity [MacKenzie, 2006], the\npricing instance of the general point that a relation estimated under one policy regime need not survive a\nnew one [Lucas, 1976]. Once the engine moves prices, future variation is the child of its own estimates. The\ndefense is experimental, and it is instrumented rather than recommended (Appendix B.19).\n\n4.5\n\nArchitecture, quality gate and artifacts\n\nFive principles are enforced in code rather than recommended in documentation: a single residualization\nkernel feeding the causal estimator, the demand system and the pass-through deconvolution; a single temporal resampling policy, because one component that shuffles rows contaminates everything downstream\nwith optimism; labeled placeholders, never disguised ones; no undeclared control; and one effective\nunit of inference, meaning that the count entering any statistic whose distribution depends on a sample\nsize is the number of independent periods, never the number of panel rows.\nThe fifth is stated as a principle because its violation was found where the rest of the system had already got\nit right. The bootstrap clusters on the week and the break tests aggregate to it, but the robustness value and\nthe first-stage F statistic each consumed a raw row count, which inflated their degrees of freedom by the\nnumber of regions and rendered the weak-instrument screens effectively non-binding. That both instances\nsurvived a careful build is the concrete form the paper’s general thesis takes inside its own implementation:\n\n13\n\n\fthe failure that survived was a tacit assumption about the unit of analysis, not an error of estimation.\nSection 6.8 reports what correcting it does to the veto rate.\nA single QA Gate halts the system with an explicit exception, rather than degrading silently, if the out-of-fold\nfit of the demand model on the modelable set falls below 0.65 or mean conformal coverage falls below the\nnominal level minus 0.03. One correction to that design is proposed and stated as such: the out-of-fold fit\nof a log-volume model is dominated by seasonality and level, so it protects the band that predicts volume\nbut not the slope that requires the treatment to be identified. The quantity that protects the second is\nalready computed, the treatment partial R2 at 0.10, and the gate is therefore best presented as two thresholds\nanswering two questions. The deliverable of a cycle is not a table but a versioned repository: a ±20% lookup\ntable in 1% steps with the percentage applied once on the base price and a per-region support flag, elasticity\ncurves by tranche, the cross-elasticity matrix, and a schema-validated dashboard feed, all persisted by cycle\nand presentation with quality flags and intervals. The feed’s verdict field is closed over exactly four categorical\nstates, ACTIONABLE, WAIT_CONTAMINATION, WAIT_TRANSIENT and WAIT_IDENTIFICATION, and the schema\nrejects any other value, so a downstream consumer cannot silently receive an unclassified verdict; the binding\ncause and the operative status of each guard travel alongside it (Appendix B.14).\n\n5\n\nValidation design\n\nEvery quantity reported below is measured against a data-generating process whose answers are known\nby construction. The design premise is that a methodological claim about identification is worth what its\nrecovery experiment shows: a system that abstains correctly on a panel where the truth is unknown is\nindistinguishable from one that abstains at random.\nTwo generators. The five-regime generator simulates W = 120 weekly periods across six regions and\nsix presentations spanning three brands and two categories, with volume generated jointly with prices, costs,\npromotion, calendar effects, weather, minimum wage and an inflation index. Five identification regimes vary\nthe amount and kind of price variation available, from a clean regime with ample list movement to regimes\nbuilt deliberately to fail on weak variation, confounding with promotion, or collinearity with the competitor;\nfour further mechanisms inject pass-through, a cross-price response, forward buying and margin-dependent\ndelisting. Appendix B.1 states every parameter.\nA generator with known answers is also a generator with chosen answers, so the run uses a second. The\nextended generator draws eight presentations across a wider set of brands and varies pass-through, regime\nassignment and elasticity magnitude in three ways chosen to remove what the first generator makes artificially\nclean; Appendix B.1 states the ranges. Results are reported on both generators wherever they disagree, and\nthe disagreements are among the more informative outputs of the run: a property that holds on one and not\nthe other is a property of a configuration and not of the method. Two features both generators share bound\nwhat either can establish, and are declared: both are stationary apart from an inflationary drift, and in both\nthe confounding is injected at magnitudes the designer chose.\nFour layers plus an experimental one. Validation runs in four layers: a statistical layer requiring\nsignificance, a negative own-price sign, dominance of own over cross elasticities and the QA-Gate threshold; a\ncommercial sanity layer confronting the estimated substitution hierarchy with the consumer decision tree\nthe business knows; a back-testing layer that deliberately decouples prediction from inference, because a\nsingle predictive model conflates the two and answers both poorly; and a stress layer at the extremes. The\nsign requirement carries a selection cost that the run measures rather than assumes away (Section 7).\nNone of the four can establish that an executed price change caused what followed it. The experimental layer\ntherefore implements a holdout drawn by a deterministic function of the cycle identifier, so the assignment is\nauditable months later; a synthetic control with convex unit weights reproducing the pre-treatment trajectory\n[Abadie et al., 2010], with its pre-fit RMSE published alongside the effect and the intercept adjustment of\nDoudchenko and Imbens [2016], the time weights of Arkhangelsky et al. [2021] being declared as the natural\nupgrade rather than claimed; permutation inference over the assignment mechanism itself, since with six\nregions no asymptotic standard error would be credible; and a contamination test of the no-interference half\n14\n\n\fof SUTVA [Rubin, 1980] against the panel’s own placebo distribution plus an economic floor of 0.005 in log\nprice. Appendix B.19 states the protocol and its resolution limits.\nBookkeeping. Three conventions govern how every figure below should be read, and they are what\nseparates a validation run from a demonstration. Pre-registered verdicts: thirty decision rules were written\nbefore the run, each pairing a claim the manuscript makes with a threshold that would settle it; eleven pass,\neighteen fail, and one does not apply because the failure it was written to adjudicate did not reproduce.\nTable D.2 lists all thirty against their declared thresholds. These thirty rules are engineering verification:\na device against post-hoc selection, internal to this validation run and scoped to individual components of\nthe system. They should not be confused with the paper’s scientific contributions, the ACT/WAIT decision\nframework, the audit of tacit assumptions into diagnostics, and the conditions for legitimate aggregation,\nstated in Section 1, which the run as a whole is designed to support and which no single row of Table D.2\neither makes or breaks. Separated seeds: where a design choice was selected by measurement, the selection\nran on one set of seeds and the published figure on another, so a variant chosen on one panel does not\ninherit the selection bias of having been the best of several. No single R: the replication count is set per\nexperiment, from 300 seeds for the panel-width and randomized-shock experiments down to 5 for the re-run\nliquidity-screen and pass-through-heterogeneity scenarios, and the counts are collected in Table D.1 rather\nthan folded into one number that would overstate the evidential weight of the thinner blocks.\n\n6\n\nResults\n\n6.1\n\nRecovery of the injected elasticities\n\nTable D.3 reports, for each regime of the five-regime generator over 63 replications, the injected consumer\nelasticity and pass-through with the list-price elasticity they imply, the median estimate, bias, root mean\nsquared error, empirical coverage, and the shares of replications passing the admissibility gate and reaching\nACT.\nThe two rightmost columns are unambiguous. Clean reaches admissibility in 0.968 of replications and ACT\nin 0.944; every other regime reaches both in exactly 0.00. In clean the estimate is −0.962 against a truth\nof −1.105, a bias of 0.143 toward zero, at a coverage of 0.484 against a nominal 0.95. Where identification\nfails the estimator does not degrade gracefully: in collinear the median estimate is +0.248, the wrong sign,\nand in confounded and weak the estimates are −0.103 and −0.183 against truths of −1.190 and −0.850,\nattenuated by an order of magnitude. That is the failure mode which survives casual inspection, because a\nsmall elasticity is a plausible-looking number.\nThe round-point regime does not produce a large spurious estimate. A dedicated mechanics\nexperiment isolates the two candidate explanations for what a round shelf price could do to the recovered\nelasticity, a collapsing denominator that the partial R2 would catch against a spurious correlation at ordinary\ntreatment variance that it would not, and finds support for neither: over 19 replications the estimator recovers\nθ̂ = −0.069 against a truth of 0, at a residual treatment variance of 0.6385, comparable to the 0.5767 of the\nclean reference, with the partial R2 passing in every replication, a coefficient of variation of the shelf price\nof 0.068 confirming that the shelf does not transmit, and a residual correlation of −0.031. The variation\ndiagnostic correctly flags a pinned shelf as such, but an outsized estimate is not what this experiment measures\nthere: whatever produces one is a property of a specific configuration and not of the round-point regime in\ngeneral.\nThe extended generator. Table D.4 repeats the exercise where ρ is drawn per region, over 53 replications.\nTwo results qualify the separation above. The gate is no longer perfect: the round-point presentation is\nadmitted in 0.981 of replications under the boosted learner and the confounded presentation likewise in 0.981,\nagainst 0.00 for both on the five-regime generator. Their estimates are not damaging (0.062, and a bias of\n0.293), so the gate does not admit a disaster, but it admits them, and the near-perfect separation is therefore\na property of the configuration the thresholds were set on. Where it matters most the two agree: in the\nclean regime the original returns a bias of 0.143 and the extended a mean bias of 0.197 across its four clean\n15\n\n\fsub-presentations under the boosted learner, a difference of 0.054, so the estimation results transfer between\ngenerators even where the gating results do not.\nThe competitive bracket. Reporting the internal consistency checks requires first settling whether the\npanel can support them, and it cannot: the competitor’s real log price varies by a standard deviation of\n0.0359, which propagates to a signal of 0.0108 in log volume and, against a demand noise of 0.05, places the\ninjected +0.30 cross term below the noise floor at a signal-to-noise ratio of 0.215. The audit identity then\nholds trivially because r̂ ≈ 0 zeroes both sides. Both checks are reported instead on a variant in which the\ncompetitor makes eight discrete moves and follows the own price at r = 0.5, raising the dispersion of its real\nlog price to 0.1161. Over 31 replications the cross elasticity returns a median of 0.148 against the injected\n+0.30, the reaction is recovered at r̂ = 0.502, and the identity returns a median left-hand side of 0.153 against\na median right-hand side of 0.059 at a median absolute error of 0.101. The three are not equally successful,\nand saying so is better than presenting a near-equality the data do not deliver: the reaction function is\nrecovered almost exactly, the cross elasticity at 49% of its injected value, and the identity has a residual the\nsize of the quantity it constrains, so it detects gross violations and nothing finer.\n\n6.2\n\nWhy a resampled interval is not enough on its own\n\nThe block-bootstrap interval that the causal layer reports is a standard resampling construction [Künsch,\n1989], clustered by week to respect the panel’s serial and cross-sectional dependence. Measured against known\ntruth, it undercovers relative to its nominal level, and testing eight alternative resampling and clustering\nschemes, at two nuisance learners, does not close the gap: none reaches the coverage this paper requires\nbefore an interval is allowed to certify a decision. The shortfall turns out to be a matter of where the interval\nis centred rather than of how wide it is, which rules out simply widening it as a fix. The conformal band\nused elsewhere in the system (Section 4.2) does not share this problem; measured at rolling origins it remains\nthe one component of the uncertainty machinery whose behaviour matches its claim.\nThis finding is a statement about estimator behaviour rather than about any single pricing decision, and a\ndirect decomposition on this same panel is reported here rather than deferred in full, because it is already\ncomputed and it is what turns the diagnosis above from qualitative to quantitative. Over four independently\ndrawn designs with forty noise draws taken within each, holding the data-generating process fixed and\nredrawing which weeks carried which price movements: the dispersion of θ̂ within a realized design, relative\nto the bootstrap’s own standard error, is 0.51 under the boosted learner and 0.41 under the sieve, both below\none, so the bootstrap is, if anything, conservative conditional on the design it was computed from – empirical\ncoverage conditional on the design is 0.881 for the boosted learner and 0.475 for the sieve. The same ratio\ncomputed against total dispersion, within designs and between them together, is 0.62 for the boosted learner\nand 2.04 for the sieve: for the sieve, total dispersion is roughly double the bootstrap’s own width, consistent\nwith a between-design component the resample cannot see; for the boosted learner it is not, and the two\nlearners are read separately rather than pooled into a single verdict on this point. Table D.8 reports the full\neight-construction comparison this implies: the best of the eight, multiway clustering, reaches 0.68 under the\nboosted learner and 0.80 under the sieve, still short of the 0.95 nominal level, and the rest range down to\n0.20, so no resampling or clustering choice tested closes the gap. Section 6.3 takes up the remaining question\ndirectly: whether an interval built on the missing between-design component, rather than on resampling\nwithin one design, can. A full theoretical treatment of that construction remains companion-paper material\n(Delgado, 2026); the decomposition above is reported here, rather than left entirely to that work, because it\nis what licenses the diagnosis of this section rather than merely asserting it. What matters for the argument\nhere is the consequence: an interval computed from a single realized panel cannot always be certified as\nhonest, and a decision layer that ignored that possibility would sometimes authorize a move on evidence\nweaker than it appears.\n\n6.3\n\nWhat a single realized panel cannot tell you\n\nPart of why a resampled interval can be too narrow is conceptual rather than computational. Resampling\nweeks inside one realized panel can only characterize dispersion within that panel; it cannot characterize the\ndispersion that would arise from having observed a different set of price movements altogether, what Section 6.4\n16\n\n\ftreats as design variance. A hierarchical variance component, estimated across several presentations each\nof which is effectively a different realized design, can represent that second source in a way a within-panel\nresample structurally cannot.\nThis system folds such a component into its uncertainty accounting through the between-presentation heterogeneity τ̂ 2 it already estimates for shrinkage (Section 4.2), built on a standard between-study heterogeneity\nestimator [Paule and Mandel, 1982]. A full theoretical account of that construction’s coverage properties\nis companion-paper material; the operational point this paper needs is narrower, and a direct comparison\non this panel is reported below since it is already computed: some of the uncertainty this system faces is a\nproperty of which design was realized, not of how well it is estimated, and no amount of resampling the same\npanel removes it.\nA demonstrated comparison on this panel. What adopting the hierarchical component would change\nis not left unquantified, since it is already measured on a real replica of this panel. Table D.9 contrasts the\nproduction block-bootstrap against three constructions built on τ̂ 2 , over 70 replications on eight presentations,\nunder a scenario with heterogeneous truths and one in which every presentation shares the same truth, which\nisolates design variance by construction. The posterior-predictive construction reaches coverage of 0.914 in the\nhomogeneous scenario and 0.92 in the heterogeneous one, against the bootstrap’s 0.484 in both; the predictive\ninterval before shrinkage is close behind, at 0.902 and 0.916. Folding the between-presentation component\nback in closes most of the gap the earlier decomposition predicted it would, which is direct evidence that\nthe missing variance is design variance and not an artefact of this panel. The posterior point estimate\nalone tells the opposite story: shrinking the estimate toward the pooled mean without also propagating\nthe predictive uncertainty covers worse than the bootstrap it would replace, at 0.407 in the homogeneous\nscenario and 0.42 in the heterogeneous one, with a residual bias of 0.193 in the homogeneous scenario and\n0.179 in the heterogeneous one, because shrinkage narrows the interval without correcting the centre it is\nbuilt around. The ratio of between- to within-presentation dispersion, τ̂ /ŝ, runs from 2.51 to 2.78 across\nscenarios, confirming directly what Section 6.2 inferred indirectly: on this panel the bootstrap is answering\na question several times narrower than the one a decision needs answered. None of this is adopted in the\nproduction configuration reported throughout this paper; it is reported here because it is what the earlier\ndiagnosis would look like acted on, and a full account of the construction’s own theoretical properties remains\ncompanion-paper material (Delgado, 2026).\n\n6.4\n\nThe unit at which this channel identifies\n\nIf part of what defeats a per-presentation interval is design variance, it should average out across units whose\ndesigns are independent, at a rate the arithmetic predicts, and this is the paper’s third contribution: showing\nwhen the unit of decision should move from the presentation to the brand or the category. That prediction is\ntestable, and testing it turns a decomposition into a usable prescription for where a decision is legitimate.\nTable D.5 aggregates from the presentation to the brand and to the category over 70 replications. Under\nthe boosted learner the standard deviation of the estimate across replications falls by a factor of 2.03 at\nthe brand, where four presentations are pooled, and by 2.94 at the category, where eight are; the factors\npredicted by independent design biases averaging at the square root of the unit count are 2.00 and 2.83.\nUnder the sieve learner the measured factors are 2.02 and 3.04 against the same predictions, and the largest\nrelative deviation from the square-root law across the four contrasts is 7.4%. The mechanism is therefore\nconfirmed rather than argued.\nTwo consequences follow that a decomposition alone would not deliver. The first is a usable estimate: at\nthe category level under the sieve learner the root mean squared error is 0.159 on an elasticity of magnitude\n1.1, against 0.571 for a single presentation under the boosted learner, a factor of 3.59. Note which learner\nwins and why: the low-bias learner looked worst when a single presentation was in view because its problem\nwas variance, and aggregation is precisely what removes variance. The second is a boundary on where\nthis purchases anything. Widening the panel in regions does not average design bias, because the list\nprice is common across regions of the same presentation and the design is therefore shared; widening it in\npresentations does. This is the effective-unit-of-inference principle applied to identification rather than to\n\n17\n\n\fdegrees of freedom.\nPoint-estimate accuracy is not the whole of what a decision needs, and aggregation is not a free repair for\nthe rest of it: whether an interval at the brand or category level also reaches nominal coverage, and inside a\nusable width, is a harder and separate question that Section 6.5 takes up briefly and that companion work\ntreats in full. What this section establishes on its own is enough to act on: where item-level identification is\nunavailable, the brand or the category is where this channel’s evidence is strongest, and a committee choosing\nwhere to publish a number should choose accordingly.\n\n6.5\n\nThe limit on per-presentation precision in this panel\n\nAggregation improves the point estimate; it does not, by itself, guarantee an interval narrow enough for\na per-presentation decision. Across the combinations of aggregation level and estimator examined on this\npanel, none delivers both nominal interval coverage and a width inside the admissibility threshold defined\nin Section 3 at the presentation level, and the picture improves only partially at the brand and category\nlevels. Concretely, the narrowest interval that also covers at the nominal level, the hierarchical predictive\nconstruction of Section 6.3, is never narrower than 1.47 times the admissibility width threshold even in the\nbest combination tested, the category level under a homogeneous scenario and the boosted learner, and runs\nup to 3.38 times the threshold at the presentation level under the sieve learner in the heterogeneous scenario;\nnone of the twelve level-learner-scenario combinations tested clears the threshold (Appendix B). That is a\nproperty of how little independent price variation a hundred-and-twenty-week panel of this shape supplies,\nnot a defect specific to this implementation.\nIt is also the operational justification for treating WAIT as a first-class output rather than a residual category,\nthe paper’s first contribution: on some presentations, at some levels of aggregation, the evidence-honest\nanswer is that the data does not yet support a per-item decision at the confidence a committee should require.\nCharacterizing exactly where that limit sits, how it scales with the panel, and what would close it, is beyond\nthe scope of this paper and is developed in companion methodological work [Delgado, 2026]; Section 8.3\ndiscusses what a committee should do while the limit stands.\n\n6.6\n\nDetectable effect, and how far the gate travels\n\nThe diagnostic thresholds are cutoffs on observable variation, but the question they exist to answer is about\ndetectable effect. The generator injects a known magnitude through the same portfolio mechanism used\nin Appendix C.9, swept over six points from 0.1 to 1.0 at W = 120 and 35 replications per point, and\nFigure 3 reports, for six candidate learners, the injected magnitude at which the block-bootstrap interval\nfirst excludes zero in 80% of replications. The learners split sharply on where that point falls: under the\nproduction-adjacent boosted-tree learner it is 0.9243, close to the top of the range swept, and under the\nalternative tree implementation the curve never reaches 80% power inside the range tested at all; the lasso,\nridge and the sieve reach it at 0.5 or below, and a radial basis kernel at 0.8804.\nA companion sweep asks the second half of this section’s question directly: holding the injected magnitude fixed\nand varying only how many price movements the portfolio contains, over (2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 10, 12) movements.\nUnder both the boosted-tree and the sieve learner the admissibility gate stays below the point where half\nof draws are admitted at six movements or fewer, crosses that mark at seven, and settles at an admitted\nshare of 0.55 to 0.675 for the boosted-tree learner and 0.525 to 0.675 for the sieve by twelve movements;\nthe mean bias measured only among the admitted draws does not move in one direction as the count of\nmovements rises, which is itself informative – the gate is filtering on whether estimation is possible at all, not\non which replications happen to be least biased. Beyond how far the gate travels, the frontier itself needs\ncorrecting: comparing the honestly simulated detectable effect against the textbook formula built on each\nlearner’s own published standard error, the ratio between them is 2.335 for the boosted learner, the largest of\nsix learners compared, against 1.738 for a radial basis kernel, 1.183 for the lasso, and close to parity, at 1.057\nand 1.045, for ridge and the sieve. The published frontier is optimistic by a factor that depends strongly on\nwhich learner computed the standard error it is built on, from essentially none for ridge and the sieve to\nmore than double for the boosted tree; it is the same standard error this paper shows to be too small relative\nto the dispersion that matters. It is recorded as a quantity to be recomputed from realized dispersion, and\n18\n\n\fFigure 3: Detection rate — the share of replications whose block-bootstrap interval for θ excludes zero — as\na function of injected magnitude, for six candidate learners, at W = 120 weeks and 35 replications per grid\npoint. The dotted line marks 80% power; each learner’s honestly simulated minimum detectable effect is the\nmagnitude at which its curve first crosses that line. The text compares this simulated frontier against the\ntextbook formula built from each learner’s own standard error in the same run, which measures precision\nrather than simulated detection and is shown there to be substantially optimistic for several learners.\n\n19\n\n\fthe correction changes a published figure rather than a running threshold, since the frontier is not declared\nanywhere in the operating configuration today.\n\n6.7\n\nDoes the gate generalize beyond the configuration it was calibrated on?\n\nThe abstention gate is the system’s contribution, so whether its thresholds encode a property of the problem\nor of the generator they were set on is the most consequential question a validation can ask. Freezing the\nthresholds at the values calibrated on configuration A and moving the data-generating configuration answers\nit directly. Table D.10 reports the calibration configuration A, a low-magnitude variant B, a few-movements\nvariant C and a mid-pass-through variant D: under the boosted learner precision is 0.5 in A, undefined in B\nbecause the gate admits nothing at all, 0.0 in C and 0.75 in D; under the sieve learner it is 0.5 in A, 0.0 in B,\nwhere the gate still admits an eighth of cells but none of them is usable, 0.5 in C and 0.6 in D. The worst\nprecision reached outside the calibration configuration, across both learners and every configuration where\nthe gate admits anything at all, is exactly 0.0, against a rule of 0.80.\nThree consequences are drawn rather than deferred. The near-perfect separation on configuration A is an\nartefact of calibration and does not travel as a general claim; what remains true is that on the configuration\nthe thresholds were set for, the gate separates. The recalibration this implies is not a matter of moving a\ncutoff but of publishing the frontier: an admissibility rule stated as a single number on a single configuration\ncannot travel, and the object that travels is the surface of precision over the configuration space. And the\ndirection of the failure is not uniform: a gate that admits nothing, as under the boosted learner in B, wastes a\npanel without authorizing a bad decision, whereas a gate that admits cells at zero precision, as both learners\ndo somewhere in this sweep, authorizes moves it should not. Read against the rest of the paper this is not a\nreason to discard the gate but to stop describing it as solved and describe it as calibrated.\n\n6.8\n\nThe abstention funnel\n\nThe most informative quantity about a system whose contribution is abstention is where its portfolio is lost.\nTable D.11 reports the funnel over 50 replications and 300 presentation evaluations. Its coverage is declared\nrather than implied: guards 5 through 8 vote on the verdict but the aggregator records no per-guard exit\ncount for them, so four of the ten abstention causes of Definition 1 appear in the table as (pending) and their\ncontribution sits inside the ACT row. Those cells are left empty on purpose. The extended-taxonomy study\nof Section 6.9 does measure per-cause incidence, but on a different generator, a different estimator and a\ndisjoint draw, so transplanting its figures into this funnel would manufacture a coherence the two runs do not\nhave.\nThe funnel ends at an ACT rate of 0.307, and the most informative property of that number is what it\nis nearly equal to. Two of six presentations are drawn from the clean regime, so 0.333 of the portfolio\nis identifiable by construction; the variation diagnostic passes 0.33, the admissibility gate 0.307, and the\ninstrumented guards remove no further share at this precision. By regime, the clean presentations reach ACT\nin 0.92 of evaluations and every other regime in 0.000. The dominant recorded exit reasons are an unreliable\nθ at 0.429, the broken-weeks regime gap at 0.252, the robustness value at 0.21 and the instability of P ∗ at\n0.078; reasons are recorded per cell and a cell can carry several, so these are incidences and not a partition.\nWhat the guards cost on a panel that deserves an answer. A funnel measured on a portfolio that is\ntwo-thirds non-identifiable cannot distinguish discipline from paralysis, so the run adds the complementary\nmeasurement. On a panel identifiable by construction throughout, every veto is by construction a false\npositive. On a clean-regime panel with ρ = 0.85, ten movements of 3 to 7.5% and an exogenous competitor,\nover 19 replications, the family-wise false-veto rate of the whole harness is 0.053 (one veto out of 19). The\nadmissibility gate is held inert on this panel by construction, so the rate is produced entirely by the eight\nguards. The diagnostic classifies the triggering guard: at this sample size a single cause accounts for the\nentire rate, the regime-shift guard (Guard 1a, θ shifting when broken weeks are excluded), at 0.053; no other\nguard contributes. The median estimate on that panel is −1.024. Abstention therefore has a bounded cost:\nthe harness does not veto identifiable panels, and the 0.307 of the funnel is the portfolio and not the guards.\n\n20\n\n\fThe unit of inference in the robustness value. Consuming panel rows in place of independent weeks\ninflates the degrees of freedom by a factor of 6.0, exactly the number of regions, which confirms the inflation is\nthe mechanical one the principle predicts. Correcting it raises the mean robustness value from 0.168 to 0.317\nand lowers the veto rate from 0.575 to 0.366; adding a clustered standard error alongside the corrected degrees\nof freedom, at a clustering factor of 1.14, returns 0.402 and a veto rate of 0.21. All three are published, since\nreporting the middle variant alone would understate the statistic and overstate the veto rate, and choosing\nbetween them is a calibration decision for a real category rather than a result. The correction widens the\nseparation between regimes rather than blurring it: in clean the robustness value moves from 0.392 to 0.672.\nThe funnel is the quantity a committee should be shown first, because it converts an abstract property of the\ndesign into an operational expectation. A committee that learns from a table before the first cycle that a\nsubstantial share of a real portfolio returns WAIT reads it as discipline; one that discovers it from an empty\nprice list reads it as malfunction.\n\n6.9\n\nDoes the taxonomy close the classification gap?\n\nA taxonomy that partitions ten causes on paper is worth nothing if the population it is applied to never\nexercises the cells it adds. This subsection measures the property directly: how much of a blocked portfolio\nthe classification rule actually reaches, under the rule as it stood before Definition 1 and under the definition\nitself.\nThe measurement is deliberately separate from the funnel above. It runs 50 independent realizations of\nthe decision mechanism under the gradient-boosting nuisance learner on synthetic panels disjoint from the\nfunnel’s draws, for 300 decision instances in total. Of those, 250 return a WAIT of some kind, an 83.3% block\nrate against an ACT rate of 16.7%. Two populations, two estimators, two block rates: the numbers below are\na measurement of the classification rule and not a recomputation of Table D.11.\nApplied to those 250 blocked instances, the informal five-cause assignment the framework carried before this\npaper resolves 97.2% of them. Definition 1 resolves 100%. The difference is 7 instances, 2.8% of the blocked\nportfolio, whose only binding causes were among the five the earlier rule never named: Guard 1’s coverage\ncondition, Guard 3, Guard 4 and Guard 5. Under the earlier rule those seven presentations returned a WAIT\nwith no attached remedy, which is the operational definition of an unclassified terminal state: the committee\nis told to hold the price and told nothing about what would change that.\nAcross the ten causes on this population, admissibility binds most often at 68.7% of the 300 instances, followed\nby Guard 6 at 64.3%; Guard 1a and Guard 7 come next, at 43.7% and 43.0%, with Guard 2 close behind at\n35.7%. Among the five causes the definition newly assigns, Guard 1a regime sensitivity is the most frequent\nat 43.7%. Guard 5 never binds, at 0.0%. Contamination-type causes dominate the blocked set overall, 96.0%\nagainst 4.0% identification-type, which is a property of this population’s guard incidence rather than of the\nclassification rule; a portfolio with cleaner channels and thinner panels would invert it.\nTwo readings of that last figure deserve to be separated. The taxonomy closes the classification gap completely\non this population, without altering a single guard’s threshold or binding rate, which is what it was written\nto do. It does not follow that most blocked presentations are experimentable here: on this draw only 4.0% of\nthe blocked set is identification-type and therefore eligible for the experimental route. The classification is\nexhaustive; the eligibility it produces is an empirical quantity that varies with the portfolio, and this paper\nreports it on one population rather than claiming it as general.\n\n7\n\nRobustness\n\nThis section asks a different question than Section 6: not whether the system’s estimates and decisions\nare accurate on the calibration generators, but whether that accuracy depends on specific implementation\nchoices – which learner, which cross-fitting scheme, which point estimator, which filter – rather than on the\nidentification strategy itself. Table 3 reports eleven such contrasts, each substituting one alternative for one\nproduction choice and re-running the same generators used in Section 6. These are stress tests of design\n\n21\n\n\fFigure 4: Share of blocked presentation-instances resolved to a verdict type under the five-cause assignment\nand under Definition 1’s ten-cause partition, by verdict type, over 50 replications and 250 blocked instances.\ndecisions, not the pre-registered pass/fail rules of Section 5; a contrast can produce an informative null, a\nreversal, or a retirement without any rule failing.\nEach contrast in Table 3 removes or replaces one design decision and measures what that costs against\nthe same generators. The block is net unfavourable to the production configuration, which is the strongest\navailable evidence that it was run to measure and not to confirm: two contrasts return nulls, three return\nfindings against the production configuration, two retire a component, and one returns opposite verdicts on\nthe two generators. Appendix C reports each in full.\n\n22\n\n\fTable 3: The ablation block. Each row removes or replaces one design decision. A null is reported as the\noutcome rather than as a step toward a positive one, and the two components that fail their own test are\nwithdrawn from the published output rather than retained.\nContrast\n\nAlternative tested\n\nOutcome\n\nOutcome variable (§C.1)\n\nSell-in against sell-out under injected forward buying\n\nStructural-break\n(§C.2)\n\nAsymptotic against simulated\ncritical value, four residual paths\n\nNull. Gap −0.088, and of the opposite sign to\nthe mechanism. Sell-out keeps its argumentative support and loses its quantitative one\nGuard withdrawn. Union of both tests rejects at 0.94 under the asymptotic critical; calibration restores size to between 0 and 0.107 on\nevery path but leaves power at ≤ 0.161 at the\nlargest break, 0.286 inside the recency window\nagainst 0.214 outside it\nDisagreement across generators. Dominated on the five-regime panel; on the extended\none the best rival is not distinguishable from\nthe production learner (t = −0.30). Declared\na trade-off; the learner with the lowest error is\nthe one that breaks the break test\nNull. Difference +0.063; both recover a blend\nof two regimes, so the tolerance is not what\nbinds\nLearner-dependent, neither significant.\nPaired t = −1.43 for the boosted learner, t =\n1.99 for the production (sieve) learner; both\nshort of a conventional threshold, so the choice\nis declared a convention\nFilter replaced. It keeps 0.20 of estimates and\ndisplaces the published mean by −1.293; the\na priori rule keeps 0.96 and displaces by 0.022,\nrevealing an attenuation the filter concealed\nEstimator retired. Size 0.286 against power\n0.321 on the nominal specification, 0.321 against\n0.357 on the deflated one; a noise floor of about\n0.25 on the gap, larger than any asymmetry a\ncommittee would act on\nNull confirmed. Pass-through contributes ≤\n0.59% of the response band’s variance even with\nheterogeneity and state-dependence injected;\nthe estimator itself recovers at a bias of −0.007\nto −0.048\nScreen vindicated. The non-finite t was a\nconstant regressand, not a defect; with refusal\ninjected the screen operates, and declines correctly where there is no signal\nRestrictions rejected. Adding-up holds by\nconstruction; homogeneity and symmetry are\neach rejected at 5% in 0.625 of replications. The\nmatrix is published with the rejections attached\nCalibrated with scale. Attainable p-floor\n0.067 → 0.002; pre-fit RMSE 0.189 → 0.133;\ndonors carrying non-zero weight 3.9 → 12.1;\nfalse-positive rate 0.0 → 0.06, not monotonic in\nbetween\n\nguard\n\nNuisance learner (§C.3)\n\nBoosted trees against a regularized trend-harmonic learner, six\nlearners in total\n\nCross-fitting scheme (§C.4)\n\nContiguous blocks against forward chaining, under an injected\nbreak\nMedian across folds against\npooled DML2\n\nPoint estimator (§6.2)\n\nSign filter (§C.5)\n\nSignificant-and-negative against\nan a priori partial-R2 criterion\n\nDual Beta (§C.6)\n\nAsymmetric against symmetric\nresponse, deflated and nominal\n\nvariance\n\nRegional and state-dependent ρ\ninjected to break the null\n\nCash-pressure screen (§C.7)\n\nOrder refusal injected to give the\ndependent variable variation\n\nDemand system (§C.8)\n\nBootstrap inference on the restrictions\n\nExperimental layer (§C.9)\n\nSix, twelve, twenty and thirty regions\n\nPass-through\n(§C.7)\n\nTwo of these rows deserve to be read together, because the pair sharpens the paper’s organizing claim rather\n23\n\n\fthan merely qualifying it. The nuisance-learner contrast reverses between two generators that differ only in\nhow pass-through is drawn, and no learner wins on all three of bias, coverage and root mean squared error: a\nradial basis kernel has both the lowest bias and the best coverage, while the boosted tree has the lowest error.\nA system whose value rested on the estimator would therefore have no defensible way to choose one. What is\nstable is the discipline of measuring, and it is the measurement, not the learner, that transfers.\nThe last row is a validity check on the system’s own experimental-comparison machinery rather than on the\npricing estimator. The experimental layer (Appendix B.19) supplies an independent way to confirm the effect\nof an already-executed price change through a holdout and synthetic-control contrast, and Table D.13 shows\nthat its resolution is sensitive to how many independently assigned units back it: the same construction that\nis uninformative at six regions is usable at thirty. A committee planning a holdout of this kind should size it\nagainst that table rather than assume six regions behave like thirty; the general relationship between panel\nwidth and attainable resolution is developed in companion methodological work.\n\n8\n\nDiscussion\n\n8.1\n\nSeven tacit assumptions, seven executable tests\n\nThe strongest claim this work supports is not the integration of causal econometrics into a pricing workflow,\nwhich is by now a well-populated genre [Chernozhukov et al., 2018], but a pattern that emerged from auditing\na carefully built system and then executing it. The failure modes that survive a competent build are rarely\nerrors of estimation. They are unstated assumptions about the environment, and each can be converted into\na quantity that costs less to measure than its consequence costs to absorb. Table 4 states the seven found\nhere: four by reading the design, and three by running the validation.\n\n24\n\n\fTable 4: Seven assumptions that a careful build leaves tacit, and the executable contrast each becomes. The\nupper block was found by auditing the design and the lower by executing the validation. In every row the\ntest is cheaper than the consequence of leaving the assumption implicit, which is the property that makes the\npattern worth generalizing past pricing.\nTacit assumption\nFound by auditing the design\nThe controls are pre-treatment\n\nThe instrument acts through\none channel\n\nThe comparison group is inert\n\nNo other optimizer acts on the\nsame unit\n\nExecutable test\n\nConsequence if left implicit\n\nDeclared temporal status per column;\nestimation aborts on an undeclared\none; mediator bracket (Guard 6)\nExclusion audit: residual correlation\nof instrument and competitor price\nagainst a set carrying an explicit trend\n(Guard 7)\nContamination test on the control\ngroup’s price shift, against a permutation distribution of false cuts and an\neconomic floor\nQuota-pressure bracket with a declared source hierarchy (Guard 8)\n\nConditioning on a mediator inflates |θ|;\nthe engine under-recommends increases\nand the number stays plausible\nA common cost shock moves both estimators together, so the Hausman-type\ncontrast is small because the restriction\nfails\nA competitor reading regional prices contaminates the donor pool; the measured\neffect is attributed to the intervention\n\nFound by executing the validation\nA computed interval is an hon- Coverage against a known truth;\nest interval\nconditional-versus-unconditional dispersion across designs\n\nThe unit of decision is the unit\nof identification\n\nAggregate the estimate to brand and\ncategory and measure the dispersion\nacross replications\n\nA threshold calibrated on one\nconfiguration is a threshold\n\nFreeze the cutoffs and sweep the datagenerating configuration\n\nEnd-of-period effort lowers effective price\nand raises volume together; the engine\nlearns its own firm’s commercial policy\nas demand\nThe bootstrap targets dispersion conditional on the realized design, which is\nnot what a guard certifies; three governance switches appeared to operate and\ndid not\nDesign bias averages at the square-root\nrate, so a category estimate reaches an\nRMSE of 0.159 where a presentation estimate reaches 0.571; publishing per presentation publishes at a grain the evidence does not support\nGate precision falls from near-perfect\non the calibration configuration to 0.000\nonce evaluated outside it\n\nThe symmetry of the table is the argument. Each row names an assumption that nothing in the estimation\nstage can detect, because each is a statement about the environment rather than about the data; each is\nanswered by a contrast the system computes and publishes; and in each case the test is cheap while the\nconsequence is not. That the last three were found inside the system’s own uncertainty machinery, its choice\nof unit, and its own thresholds is what makes the pattern worth stating as a general one. The sixth row\ndeserves one further remark against this paper’s own account: that the same work argues at length for\nestimating at the week rather than the month, because the grain of a panel is an identification question, and\nthen publishes per presentation without asking the same question of the cross-section, is the clearest instance\nhere of a principle applied on one axis and not on the other. It is also the part of this work with no close\nequivalent in the enterprise deployments taken here as the comparison set [Hormby et al., 2010, Deng et al.,\n2023, Llenas et al., 2026], which report what the deployed system achieved rather than which of its identifying\nassumptions were checked; the observation is scoped to those three and is not offered as a survey finding.\nProvenance differs across the seven, and stating it plainly matters for judging what this audit adds against\nwhat it borrows. Three operationalize a concern already established in the causal-inference literature: the\nexclusion audit (Guard 7) turns the standard instrumental-variable exogeneity assumption into a residualcorrelation contrast; the contamination test adapts permutation-based inference on the no-interference half\nof SUTVA [Rubin, 1980] to a donor-pool interference question; and the threshold-generalization check (row\n25\n\n\fseven) adapts ordinary out-of-configuration validation to a decision cutoff rather than to a fitted model. The\ncoverage diagnostic of row five reuses established conformal machinery [Gibbs and Candès, 2021, Barber et al.,\n2023] for the test itself; what that test revealed about the source of the shortfall is taken up in Section 8.3 and\ndeveloped formally in companion methodological work [Delgado, 2026]. Two rows have no close equivalent in\nthe enterprise-pricing or causal-inference literatures this paper compares itself against: the quota-pressure\nbracket (Guard 8), because interference between independently operated optimizers acting on the same unit\nis not a standard identification concern, and the aggregation test of row six, which this paper develops as its\nown contribution (Section 6.4) rather than importing one. The mediator bracket (Guard 6) sits between the\ntwo groups: the underlying bad-control concern is well known, but declaring temporal status per column and\naborting estimation on an undeclared one is this audit’s own enforcement mechanism rather than a borrowed\ndiagnostic.\n\n8.2\n\nWhere the value of a system like this one lies\n\nIn the modern-trade deployments this literature documents, where promotional variation is abundant and\nshelf prices are controlled, the reported value rests in the optimization layer navigating a combinatorial space\nbeyond manual planning. In intermediated retail the binding constraint shifts: the decision space for a single\npresentation is small and one-dimensional, the input slope is the fragile link, and optimization is the simplest\ncomponent of the system.\nThe run sharpens that ordering and organizes what follows around the paper’s three contributions, each\nstated as a question in Section 1. Value does not concentrate in the estimator, since which of two nuisance\nlearners is better reverses between two generators that differ only in how pass-through is drawn. What\nsurvives is narrower and more durable: the value lies in measuring which of the system’s own claims hold,\nand in publishing the measurement. That discipline is what the three contributions below operationalize,\neach in a different part of the system.\nThe ACT/WAIT framework (RQ1, Section 4.4). Causal identification isolates price effects from\npromotion, competitor action and co-trending inflation, and the recovery experiments establish where it does\nand does not. The conformal layer is the one uncertainty component whose measured behaviour matches its\nclaim. Neither is this paper’s contribution on its own; DML, the instrumental-variable contrast and conformal\ncalibration are supporting tools. The contribution is the gate that reads them together and abstains rather\nthan publishes a number the evidence does not support, at a family-wise false-veto cost estimated at 0.053 on\nan identifiable panel (N = 19; §6.8) – the difference between publishing several different wrong answers and\npublishing none. Its specific thresholds are calibrated rather than general (Section 6.7), which is stated as a\nlimitation rather than qualified away. A WAIT verdict is not a dead end, either: which guard produced it\nindicates whether the shortfall is one of identification, calling for independently assigned variation, or an\noperational threat that any experiment supplying that variation would have to control for first (Section 8.3).\nThe admissibility audit (RQ2, Section 8.1). An engine correct in isolation yet blind to an adjacent\noptimizer acting on the same unit will model its own firm’s commercial policy and mistake it for demand;\nconditioning on a mediator inflates the recovered effect; an instrument’s exclusion restriction can fail silently.\nSection 8.1 converts seven such assumptions into diagnostics the system computes and publishes before a\nrecommendation is released, and states which formalize a concern the causal-inference literature already\nrecognizes and which do not have a close equivalent there.\nLegitimate aggregation (RQ3, Section 6.4). A component should report the unit at which its evidence\nidentifies rather than the unit at which the business happens to decide. Design bias averages at the square-root\nrate across replications, so a category estimate reaches an RMSE of 0.159 where a presentation estimate\nreaches 0.571; Section 6.4 supplies the procedure – aggregate, then measure the dispersion the aggregation\nbuys – for finding the level at which a decision is legitimate rather than assuming it is the level at which the\nbusiness happens to decide.\nThe three are complementary rather than substitutable. Causal elasticity without an admissibility gate is\ndescriptive analytics that cannot say when to trust itself. A gate without the aggregation result enforces a\nprecision standard at a unit the evidence cannot reach. Either without the tacit-assumption audit is blind\nto failure modes that are properties of the environment rather than of the estimator – and, as the previous\n26\n\n\fsection notes, some of those failure modes have no close equivalent in the enterprise deployment literature\ntaken here as the comparison set.\n\n8.3\n\nGovernance, adoption and the recalibration this run forces\n\nThe artifacts feed a human-in-the-loop process, not an autopilot: a periodic trigger, the data system, the\nrecommendation layer with its corridor, commercial validation with a documented override, a committee\nwith minutes linked to the analysis, and execution with ex-post measurement. Governance assigns explicit\nroles and typifies valid overrides (strategic, commercial, model-based, always documented) against the single\ninvalid one, that this is how it has always been done. To these is added the cross-system convention under\nwhich each alternative declares its expected cost on adjacent decision systems, so conflicts between models\nare adjudicated on the same sheet as the price.\nThree implementation challenges dominated, and the third has changed. The first was the internal coherence\nof the resampling policy, since one component that shuffles rows suffices for optimism to leak into everything\ndownstream. The second was the discipline of declaring estimands, since the difference between a competitorconditional elasticity and an equilibrium response is invisible in a report that shows only “the elasticity”. The\nthird is organizational, and the conversation a committee must be prepared for is harder than “the system\nwill sometimes decline to recommend.” It is instead “the system cannot, on the evidence this channel supplies,\npublish a per-presentation elasticity with an interval narrow enough to act on, and here is the level at which\nit can.” A system that says the first earns trust; a system that says the second earns it and changes the\noperating unit, which is a larger organizational ask and the honest one.\nOne governance risk is created by the system’s own discipline and should be anticipated rather than discovered.\nAn engine designed to halt rather than degrade returns WAIT for a substantial share of a real portfolio, which\nreads to a committee expecting a price list as a malfunction, and the pressure that follows is predictable:\nrelax a threshold for one category, waive a guard for a strategic presentation, publish the midpoint because a\nnumber is required. The countermeasure is not technical. It is to publish an explicit degraded mode (no\nrecommendation, reason, date of next review) rather than silence, and to accompany every abstention with\nthe decision arithmetic, so that holding is legible as discipline rather than as incapacity.\nThe run adds a second and more consequential governance item. Two calibrations, the admissibility width\nthreshold of 0.6 and the construction of the interval it reads, were set independently, and Section 6.5\nreports that the interval this implementation currently produces does not reliably clear that threshold at the\npresentation level. A committee cannot be asked to adjudicate that as two switches. It is one decision with\nthree defensible resolutions, each of which should be presented costed: publish at a coarser unit, where the\ninterval is narrowest; publish per presentation with an interval that does not cover, and say so on the artifact;\nor supplement the observational panel with experimental variation, a path Section 7 takes up empirically and\ncompanion methodological work [Delgado, 2026] develops further. The first is available now and the second is\nwhat the system does today without declaring it.\nA WAIT verdict is itself diagnostic, and which guard produced it indicates what kind of remedy is called for\nand not only that one is needed. Two failure modes are structurally different. When the admissibility gate\nfails for want of price variation, or Guard 2’s robustness value flags that a plausible unmeasured confounder\ncould overturn the estimate, the shortfall is one of identification: the observational record does not contain\nvariation an omitted-variable story cannot also explain, and only variation assigned independently of that\nstory resolves it. When the trigger is instead Guard 8’s quota-pressure bracket or the contamination test\nof Appendix B.19, the threat is operational rather than statistical: a sales team retaining discretion over\nincentives during a test window, or a competitor able to observe and react within the assignment’s own\ngeography, would undermine an experiment as readily as it undermines the observational estimate. A finding\nof the second kind is therefore not evidence against experimenting; it is a requirement on how the experiment\nmust be run – incentive discretion frozen or centralized for the test’s duration, and treatment and control\ndrawn at a geographic scale wide enough that a competitor’s local reaction cannot cross between them. Sizing\nthat scale, and the level of aggregation at which such a design becomes organizationally realistic, is beyond\nthis paper’s scope and is taken up in companion methodological work [Delgado, 2026].\nThe ex-post loop remains the most valuable asset. The residual uncertainty this validation isolates behaves\n27\n\n\flike dispersion across designs rather than sampling noise within one – a distinction developed formally in\ncompanion methodological work [Delgado, 2026] – and if that description is right, better estimation of the\nsame panel narrows it only partially, while generating new designs narrows it further. For the pilot that\nwould follow, success criteria are stated in advance rather than reported as results, staged across process,\ndecision, model and business; the business target of a 2–5% revenue increase is recorded as the sponsoring\norganization’s commercial ambition and not as a forecast the evidence supports, since the classic evidence\nestablishes only that price carries high leverage on profit [Marn and Rosiello, 1992] and the documented\ndeployments only that analytics-enabled pricing has delivered material gains at scale [Deng et al., 2023,\nLlenas et al., 2026]. One criterion is added by the run: rather than assume a holdout of any size is adequate,\nsize it against the experimental layer’s own measured resolution, for which Table D.13 gives the committee\na concrete range to plan against, between twelve and thirty independently assigned units in this panel.\nAppendix C.10 develops the governance design and the roadmap.\n\n8.4\n\nDeclared limitations\n\nThe system is deliberately explicit about what it does not identify. The cross elasticity inherits the endogeneity\nof the competitor’s price, for which no instrument exists with these data; the competitive scenarios are\nreduced-form, one round, with a constant reaction; and the performativity of the engine has no econometric\nfix, only an experimental one.\nTwo limitations attach to the classification of Definition 1 specifically, and both are properties of the evidence\nrather than of the rule. First, the transient category is empirically empty in everything this paper measures:\nGuard 5 is reported as not operative in the validated configuration (Section C.2) and binds in 0.0% of the\n300 instances of the extended-taxonomy study (Section 6.9). The category is defended on the argument that\na recent structural break is neither a precision shortfall nor a validity violation and that assigning it to either\nwould be a category error; that argument does not depend on the cell being populated, but the cell is not\npopulated, and a reader is entitled to treat the third branch as untested. Second, the funnel of Section 6.8\nreports per-cause incidence for six of the ten causes and marks four as pending, because this run’s aggregator\ndoes not record separate exits for Guards 5 through 8. A complete per-cause funnel on the same population\nas the main run is the obvious next measurement and is not in this paper.\nSix further limitations were produced by the validation run itself, and each qualifies a claim made elsewhere\nin this paper.\n1. The interval this implementation currently produces falls short of nominal coverage at\nthe presentation level, and the shortfall is one of centring rather than width. An alternative\nconstruction was identified that reaches a nominal-appropriate coverage rate; it is not yet adopted, and\nevery figure in this paper is produced under the construction the implementation currently runs. The\ncomparison between the two constructions is developed in companion methodological work [Delgado,\n2026].\n2. No aggregation level tried both covers and clears the admissibility gate at its current\nthreshold. Any statement here about the calibration of the three width-dependent switches is\nconditional on a threshold that has not yet been reconciled with an interval construction that covers;\nSection 6.4 takes up the reconciliation at the level of aggregation, and companion methodological work\n[Delgado, 2026] takes it up at the level of interval construction.\n3. Guard 5 is not operative. With the asymptotic critical value the break tests reject at 0.94 under\nno break; with a calibrated critical the size returns to range on every residual path, but power at the\nlargest break injected still does not exceed 0.161. Both the recency veto and the sequential reset that\ndepend on detection are withdrawn, and the currency of the elasticity rests on the drift alert and the\nrevalidation cadence alone.\n4. The gate is calibrated, not general. Precision is 0.000 once the frozen thresholds are evaluated on\na configuration outside the one they were calibrated on, against a rule of ≥ 0.80 (§6.7).\n5. The Dual Beta is withdrawn. Size 0.286 against power 0.321 on the nominal specification, 0.321\nagainst 0.357 on the deflated one; the lookup table loses its per-direction band.\n6. The conformal guarantee is calibrated within the historical regime. A band can be narrow\nand wrong if the regime shifts, and the system’s defense against that lived in the structural-break guard,\n28\n\n\fwhich this run reports as not operative. That exclusion is therefore now open rather than delegated.\nTwo further items are recorded because they run against the system rather than for it: the claim that a\nboosted nuisance learner is dominated by a regularized one does not hold on the extended generator, where no\nrival dominates the production learner at a conventional threshold; and the round-point failure, the sharpest\nillustration this literature typically reaches for, does not reproduce on the experiment built to demonstrate it.\nOne further item belongs with these because it is a property of the run rather than of the design. The\ncross-fitting partition this run uses is the unembargoed one (Section 4.1), so a single week can appear on both\nsides of a fold boundary through a different region. The embargoed variant is implemented and its coverage\nmeasured; it was not adopted here because it would change the residualizer that two of the ablations compare.\nEvery figure in this paper is therefore produced under a partition that carries a declared and unquantified\nleak.\nLimitations of scope. Three are inherited from the validation design and bound every number here. The\nsystem has not been run on a commercial panel: it is complete and a single configuration switch redirects\nit, but the switch has not been thrown, and no result reported here is an observation of a category. Both\ngenerators are stationary apart from an inflationary drift and inject their confounding at magnitudes the\ndesigner chose, so agreement between them is evidence of robustness to configuration and not of external\nvalidity. And promotional incrementality is outside the system’s scope entirely: the engine treats promotion\nas a confounder to be removed, not as a lever to be optimized. Layer-specific limitations, including the likely\nviolation of the exclusion restriction and the inter-area agreement the quota layer awaits, are recorded in\nAppendix A.17 together with every governance threshold, all of which must be recalibrated against a real\ncategory before being fixed. None of these is hidden in the artifacts: they are declared, because a decision\nsystem that feigns certainty where it has none is more dangerous than none at all.\n\n9\n\nConclusion\n\nThis paper set out to ask, of an operationally complete causal pricing system, not what the elasticity is but\nwhen there is enough evidence to act on it and when honest conduct is to wait. The system was implemented\nend to end and validated against two data-generating processes with known ground truth under thirty\npre-registered rules. Eleven pass, eighteen fail, and one does not apply. That the failures outnumber the\npasses is the result rather than a caveat on it: a validation designed to confirm returns confirmations, and\nthe value of this one lies in the places where the system’s own claims did not survive the test written for\nthem. These thirty rules are engineering verification internal to this run; the paper’s scientific claims are the\nthree contributions below, and a high fail count on the former says nothing against any of the latter.\nSection 8.2 states the three contributions in full; briefly, each answers one question posed in Section 1. The\nACT/WAIT framework (Section 4.4) answers when one can act: a gate that reads causal identification,\nconformal calibration and the tacit-assumption audit together and abstains rather than publish a number\nthe evidence does not support, at a false-veto cost estimated at 0.053 on an identifiable panel (N = 19;\n§6.8), with thresholds this paper reports as calibrated on the configurations tested rather than shown to be\ngeneral – and whose abstentions are diagnostic rather than terminal. Definition 1 makes that last property\nexact: every one of the ten abstention causes maps to exactly one of three verdict types, so a WAIT always\ncarries the reason it would stop being one. A wait-contamination calls for control over the offending\nchannel, a wait-transient for accumulated history under the new regime, and a wait-identification for\nindependently assigned price variation, which makes that third verdict a formal statement of experimental\neligibility rather than a shrug (Section 8.3). What this paper does not do is design that experiment. It\nestablishes the epistemic necessity of one, by showing that a resampled interval computed on observational\nvariation is blind to the variance a deliberate design would control, and it marks the presentations for which\nthe necessity binds; the optimization problem of acquiring that variation across a portfolio at least operational\ncost is a separate contribution and is left to companion work. The admissibility audit (Section 8.1) answers\nwhat has to hold for that gate to be trusted: seven assumptions a competent build otherwise leaves tacit,\nconverted into diagnostics the system computes and publishes before it recommends, some adapting a concern\nthe causal-inference literature already recognizes and some original to this audit. And the aggregation\n29\n\n\fresult (Section 6.4) answers at what level a decision is legitimate: a category-level estimate reaches a root\nmean squared error of 0.159 against 0.571 at the presentation level, and the paper supplies the procedure –\naggregate, then measure what the aggregation buys – for finding that level rather than assuming it is the\nlevel at which the business happens to decide.\nThe second and third contributions connect through a single observation. A resample inside one realized\npanel cannot represent dispersion that comes from the panel being the particular one it is; in this run\nthat dispersion behaves like variation across designs rather than noise within one, a distinction Section 8.3\nmotivates conceptually and companion methodological work develops formally. If that description holds, better\nestimation of the same panel would narrow it only partially, which is why aggregation is often the difference\nbetween a decision the evidence supports and one it does not, on the kind of thin, infrequently-moving panel\na intermediated retail channel typically supplies (Section 8.4).\nThis paper does not resolve that limitation, and does not claim to. What it delivers instead are the three\ncontributions above, and a system that would rather report a coarser unit, an interval that declares what it\ndoes and does not cover, and an abstention whose cost has been measured, than publish a number it cannot\nsupport. The lesson this case adds to the literature on enterprise pricing systems is that, in hard-identification\nenvironments such as the intermediated retail channel of emerging markets, the greatest returns lie not in\nmore powerful optimizers but in more honest inputs, and that honesty has a measurable price which a system\nshould be built to report rather than to obscure. A second lesson generalizes further: the failures that survive\na careful build are rarely errors of estimation, but unstated assumptions about the environment, each cheaper\nto test than to leave implicit. Ranges rather than points, abstention rather than false precision, and declared\nassumptions rather than convenient ones are what let a system of this kind be adopted, audited, and trusted\nto say when it does not yet know.\n\nDeclarations\nData availability. The results reported in this paper are produced by a synthetic data-generating process\nthat contains no commercial information; it is described in Appendix B.1 and published in full with the\nimplementation. The production panel that the same system is designed to consume derives from proprietary\ncommercial sources and is subject to confidentiality restrictions; it is not publicly available, and it was not\nused to produce any result reported here.\nCode availability. The complete system is published as an executable reference implementation, configured\nby default against the synthetic generator, so that every figure, table, and threshold in this paper can\nbe reproduced end to end without access to any commercial data. Reproducibility is a property of the\nimplementation rather than a claim about it: the global seed is fixed, the cross-fitting folds are deterministic\nfunctions of the temporal ordering, the conformal split is chronological, holdout assignment is a deterministic\nhash of the cycle identifier, and the sequential state of the pooling layer is persisted and versioned per cycle.\nRedirecting the system to a production panel is a single configuration change.\nFunding. This research received no financial support from any public, commercial, or non-profit organization.\nThe work was conducted independently, without institutional funding or the use of proprietary data.\nConflicts of interest. The author declares no competing interests. The research was conducted in the\nabsence of any commercial or financial relationships that could be construed as a potential conflict of interest.\nNo proprietary or commercial data were used in this study.\nAuthor contributions. Pedro Cadahia Delgado: Conceptualization, Methodology, Software, Validation,\nFormal analysis, Investigation, Data curation, Original draft preparation, Review and editing, Visualization.\nEthics approval. This study involves no human or animal subjects. It analyses aggregated commercial\ntime series and a synthetic generator, and no personal data are processed at any stage.\n\n30\n\n\fA\n\nMethodological Appendix\n\nA.1\n\nCausal Identification: Formulations\n\nThe main text presents the concepts in natural language; this appendix documents the mathematical structure\nfor transparency.\n\nA.2\n\nDML Partialling-Out Estimator\n\nLet Y = log(sell-out volume) and T = log(sell-in price), and let W be the confounder vector\nW = {promo_flag, promo_intensity, log P comp , weather,\nholidays, working days, woy, log(inflation)},\nextensible with numeric and weighted distribution, out-of-stock, media investment, and trade investment\nwhen the panel carries them (the optional set of Section 4.1). The partially linear model [Robinson, 1988] is\nspecified as\nY = θ T + g(W ) + ε,\n\nT = m(W ) + η,\n\nE[ε | W, η] = 0,\n\nE[η | W ] = 0.\n\nTaking conditional expectations yields the outcome nuisance function ℓ(W ) ≡ E[Y | W ] = θm(W ) + g(W )\nand the treatment nuisance m(W ) ≡ E[T | W ].\nThe cross-fitting algorithm partitions the panel into K contiguous temporal blocks {Ik } (with all observations\nof a given week assigned to the same block). For each fold k, the nuisance models ℓ̂(−k) and m̂(−k) (fit\nvia Gradient Boosting) are estimated on the remaining K − 1 blocks and evaluated on Ik , yielding the\northogonalized residuals\nỸi = Yi − ℓ̂(−k(i)) (Wi ),\n\nT̃i = Ti − m̂(−k(i)) (Wi ).\n\nThe fold-specific elasticity estimator is given by\nP\n\nθ̂k = Pi∈Ik\n\nT̃i Ỹi\n\n2\ni∈Ik T̃i\n\n.\n\nThe decision\nis the median across folds, θ̃ = mediank θ̂k , rather than the pooled aggregate\nP estimator\nP\nθ̂pooled = i T̃i Ỹi / i T̃i2 . With K = 5 temporal folds on a short panel, a single fold spanning an atypical\nregime can materially distort the pooled aggregate; the median provides a robust defense without requiring\nprior identification of the outlier fold. The pooled estimator θ̂pooled is retained solely for non-decisional\ndiagnostic layers (such as the competitive bracket and scenario elasticities). Section 6.2 contrasts the bias\nand variance of the two on the generator. The natural comparator\nfold-level aggregation is DML1 (the\nP for P\nmean across folds) rather than DML2 (the pooled aggregate\nT̃ Ỹ / T̃ 2 ). Because taking the median\nacross K = 5 folds falls outside the standard asymptotic framework of Chernozhukov et al. [2018], the\nblock-bootstrap interval for θ̃ rests on the empirical coverage validated in Section 4.2 rather than on a limit\ntheorem, and is presented as such.\nOne property of the partition adopted for the run reported here is recorded because it differs from the\nscheme the rest of this appendix describes. An embargoed variant of the contiguous-block partition, which\ninserts a gap between the training and held-out blocks, is implemented and its coverage is measured, on the\ndesign argument that the unembargoed partition can place a single week on both sides of the split through a\ndifferent region. It is not the partition this run uses. The reason it was not adopted is recorded because it is\na real constraint and not a preference: switching the partition would change the residualizer that the learner\ncontrast and the cross-fitting contrast compare, so those two ablations would stop testing what the text says\nthey test. The audit reports the change as informative rather than as passing a threshold, and the ablation\nof Appendix C.4 finds the difference between block cross-fitting and strict forward-chaining to be 0.063 under\nan injected break. Every figure in this paper is therefore produced under the unembargoed partition, whose\nleak is declared here and is not quantified.\n31\n\n\fThe interval comes from a moving-block bootstrap [Künsch, 1989] with weekly clustering. The block length\nis b = clip(round(1.5 W 1/3 ), 2, ⌊W/3⌋), giving b = 7 weeks at W = 120 (as 1.5 · 1201/3 = 7.40 rounds to 7),\nwith ⌈W/b⌉ blocks drawn from uniform starts; a resampled week carries all of its regional rows, which is\nwhat preserves the cross-sectional correlation within the week alongside the serial autocorrelation across\nweeks. The production bootstrap runs at 250 draws, uniform across the main elasticity, the Dual Beta\nand the pass-through, and the diagnostic estimators, with percentile intervals at 2.5 and 97.5. That is the\noperating value of the system, and it is not the replication count under which the results of Section 6 were\nproduced: the validation sweeps run the same estimators at counts set independently per experiment, from\nas few as 4–5 up to 300, because each cell of a sweep is itself repeated across seeds and the product would\notherwise be intractable, so some sweeps run at higher counts than production and others at far lower ones.\nThe distinction is declarative but not cosmetic, since a bootstrap at 40 draws yields a noisier interval than\none at 600, and the coverage figures of Section 6.2 are therefore a conservative reading of the interval the\nproduction system emits rather than a measurement of it. That qualification bounds the direction of the\nreported undercoverage without rescuing it: a bootstrap at 250 draws in place of 600 adds Monte Carlo noise\nto each interval’s endpoints, which widens the realized intervals on average rather than narrowing them, so it\ncannot account for undercoverage in the direction measured. Section 6.2 establishes in any case that the\nshortfall is not one of width: conditional on the design the interval is wider than the dispersion it targets,\nand what defeats it is the location of its centre. In the instrumental contrast of Section A.5 the resampling\nindices are shared between θ̂IV and θ̂DML , so that the resulting interval belongs to the contrast itself rather\nthan to the difference of two independently drawn noises. Section 6.2 verifies the interval’s coverage against\nknown truth.\nThe nuisance learners are gradient-boosted regressors with depth 3, 200 estimators, learning rate 0.05 and\nsubsample 0.8 (300 estimators for the quantile regressors and the QA Gate’s demand model). One property\nof that choice constrains the design and is declared: a boosted tree partials a monotone trend poorly, so\non a hundred and twenty periods low-frequency components can survive residualization and inflate any\nstatistic computed on the residuals, which is why the exclusion audit below adds an explicit linear time index.\nGeneralizing that fix to W as a whole, and contrasting the boosted learner against a regularized alternative\ncarrying harmonics and a trend, is the experiment of Appendix C.3.\nThe fraction of treatment variation remaining after residualization, the partial R2 , accompanies each estimate\nas a measure of effective identification, and it is the quantity the admissibility gate of Appendix B.3 thresholds\nat 0.10.\nFinally, a property of this scheme is stated rather than glossed, because it is a real tension and not a detail.\nThe scheme is standard cross-fitting: the held-out block is predicted with models trained on all other blocks,\npast and future, which is not forward-chaining. It eliminates the cross-sectional leak within the week and the\noptimism of a shuffled split, and Neyman orthogonality tolerates the remaining use of future information under\nstationarity. Stationarity is precisely what Guard 5 exists because it expects to fail, so the system delegates\ntemporal validity to the components that are strictly chronological (the conformal layer, the backtest, the\nbreak tests) rather than claiming the estimation stage provides it. Appendix C.4 measures what the tolerance\ncosts under an injected break; where the cost is material, the implied policy is to re-estimate within the\nregime following the most recent detected break.\n\nA.3\n\nDual Beta (Houck Decomposition on Residuals)\n\nFollowing the nonreversible-function decomposition of Houck [1977], on the deflated real price, per region\nseries:\nlog Pt = log P0 + Pt+ + Pt−\nP\nP\n−\nwith Pt+ =\n=\ns≤t max(∆ log Ps , 0) and Pt\ns≤t min(∆ log Ps , 0). Both cumulative treatments are\nresidualized against W with the same cross-fitting kernel and the joint regression\nỹ = ε↑ P̃ + + ε↓ P̃ − + e\nis estimated, so that all weeks participate and nothing is conditioned on the sign of the treatment. The\nweekly-block intervals of ε↑ , ε↓ travel to the lookup table as the effect band per direction. Three operating\n32\n\n\fconditions govern this estimator. The decomposition is built on the deflated price: in nominal terms P + is\nnearly collinear with the inflation index already in W , so the asymmetry would be identified from the drift\nrather than from decisions. The event counts n↑ and n↓ are reported with the estimates, and the estimator\nfalls back to the symmetric specification when either falls below the declared minimum nmin = 20 (Table D.14),\nrather than publishing an asymmetry the events cannot support; the fallback triggers on an event count and\nnot merely on non-zero variance, since a single movement produces variance without producing identification.\nAnd the variance inflation factor between P̃ + and P̃ − is published, because the two cumulative regressors\nare mechanically related and their collinearity is the binding constraint on separating the slopes. With on\nthe order of twenty events per direction, a gap |ε↑ − ε↓ | of the same order as the confidence interval is noise\nand is reported as such.\nBoth open questions about this estimator are settled below, and the resolution is to withdraw the estimator.\nOperating regime. Over 38 replications on the production configuration the estimator has 24.4 upward\nand 25.7 downward events on average, so both clear the declared minimum of nmin = 20 and the symmetric\nfallback is never triggered; the variance inflation factor between the two residualized cumulative regressors\nis 1.16, so collinearity is not the binding constraint either. The estimator is operating in the regime it was\ndesigned for. What it returns there is a gap |ε↑ − ε↓ | of 0.322 against a mean interval width of 0.583, so\nunder the rule stated above the gap is noise and no asymmetry is identified. The generator injects none, so\nthe true gap is zero and the estimator is returning a spurious asymmetry.\nSize and power, and the specification question. That an estimator returns noise where none was\ninjected establishes only half of what a decision to retain it requires; the other half is whether it would find\nan asymmetry that was there. A dedicated experiment measures both, at 28 replications per cell, by crossing\nan injected asymmetry of 0 and of 0.25 with the deflated and nominal specifications. With no asymmetry the\ndetection rate is the estimator’s size: 0.321 deflated and 0.286 nominal. With an asymmetry implying a true\ngap of 0.557 the detection rate is its power: 0.357 deflated and 0.321 nominal. Power exceeds size by 0.036\nand 0.035. The estimator declares an asymmetry in roughly a third of panels containing none and is barely\nmore likely to declare one when an asymmetry is present than when it is absent.\nThat result also closes the specification question this appendix raises elsewhere, and closes it by making it\nmoot. The deflation condition is a requirement on the production estimator, and the validation experiment\nruns both the deflated and the nominal specification rather than assuming which the paper should describe.\nThe two agree on the substantive verdict: the nominal specification has slightly better (lower) size, at 0.286\nagainst 0.321, and the deflated specification has slightly better (higher) power, at 0.357 against 0.321, and\nneither discriminates well enough for the difference to matter. Since the choice between them changes nothing\nthat matters, it does not need to be made. The event counts do differ substantially, at 99.1 upward events\nunder the nominal specification against 24.5 under the deflated one, which confirms the mechanism the\ndeflation condition was written to prevent, that nominal cumulative components absorb the inflationary drift\nand count it as decisions; it simply does not rescue the estimator to prevent it.\nWhat is withdrawn and what is retained. The Dual Beta is removed from the published output. The\nlookup table carries the symmetric response only and loses its per-direction effect band; the rescaling factor\nk = θdec /θ̂ that carried the asymmetry to the published level is removed with it, along with the cap that\nbounded its divergence. The architecture documented above is retained as a specification for a panel that can\nsupport it, together with the quantity this experiment establishes and which a future run should test against:\na noise floor of roughly 0.25 on the gap, below which an asymmetry recovered from a panel of this length\nand event count cannot be distinguished from zero, which is larger than any asymmetry a committee in this\nchannel would act on. Appendix C.6 reports that the same decomposition recovers a known asymmetry\ncleanly once shocks are assigned by design rather than merely observed.\n\nA.4\n\nCompetitive Bracket\n\nWith the reference model log Y = a log P own +b log P comp +g(W−comp )+ε: θcond = a (DML with log P comp ∈\nW ), θtotal ≈ a + b r (DML without log P comp ), θcross = b (DML with T = log P comp and the own price in W ).\n33\n\n\fPqmax\nP\nown\nrq comes from the distributed-lag regression ∆ log Ptcomp = α + q rq ∆ log Pt−q\nThe reaction r̂ = q=0\n+ ut\nin within-region log differences. Internal audit: θtotal − θcond ≈ θcross · r̂. The scenario elasticity is θsc (r) =\nθcond + θcross · r, evaluated at r ∈ {0, r̂, 1}.\n\nA.5\n\nPLIV Contrast\n\nWith instrument Z = log(variable cost) (shocks to the principal ingredient, the packaging substrate, the\nexchange rate, and category excise taxes; disaggregated by P\ninput soP\nthat format families receive distinct\nshocks): Y , T , and Z are residualized against W and θ̂IV = Z̃i Ỹi / Z̃i T̃i is estimated, with instrument\nstrength measured by the F statistic of the residualized first stage. Two exclusion caveats are declared\nrather than assumed away: cost shocks common with the competitor are only partially controlled through\nlog P comp ∈ W , and a cost shock can move the shopkeeper’s margin [sticky shelf, delisting (Section A.10)],\nopening a cost→volume channel that bypasses the list price; for this reason Guard 4 never decides alone.\nThe contrast θ̂IV − θ̂DML is bootstrapped with the same weekly moving blocks and shared resampling indices\nbetween both estimators, so the interval belongs to the contrast itself rather than to the difference of two\nindependent noises; it feeds Guard 4.\nExclusion Audit (Guard 7). The first caveat above is tested rather than declared. Let Z̃, T̃ and log^\nP comp\nbe the residuals of the instrument, the treatment and the competitor’s price against W−comp ∪ {t}, where t is\nan explicit linear time index: a boosted tree partials a monotone inflationary trend poorly, and without t the\ncommon drift survives residualization and inflates the statistic below. First-stage strength is measured on\nthe effective unit of inference (Tw independent weekly periods) rather than on raw panel rows to prevent\ndegree-of-freedom inflation, using the first-stage F statistic with cutoff fmin [Staiger and Stock, 1997]:\nπ̂ 2\n\nF = (Tw − 1) PTw\n\nPTw\n\n2\nt=1 Z̃t\n\n2\nt=1 (T̃t − π̂ Z̃t )\n\nPTw\n\n,\n\nπ̂ = Pt=1\nTw\n\nZ̃t T̃t\n\n2\nt=1 Z̃t\n\n.\n\nThe exclusion diagnostic is the residual correlation ϱ = corr(Z̃, log^\nP comp ). The verdict is exclusion defensible\nwhen F ≥ fmin and |ϱ| < ϱmax ; common shock: exclusion in doubt when F ≥ fmin and |ϱ| ≥ ϱmax , which\nvetoes; and weak instrument: not conclusive when F < fmin , which records without vetoing, mirroring the\nasymmetry of Guard 4. Note the diagnostic direction: a common cost shock displaces θ̂IV and θ̂DML together,\nso the Hausman-type contrast is small under the violation. The two guards are therefore complementary and\nnot redundant: Guard 4 detects residual endogeneity, Guard 7 detects that the instrument is not entitled to\nadjudicate it.\n\nA.6\n\nPartial Pooling with a Decision-Grade Elasticity\n\nThe whole layer is one conjugate normal–normal update. Given a prior N (m0 , v0 ) and the cycle’s raw estimate\nθ̂ with variance s2 (from the block-bootstrap interval),\nθpost =\n\nv0−1 m0 + s−2 θ̂\n,\nv0−1 + s−2\n\nvpost = v0−1 + s−2\n\n\u0001−1\n\n,\n\nwdata =\n\nv0\n,\nv0 + s 2\n\nwith three origins of the prior:\n1. Hierarchical branch → θpool : At each level, the heterogeneity τ̂ 2 is estimated by Paule–Mandel\n[Paule and Mandel, 1982], solving Q(τ 2 ) = k − 1 with weights 1/(s2i + τ 2 ); using the sample variance\nof the θ̂i instead inflates τ 2 by the estimation noise (Var(θ̂) = τ 2 + s2 ) and systematically weakens\n2\n2\nthe shrinkage. P\nUnder random effects,\nP the2 branch mean uses weights 1/(si + τ̂ ) and the precision\n2\n2\nof a branch is i 1/(si + τ̂ ), not i 1/si . The prior mean is the leave-one-out brand mean (no\npresentation shrinks toward itself), the brand itself shrunk toward its category with the same update,\nand v0 = τ̂w2 + Vµ carries both the within-branch heterogeneity and the uncertainty of the branch mean,\nwith fallback brand → category → global when a branch has no siblings with data.\n\n34\n\n\f2. Previous cycle → θdec : A local-level DLM with discount factor δ [West and Harrison, 1997]: the\nprior of cycle t is the posterior of cycle t − 1 with variance inflated to vt−1 /δ, and the data point is the\nraw θ̂ of Section A.2. The state (m, v, ncycles ) is versioned per cycle.\n3. Cold start / reset: A presentation without an own estimate takes the branch prior with predictive\nvariance as posterior, with the origin declared (prior_marca / prior_categoria / prior_global); a\nstructural break (Guard 5) marks reset in the state, and the next cycle restarts from the branch; this\npath is inert while Guard 5 is reported as not operative (§C.2).\nThe gate of Section A.12 votes with the raw θ̂; θdec drives the level of the emitted P ∗ and the published\ncurves, scaling the Dual Beta as ε↑,↓ · k with k = θdec /θ̂ (the ε↓ /ε↑ ratio is preserved; the guard k > 0 prevents\nsign inversion, and k is capped at kmax because the ratio diverges as θ̂ → 0).\nTwo declared approximations. The update above treats τ̂ 2 as known when forming v0 = τ̂w2 + Vµ .\nIgnoring the sampling variation in τ̂ 2 leads to overly tight posterior intervals. This downward bias in variance\nestimation is most acute when the count of branch siblings is small. The Paule–Mandel equation is solved via\n2\nbisection subject to a floor τmin\n. On failure, the algorithm falls back to the sample variance of θ̂i . Since this\nfallback suffers from the signal-to-noise conflation highlighted earlier, it is explicitly flagged in the artifact\nwhen triggered. The available corrections are the Hartung–Knapp–Sidik–Jonkman adjustment [Hartung\nand Knapp, 2001, Sidik and Jonkman, 2002] or a Hardy–Thompson propagation [Hardy and Thompson,\n1996], and adopting one is a one-line change with a direct effect on the coverage of the posterior that sets the\npublished price level.\nThe second approximation concerns the data variance. The system takes s2 = ((cihi − cilo )/3.92)2 , reconstructing the variance from a percentile bootstrap interval under an assumption of normality and symmetry that a\nblock bootstrap on a short panel does not guarantee. Taking s2 directly as the variance of the bootstrap\ndistribution removes the assumption at no cost, and is the form this layer should adopt.\n\nA.7\n\nTemporal Status of the Controls and the Mediator Bracket\n\nEvery column w ∈ W carries a declared status time(w) ∈ {pre, post} relative to the pricing decision, together\nwith a family label\nfam(w) ∈ {commercial, execution, competition, exogenous};\nestimation aborts on an undeclared column.\nDefine\n\nW − = W \\ {w : time(w) = post ∧ fam(w) ∈ {commercial, execution}},\n\nwhich prunes the post-treatment commercial and execution controls while deliberately retaining log P comp ,\nwhose ambiguity is resolved by the competitive bracket of Section A.4. With θ̂(W ) the estimator of Section A.2\nevaluated on a control set, the mediator diagnostic is\n∆med = θ̂(W − ) − θ̂(W ) ,\nand Guard 6 vetoes when ∆med > δmed . The quantity is a diagnostic, not a correction: neither estimate is\npromoted automatically, because θ̂(W ) and θ̂(W − ) are the direct and the total effect and the choice between\nthem is a modeling decision, not a data one [Rosenbaum, 1984, VanderWeele, 2015]. Under the sign structure\nassumed for this channel (trade activity increasing when list price rises), conditioning on the mediator inflates\n|θ̂|, and since the optimizer’s interior optimum carries a markup θ/(1 + θ) that falls toward one as |θ| grows,\nthe failure mode is systematically conservative recommendations rather than aggressive ones, which is why it\nsurvives casual inspection. That sign is an assumption and not a measurement: it is plausible where trade\nsupport accompanies an increase, but it is not established here, and if it reverses the same guard is protecting\nagainst an aggressive failure rather than a conservative one. The guard’s threshold does not depend on the\nsign; only the characterization of what an unguarded system would do does, and it is stated as conditional on\nthe assumption for that reason.\n35\n\n\fA.8\n\nConformal Layer and QA Gate\n\nCQR Recipe [Romano et al., 2019]: Mondrian-stratified [Vovk et al., 2003] by region, with an out-oftime split (the oldest 50% of weeks for training, the next 30% for calibration, the most recent 20% for testing,\ncuts at week boundaries):\n1. Fit two quantile regressors q̂α/2 and q̂1−α/2 on the training set.\n2. On calibration, compute the conformity score Ei = max{q̂α/2 (xi ) − yi , yi − q̂1−α/2 (xi )}.\n3. Take the order-⌈(1 − α)(m + 1)⌉ quantile of E within each stratum.\n4. Build the interval [q̂α/2 (x) − Q, q̂1−α/2 (x) + Q].\nTwo conditions bound this construction and both are enforced rather than assumed. First, the clamp\ne(min(k,m)) becomes active whenever m < ⌈1/α⌉ − 1, which at α = 0.10 is nine calibration points: below that\nsize the returned quantile is the largest observed score and empirical coverage falls strictly below 1 − α. A\nminimum stratum size mmin = ⌈1/α⌉ − 1 is therefore enforced before Mondrian stratification is attempted,\nand a stratum below it falls back to the global calibration score. This is a distinct failure mode from the\ndegradation of the guarantee from group-conditional to marginal described above, and it is logged separately,\nsince one is a loss of conditionality and the other a loss of coverage. Second, the finite-sample guarantee\nrequires exchangeability, which the chronological partition of Section 4.2 deliberately breaks; the construction\nis therefore justified by the adaptive-conformal argument for non-stationary environments Gibbs and Candès\n[2021], Barber et al. [2023] and validated by the out-of-time coverage reported therein, not by the finite-sample\nresult.\nCoverage is verified with rolling origins, moving the panel endpoint back in four-week steps for up to ten\norigins and requiring at least 70 weeks of history at each, and must remain around 1 − α without drift; that\nhistory requirement is what makes the verification feasible at this grain and is reported here because it binds.\nThe QA Gate halts the system if the out-of-fold R2 of the demand model on the modelable set falls below\n0.65, computed on temporal folds, or if mean Mondrian coverage falls below 1 − α minus a tolerance of 0.03,\ni.e. below 0.87; the same control protects, as a decorator, the artifact stage. As argued in Appendix B.15,\nthe R2 threshold is a test of predictive health that protects the band, and the partial-R2 threshold of the\nadmissibility gate is the corresponding test of identification that protects the slope.\n\nA.9\n\nAIDS Demand System Identified via FWL\n\nLA-AIDS form [Deaton and Muellbauer, 1980] by region. For each presentation i, with ˜· the residual against\nW obtained with the single cross-fitting kernel:\nw̃i =\n\nX\n\n^ ∗ ) + ẽi ,\n^\nγij log\npj + βi log(X/P\n\nj\n\nwhere wi is the revenue share, X the category expenditure, and P ∗ the Stone price index [Stone, 1954]. Each\nequation is estimated by least squares on residuals, without a constant (the residuals are centered); by FWL,\nthe γij coincide with those of the full system including W . The Marshallian\ncross-elasticity is recovered as\nP\nεij = −δij + γij /w\n−\nβ\n(w\n/w\n).\nThe\nrestrictions\nof\nhomogeneity\n(\nγ\n=\n0),\nSlutsky symmetry (γij = γji ),\ni\ni\nj\ni\nij\nj\nP\nand adding-up ( i βi = 0) are tested explicitly, and the matrix is published only if the violations are of the\norder of the standard deviation of the γ.\nDeclared simplifications and inference. Three properties of this implementation\nP are stated rather\nthan absorbed. First, the Stone index is constructed with observed shares, log P ∗ = i wi log pi , which is\nthe standard linear approximation and introduces the simultaneity between the deflator and the dependent\nvariable that it is known to introduce; the units-invariant correction of Moschini [1995] is not applied, and\nshould be either adopted or explicitly justified. Second, the confounders are collapsed to the date level by\naveraging across presentations before residualization, which is not equivalent to residualizing each share\nequation against its own W ; the simplification is admissible because W is dominated by date-level variables\n(seasonality, holidays, inflation, weather by region), but it is an approximation and not an identity. Third, the\n36\n\n\fcoefficients are obtained by least squares, and the restriction tests are run against a bootstrapped sampling\ndistribution rather than merely against the dispersion of the γ: the weekly moving-block bootstrap of\nSection A.2 is applied jointly to the residualization and the demand stage, which supplies standard errors for\nevery γij and a Wald statistic for each of the three restrictions against its own null.\nWhat the inference returns. Appendix C.8 reports the run and Table D.12 the restriction tests. Two\npoints belong here because they concern the estimator rather than the result. The adding-up restriction\nis satisfied almost exactly, with a mean violation of 0.0000 and a Wald statistic of 0.160 on one degree of\nfreedom, which is the expected behaviour of a restriction the estimation imposes by construction and is\ntherefore a check on the bootstrap rather than on the demand system: an inference procedure that rejected a\nrestriction the estimator enforces would be measuring its own noise. Homogeneity and Slutsky symmetry are\nrejected at the 5% level in 0.625 of replications each. The publication rule stated above, that the matrix is\nreleased only if the violations are of the order of the dispersion of the γ, is therefore testable directly; it does\nnot clear its own test. The rule is accordingly replaced rather than waived: the matrix is published together\nwith its bootstrap standard errors, the three Wald statistics and their rejection rates, and the statement that\ntwo of the three restrictions do not hold on this panel. A committee that reads a cross-elasticity matrix is\nentitled to know that the theory the matrix is named after is rejected by the data it was estimated on, and\nwithholding the matrix would communicate less than publishing it with that attached.\nOne reading rule accompanies the published matrix. The Marshallian elasticity εij divides by the revenue\nshare wi , so for small-share presentations the noise in γij is amplified by a large factor and the matrix\ncan appear visibly non-zero where the underlying coefficients are statistically indistinguishable from zero.\nCross-elasticities of small presentations are therefore to be read as bands, never as points.\n\nA.10\n\nIntermediated Retail: Shopkeeper Pass-Through and the (S, s) Architecture\n\nDeconvolution. θ = ε · ρ, with ε the consumer elasticity (volume against P so ) and ρ = ∂ log P so /∂ log P si\nthe shopkeeper’s pass-through, estimated from the residualized regression of log P so on log P si with the single\nkernel. The distribution of ρ is obtained by weekly moving-block bootstrap, and the composite response band\nis\n∆ log Q = ε · ρ · ∆ log P si + demand noise,\nwhose variance is decomposed analytically into a demand term and a pass-through term, Var(∆ log Q) ≈\n2\n(ρ ∆ log P si )2 Var(ε) + (ε ∆ log P si )2 Var(ρ) + σdemand\n, and contrasted against the end-to-end conformal band:\nthe latter provides the guarantee; the former, the attribution of how much of the width is demand and how\nmuch is pass-through. The composition is a closed-form attribution and not a Monte Carlo draw; a Monte\nCarlo composition would relax the local linearization implicit in the expression above and is recorded as an\navailable refinement rather than described as running.\nWhat the attribution returns on the generator. The layer was built on the expectation that the\npass-through term would come to govern the width of the response band at large enough moves, and it does\nnot. Evaluated at the dispersion of ρ̂ measured over 19 replications, against a demand noise of σdemand = 0.05,\nthe pass-through term contributes 0.0% of the variance at a 1% move, 0.1% at 5%, 0.2% at 10%, and 0.7%\nat 20%: it does not reach parity anywhere inside the operating range, and the crossing point the design\nanticipated does not exist on this panel. Across the whole ±20% corridor the width of the response band\nis governed by demand noise alone. Two readings follow and both are worth stating. Operationally the\nattribution is uninformative here and should be reported as a constant rather than as a decomposition, since\na committee told that 99.3% of the uncertainty is demand at every move size learns nothing from being\ntold it five times. Methodologically the null is a property of this generator, whose ρ̂ is tightly estimated\nbecause the pass-through is injected as a constant with no state dependence (Appendix B.1 declares the\nmargin as nearly a deterministic function of list price and promotion); a real shopkeeper population with\ndispersed and state-dependent transmission would inflate Var(ρ̂) and could move the crossing inside the\nrange. The attribution is therefore retained as architecture whose informativeness awaits a panel with genuine\npass-through heterogeneity, which is the same condition the Markov margin layer awaits.\n37\n\n\fMargin Dynamics. A Markov chain over the shopkeeper’s margin bands µ = (P so − P si )/P so , with\ntransition matrices conditioned on the sign of the list shock, the stationary distribution, and first-passage\ntimes.\nThree-Layer (S, s) Architecture (for Store-Level Panels).\n• Layer 1: Gold/Silver/Bronze classification by continuity of P so capture, with repricing detection\nconfirmed by persistence (a jump counts only if it persists, so as not to confuse capture noise with real\nchanges).\n• Layer 2: For Gold data, the mean cumulative |gap| at the jump captures the (S, s) band plus the\nmean overshoot, serving as an upper bound on the structural trigger rather than a direct point estimate.\nThis upper bound remains substantially more stable under small-sample event counts than the 0.5 logit\nthreshold.\n• Layer 3: Bayesian transfer with the Gold prior and a shrinkage weight anchored to measurement\nreliability (effective information ∝ reliability2 ; Fuller, 1987), with a learned hypervariance (partial\npooling, not strict pooling), the prior conditioned on store covariates against between-tier selection\nbias, and jump imputation in gaps by forward recursion / particle filtering with a structural hazard\nanchored at the Gold band. The validity of the intervals is provided by Mondrian conformal per tier on\nthe residuals of the (S, s) model.\nP si\nQijt be the store’s observed outlay with\nBudget and Liquidity of the Shopkeeper. Let Bit = j Pjt\nthe manufacturer. B is a function of both the treatment and the outcome and is therefore excluded from W\nby construction: conditioning on it identifies within-basket substitution at constant expenditure, not the\ndemand response. Three admissible objects replace it:\n(i) A cash-pressure index cpt = z(ct ) − z(st ) built only from sources exogenous to firm sales [on the\nexpenditure imprint of paycheck cycles see Stephens Jr, 2003], with ct = min(|dt − 15|, |dt − 30|) the\ndistance to the semimonthly payroll dates (dt the day of month), st the real minimum wage deflated by\nthe price index, and z(·) standardization; it is declared pre in Section A.7 and enters W . The two terms\nare not equally informative and the index is published with its variance decomposition rather than as\na single number: ct carries intra-month variation and is what identifies, while st is, absent a regional\nprice index, a national series close to a deterministic trend and nearly collinear with log(inflation) ∈ W .\nThe construct is admitted to W only after the screening test below clears it, and regional sources (a\nregional price index, a harvest calendar, a measure of informality) replace st automatically where the\npanel carries them.\n(ii) Total expenditure X enters the demand system of Section A.9 as the allocation variable, instrumented\n(lagged expenditure or cp), because the equation errors enter the total [Blundell and Robin, 1999]; it\ndelivers the expenditure elasticity ηi = 1 + εi /wi .\n(iii) The binding state enters as an effect modifier on θ and on ρ, not as a control.\nExtensive Margin. With Ait ∈ {0, 1} the acceptance of the order and Qit the volume conditional on\nacceptance, the two-part specification [Cragg, 1971] is Pr(Ait = 1) = Λ(γ ′ xit ) and E[log Qit | Ait = 1] =\nθ log Pitsi + g(Wit ). If the liquidity constraint drives A = 0, the observed volume sample is selected on the\nconstraint state [Heckman, 1979] and the single-equation estimator of Section A.2 is inconsistent\nfor the\nP\nunconditional response. The observable signature at the region level is portfolio breadth Nit = j ⊮{Qijt > 0}\nfalling while conditional depth holds. The screening test regresses N on cp controlling for own log price and\nrequires |t| ≥ 2 before the full construct is built; the store-level grain remains a declared prerequisite, since\naggregation smooths a lumpy discrete process into a mild and false elasticity [Caballero and Engel, 2007].\n\n38\n\n\fA.11\n\nOptimization, Corridor, and Decision Score\n\nLayer 1 (Objective Function and Constraints): The optimization problem solved by the decision\nengine is formulated as:\nX\nmax\n(Pi − Ci )Q̂i (P )\nP\n\ni\n\ns.t.\n\nmargini (Pi ) ≥ marginmin ,\n\n∀i,\n\nvolume and market share bounds,\nprice ladder consistency constraints,\nPi ∈ [Pi,min , Pi,max ],\nwhere Q̂i (P ) = Q0i\n\n\u0010\n\nPi\nPi0\n\n\u0011ε\n\n∀i,\n\n, with ε = ε↓ if Pi < Pi0 and ε = ε↑ otherwise (Dual Beta formulation). The slopes\n\nare scaled by k = θdec\naccording to Section A.6. The competitive corridor [P comp (1 − κ), P comp (1 + κ)] with\nθ̂\nκ = 0.10 is the sole hard constraint on the price derived from the competitive environment; the margin,\nvolume, share, ladder, and [Pmin , Pmax ] conditions listed above bind alongside it, as does the operating limit\nof ±20% on the base list price. The feasible set is the intersection of the three:\n[P 0 (1 − 0.20), P 0 (1 + 0.20)] ∩ [P comp (1 − κ), P comp (1 + κ)] ∩ [Pmin , Pmax ].\nWhat is defined rather than left implicit is the reporting rule: the artifact records which of the three bound\nfirst, since a corridor reported as the reason for a truncated recommendation is misleading when the operating\nrange bound instead.The corridor is not intersected with the conformal interval, which is a predictive band\non the volume response and travels with the recommendation rather than bounding the decision variable\n(Section 4.2); nor with tranche kink points, which are not implemented, since the demand model carries no\nthreshold terms. Both are recorded as future work: a kink specification would require threshold dummies\nin the demand model, and their interaction with the corridor would need to be defined before either could\nbound a price.\nBase price, cost, and competitor references reflect the current state of the market rather than panel-wide\naverages, operationalized as the median over the most recent 8 weeks. The window is a design decision with\na direct effect on P ∗ and is reported as such: under persistent inflation a panel-wide average centers the\ncorridor on a competitor that no longer exists, while too short a window makes the corridor track transient\ncompetitor noise.\nFor a single presentation, the optimum is obtained by exact enumeration (arg max) over the grid of integer\nprices in the corridor. The enumeration is exact rather than approximate because the grid is the feasible set:\nprices are set in the smallest circulating denomination, so the space is one-dimensional, bounded and discrete,\nand a metaheuristic would add stochastic dependence without enlarging the search. For the portfolio vector\nwith cross-cannibalization the landscape is genuinely multimodal, and global differential evolution [Storn and\nPrice, 1997] with L-BFGS-B polishing [Byrd et al., 1995] is specified and reserved for that scale-up rather\nthan implemented here.\nLayer 2 (Committee Metric):\nScore(P ) = w1 · MarginNorm(P ) + w2 · VolumeNorm(P ),\noutside the corridor ⇒ −1\ncomputed on a fixed, documented normalization window; it ranks and communicates scenarios, it never\ndefines P ∗ .\nDecision-Language Translation. For a base price P 0 , unit cost C, and recommended P ∗ , let ∆p =\n(P ∗ − P 0 )/P 0 and m = (P 0 − C)/P 0 . Three quantities accotolerable volumempany every recommendation.\nThe tolerable volume loss is the napkin arithmetic the committee can verify mentally,\nL=\n\n∆p\n,\nm + ∆p\n39\n\n\fand the critical elasticity θcrit identifies the indifference point between moving and holding under the symmetric\nlog-log benchmark:\n\u0002\n\u0003\nlog (P 0 − C)/(P ∗ − C)\ncrit\nθ\n=\n.\nlog(1 + ∆p)\nUnder Dual Beta, this threshold is evaluated using the direction-relevant slope (ε↑ or ε↓ , scaled by k). The\ndecision is declared stable across the range if the directional interval falls entirely on one side of θcrit (θlo > θcrit\nor θhi < θcrit ), rendering further decimal precision in θ̂ irrelevant to the recommendation.\nThe status of the two quantities should be stated correctly, since it is a strength of the construction rather\nthan a caveat. L is algebraically exact and free of any functional-form assumption: it follows from the\ndefinition of contribution alone, which is precisely why a committee can verify it mentally and why it is\npublished. θcrit is exact conditional on the log-log specification the optimizer itself uses, and inherits that\nassumption and no other. Neither is a linear approximation. The three together support the sentence in\nwhich a recommendation should be read aloud to a committee: this move can absorb a volume loss of up to\nL; the decision reverses only if the elasticity is worse than θcrit ; and the estimated range lies entirely on one\nside of that threshold, so the second decimal changes nothing and should not be debated.\nLookup Table:\n\nFor pct ∈ {−20%, . . . , +20%} in 1% steps,\nuplift_vs_base = (1 + pct)θ − 1,\n\napplied once on the base price, with a support flag by regional intersection and a directional band only where\nan asymmetric estimator contributes intervals, which is not the case in the configuration validated here\n(Appendix C.6).\n\nA.12\n\nThe Decision Layer\n\nA.13\n\nThe Admissibility Gate and the Eight Guards: Ten Abstention Causes\nTable 5: The admissibility gate and the eight guards of the decision\nlayer, which together define ten abstention causes: Guard 1 is one\nguard evaluating two conditions, listed here as 1a and 1b. The gate\nis evaluated first and routes a failing presentation to WAIT without\nany guard being reached. Guards 1–5 address the quality and\nthe currency of the estimate; guards 6–8, added after an external\nmethodological audit, address the ways an estimate can be well\nmeasured and still be measuring something other than what it\nclaims. All of them can move a presentation from ACT to WAIT\nand never the reverse. The final column gives the verdict type\neach cause maps to under Definition 1. Operating values for every\nthreshold are in Table D.14.\n\nCause\n\nQuestion it answers\n\nMechanism\n\nVetoes\n\nType\n\n0·\nAdmissibility\n\nIs there a number fit\nto be voted on at\nall?\nDoes θ move when\nweeks without a\nclean demand signal\nare removed?\n\nAt least half the regions\nmodelable (≥ 1/2); partial\nR2 > 0.10; CI width < 0.6\nFour observable proxies\n(stockout below q05 ,\nprice-matching on competitor\njumps ≥ 2.5%, promo > q75 ,\nclearance ≤ −4% with volume\n≥ 1.5× median); re-estimation\nwithout them; ∆-regime\nvs. threshold\n\nYes,\npre-emptively\n\nid\n\nYes\n\nid\n\n1a · Regime\nsensitivity\n\n40\n\n\fTable 5 (continued)\nCause\n\nQuestion it answers\n\nMechanism\n\nVetoes\n\nType\n\n1b ·\nBroken-week\ncoverage\n\nIs too much of the\npanel made of such\nweeks for any\nre-estimation to be\ninformative?\nHow strong must an\nunmeasured\nconfounder be to\nnullify θ?\n\nShare of weeks flagged by the\nsame four proxies, against the\ncoverage threshold\n\nYes\n\nid\n\nCinelli–Hazlett RV [Cinelli and\nHazlett, 2020] on the effective\nn (independent periods),\nbenchmarked against\npromotional intensity\nOptimization at the raw CI\nendpoints and over the\np\nposterior θdec ± 1.96 s2dec ;\nswing vs. half the corridor\nwidth\nθIV vs. θDML with the cost\ninstrument; F ≥ fmin\n\nYes\n\nid\n\nYes\n\nid\n\nOnly if the\ninstrument is\nstrong\nAs specified,\nyes if the\nbreak is\nrecent, also\nresetting the\nsequential\nprior (§ A.6);\nreported as\nnot operative\nin §C.2 and\ntherefore not\nvetoing in the\nvalidated\nconfiguration\nYes\n\ncont\n\n2 · Robustness\nvalue\n\n3 · P* stability\n\nDoes P* swing\nbeyond tolerance\nacross the\nuncertainty?\n\n4 · PLIV\ncontrast\n\nIs there residual\nendogeneity not\ncaptured by W ?\nIs θ still valid?\n\n5 · Structural\nbreak\n\n6 · Mediators\n\n7 · Instrument\nexclusion\n8 · Quota\npressure\n\nIs θ\nover-conditioned on\ncontrols that\nrespond to price?\nIs the cost\ninstrument entitled\nto adjudicate Guard\n4?\nIs part of θ\nsales-force effort\nrather than price\nsensitivity?\n\nOLS-CUSUM [Ploberger and\nKrämer, 1992] with Bartlett\nlong-run variance [Newey and\nWest, 1987] +\nChow–Bonferroni [Chow, 1960]\nat a priori candidate dates;\nrequires recency and 0.01 after\nBonferroni\n\n∆ between θ(W ) and θ(W − )\nwith the post-treatment\ncommercial/execution controls\npruned (§ A.7)\nResidual correlation between\ninstrument and competitor\nprice, with an explicit time\nindex (§ A.5)\n∆ between θ with and without\ncommercial-cycle controls,\nunder a declared source\nhierarchy: true quota fields,\nelse calendar proxy, else\nnon-operative (§ A.15)\n\ntrans\n\ncont\n\nOnly if the\ninstrument is\nstrong\n\ncont\n\nYes, where\noperative\n\ncont\n\nThe admissibility gate and the eight guards are collected in Table 5, which lists them as the ten abstention\ncauses the decision layer actually classifies: the gate, the two conditions of Guard 1, and Guards 2 through 8.\nThe count of guards is unchanged at eight; what Definition 1 partitions is causes, not guards, and the two\nenumerations should not be conflated. Guards 6–8 were added after an external methodological audit and\n41\n\n\ffollow the same asymmetry as the original five: they can move a presentation from ACT to WAIT and never\nthe reverse. The thresholds are the committee’s governance switches: they are modified by configuration,\nwithout touching the model, and every one of them is published with its operating value and its sensitivity in\nTable D.14. The ACT decision requires passing all operative guards simultaneously, plus the reliability flags\nof the diagnostic and the estimation; any failure holds the price and records a readable reason. A guard that\ncannot be evaluated on the panel at hand (Guard 8 without incentive fields or sub-monthly grain) is recorded\nas non-operative and never as passed, and the operative status of all eight travels with the verdict, together\nwith the verdict type Definition 1 assigns to whichever cause bound.\nThe decision-grade θdec and its declared origin travel alongside each verdict: the gate votes with the raw\nestimate, and the operational feed emits P ∗ only on ACT, computed at the regularized level. On the\npresentations in ACT, the re-evaluation of competitive scenarios θsc (r), r ∈ {0, r̂, 1}, issues the final verdict:\nrobust to the game, conditioned, or holding dominates. Each emitted recommendation additionally carries\nthe decision arithmetic of Section A.11 (tolerable volume loss, critical elasticity, and range stability) and the\ncross-system flag of Section A.15.\n\nA.14\n\nStructural-Break Tests: Operating Parameters\n\nGuard 5 runs two complementary tests on the residual of the causal model, both on weekly aggregates.\nThe aggregation is a correction and not a convenience: the Brownian-bridge limit of the CUSUM and the F\ndistribution of the Chow statistic both assume approximately independent observations, and running either\non rows with six regions per week inflates the effective sample size and renders both tests liberal.\nOLS-CUSUM. The cumulative sum of residuals is standardized by a Bartlett long-run variance [Newey\nand West, 1987] with bandwidth q = ⌊4(W/100)2/9 ⌋, and compared against the asymptotic critical value\n1.358 at the 5% level [Ploberger and Krämer, 1992]. Detection requires no prior knowledge of the break date\n[Andrews, 1993].\nChow tests. Candidate dates are restricted a priori to weeks carrying an own list change or a competitor\njump above 2.5%, which keeps the multiplicity finite and economically motivated. The sample is trimmed by\n15% at each end so that no candidate date falls where a subsample would be too short to estimate. The\nfamily is corrected by Bonferroni and a break is declared at p < 0.01 [Chow, 1960].\nRecency. A break counts as recent, and therefore vetoes, when it falls within the last 12 weeks; an older\nbreak is recorded without vetoing, since re-estimation already lives in the new regime. A recent break\nadditionally marks the sequential state of the pooling layer as reset (Section A.6).\nSize is controlled by three devices (aggregation, a priori candidates, Bonferroni), and none of them speaks to\npower. Appendix C.2 reports both, and the operating parameters above should be read together with what it\nmeasures.\nThe asymptotic critical value is mis-specified for these residuals. The value 1.358 is the asymptotic\n5% level for an OLS-CUSUM on independent residuals. On residuals from a boosted nuisance learner it\nproduces a rejection rate of 0.679 with no break present; on residuals from a detrended or linear learner\n— gbr-detrend, sieve, or sieve-detrend alike — it produces a rejection rate of exactly zero. It is therefore\nmis-specified in both directions and not merely conservative or liberal. Calibrating it by simulation under the\nnull returns 1.72, 0.733, 1.123, and 0.832 on the four residual paths tested (Table D.7), which restores the\nsize to as low as 0 and no higher than 0.107 on any path. Any deployment of this guard should calibrate the\ncritical value against the residual it will actually be fed rather than adopt the tabulated one, and should do\nso per residual path, since the four calibrated values span more than a factor of two.\nCalibration is necessary and not sufficient. With size restored, the detection rate at the largest break\nthe generator injects does not exceed 0.161 on any of the four residual paths, and only one of the four —\nthe sieve learner on detrended residuals — discriminates break from no-break at all once the critical value\n\n42\n\n\fis calibrated; the other three do not. The property the guard’s veto actually consumes is narrower still:\nrestricted to week 112, the break date inside the twelve-week recency window, the best calibrated detection\nrate across all four paths is 0.286, against 0.214 at week 80, outside the window. The guard is therefore\nreported as not operative: the recency veto and the sequential reset described above are specified here and\nwithdrawn from the decision layer until a configuration is found that discriminates. The parameters in this\nappendix document the design, not a running component.\n\nA.15\n\nCross-System Interference: Quota Controls and the Impact Flag\n\nCommercial-Cycle Controls. The incentive cycle enters W under a declared hierarchy of sources. Where\nthe panel supplies the true quota fields {quota, attainment, effective discount}, they are used directly and the\ncalendar proxy becomes unnecessary. Where it does not, the cycle is proxied by {dclose\n, ⊮[month-end]t } (days\nt\nto period close and a closing-window indicator, both pre by construction and both functions of the day of\nthe month, hence requiring sub-monthly grain to vary at all). Where neither is available, Guard 8 is declared\nnon-operative rather than executed on constants: a contrast between two identical specifications returns\nzero, which is indistinguishable from a clean verdict and would report a silent failure as a pass. Guard 8 is\nthus the only component of the system whose identification depends on the temporal resolution of the panel\n(Appendix B.2). Guard 8 is the analogue of Section A.7,\n∆quota = θ̂(W ∪ cycle) − θ̂(W ) > δquota ⇒ WAIT,\nwith the calendar variables serving as a proxy for an incentive intensity the firm measures but does not\nyet publish to the panel. The expected sign of the contamination is specific: end-of-period effort lowers\nthe effective price and raises volume in the same weeks, Conditioning on a mediator inflates |θ|; the engine\nunder-recommends increases and the number stays plausible [Oyer, 1998, Larkin, 2014].\nCross-System Impact Flag. For a recommendation with relative move ∆p and decision-grade elasticity\nθdec , the declared cost to the adjacent quota-allocation model is the expected volume response\n∆Q̂ = (1 + ∆p)θdec − 1,\nreported on a three-level scale (no impact, review with the owning team, conflict requiring agreement before\nexecution) against governance cutoffs on |∆Q̂|. The quantity is deliberately elementary: its purpose is not\nprecision but the existence of a common unit in which two optimizers with incompatible objectives can be\nadjudicated on the same page. The convention is defined over adjacent systems generally, not over this one\ninstance.\n\nA.16\n\nExperimental Design: Holdouts, Synthetic Control, and Contamination\n\nAssignment. Retained regions are drawn by Rhold = sample(R, ⌈f |R|⌉) under a generator seeded by a\ndeterministic hash of the cycle identifier, so the draw is reproducible and auditable ex post; f is a governance\nparameter.\nSynthetic Control Layer. With yrt = log Qrt , treated set T , control set C = Rhold , and intervention date\nt0 , weights solve\n\u00112\nX\u0010\nP\nω̂ = arg\nmin′\nȳT t − r∈C ωr yrt ,\nω≥0, 1 ω=1\n\nt<t0\n\nand the treatment effect is estimated via the double difference\n[\nAT\nT = (ȳT ,post − ȳT ,pre ) − (ω̂ ′ ȳC,post − ω̂ ′ ȳC,pre ).\nSubtracting the pre-treatment baseline difference is mathematically equivalent to incorporating an unconstrained intercept term α = ȳT ,pre − ω̂ ′ ȳC,pre . While weight estimation restricts ω̂ to convex unit weights\nas in Abadie et al. [2010], this level adjustment aligns the estimator with the intercept-adjusted synthetic\n43\n\n\fcontrol framework of Doudchenko and Imbens [2016] and the spatial weighting layer of synthetic difference-indifferences [Arkhangelsky et al., 2021], ensuring invariance to constant unobserved level shifts. The pre-period\nfit RMSE is published with the estimate: without it the effect is not interpretable, since an arbitrarily poor\npre-fit can produce an arbitrary post-period gap. Absent a real intervention the quantity is a placebo and\nmust be near zero; a material departure diagnoses an unbalanced assignment rather than an effect.\nPermutation Inference. The estimator supplies no analytical standard error, and with |R| = 6 regions an\nasymptotic one would not be credible. Inference is therefore by permutation over the assignment mechanism\nitself: re-drawing Rhold under alternative seeds of the deterministic hash enumerates assignments that could\n[\nhave been realized, and re-computing AT\nT on each yields its distribution under the null of no intervention.\nThe reported quantity is the permutation p-value of the observed effect against that distribution, which is\nwhat turns a placebo departure from zero into a diagnosis of unbalanced assignment rather than an impression\nof one.\nThe resolution of that p-value is fixed by the size of the region set and is declared as a ceiling on what the\nlayer can conclude.\nWith f = 0.25 and |R| = 6 the holdout is ⌈f |R|⌉ = 2 regions, so the assignment space\n\u0001\ncontains 62 = 15 points and\n1\nmin pperm = 6\u0001 = 0.067,\n2\n\nplacing every attainable p-value above a conventional 5% threshold. The operative level is therefore αperm\n(Table D.14), set with this floor in view, and no effect estimated on a six-region panel is declared significant\nat 5% regardless of magnitude. Two further properties follow from the same arithmetic. The loop enumerates\nall fifteen assignments exhaustively rather than resampling them, so the reported p-value carries no Monte\nCarlo noise beyond what the exact permutation distribution itself contributes; enumeration is the operating\nform of this test at every panel width validated in Appendix C.9, not a simplification available only at this\none. And with |C| = 2 the weight vector ω̂ has one free parameter, so the synthetic control is close to a simple\naverage of the two retained regions and its pre-period fit is correspondingly unconstrained; the estimator\nearns the name only as the donor pool grows.\nBecause all three properties are functions of |R| alone, they are measurable by varying it, and Appendix C.9\ndoes so at |R| ∈ {6, 12, 20, 30} over 300 replications each, enumerating the assignment\nspace exhaustively\n\u0001\n\u0001 at\nevery width tested, including the widest. The assignment space grows from 62 = 15 points through 12\n2 = 66\n\u0001\n\u0001\n20\n30\nand 2 = 190 to 2 = 435, and the attainable floor on pperm falls from 0.067 through 0.015 and 0.005\nto 0.002. That floor is a combinatorial property of the exact test and not an estimated one: at six regions\nno result can cross the nominal 5% level regardless of the data, so the false-positive rate at that width is\na structural 0 rather than a measured one. It rises to 0.06, 0.033, and 0.06 as the panel widens and the\nfloor stops binding, which is the test becoming operative rather than a new defect. Over the same range,\nthe donor pool grows from 4 to 28 regions, of which 3.9 and then 12.1 carry non-zero weight, the effective\n(inverse-Herfindahl) donor count rises from 3.16 to 7.65, the pre-period fit improves from an RMSE of 0.189\nto 0.133, and power against the 0.12 log-point shock the generator injects rises from a structural 0 to 0.227.\nAll four move together because the donor pool grows and because the exact-test floor stops being the active\nconstraint; enumeration remains exhaustive and cheap across the whole range tested here, so no width in this\ndesign forces a switch to sampling.\nContamination of the Control (SUTVA). The no-interference assumption [Rubin, 1980] is tested\ncomp\nrather than presumed. With ℓrt = log Prt\n, define the group-wise pre–post shift g(G, τ ) = ℓG,t≥τ − ℓG,t<τ\nand the discrepancy D(τ ) = g(C, τ ) − g(T , τ ). The test compares D(t0 ) against the panel’s own placebo\ndistribution, obtained by sweeping false cuts τpl across the pre-period, and declares contamination when\npperm =\n\nX \b\n1\n⊮ |D(τpl )| ≥ |D(t0 )| < αperm\n|Tpl |\n\n∧\n\n|D(t0 )| ≥ εmin ,\n\nτpl ∈Tpl\n\nwith εmin = 0.005 a minimum economic magnitude in log price. Both conditions are required, and for different\nreasons: the permutation distribution supplies the noise scale of these data but no notion of materiality, so\n44\n\n\fon a quiet panel it will flag differences of thousandths; and a fixed magnitude supplies materiality but is\narbitrary across panels. Sweeping the false cut across the whole pre-period, rather than testing D(t0 ) against\na single reference cut, is what lets the verdict be independent of where any one cut happens to fall.\n\nA.17\n\nGovernance Thresholds and Correspondence with the Implementation\n\nEvery threshold in the system is a governance switch, modifiable by configuration without touching the\nmodel, and every one of them is reported here with its operating value. A methodological paper that leaves\nits cutoffs as symbols asks a reader to evaluate a design whose behaviour it has not disclosed; Table D.14\ncloses that gap, and every value in it must be recalibrated against a real category before being fixed.\nThe fourth column reports the sensitivity of the ACT/WAIT rate to moving a threshold, which is what\ndistinguishes a calibrated cutoff from an arbitrary one, and its coverage is partial by design rather than by\nomission. The sweep runs over the six thresholds that constitute the decision gate itself, where a change\npropagates directly to the verdict: the minimum robustness value, the broken-weeks regime gap, the maximum\nshare of flagged weeks, the minimum partial R2 , the maximum interval width, and the P ∗ stability tolerance.\nEvery other row carries a dash, meaning not that the threshold is insensitive but that it was not swept. The\ndistinction matters for how the table should be used: a dash is an absence of evidence about that switch, and\nthe committee that proposes to move one of them is proposing a change whose effect on the ACT rate this\npaper has not measured.\nThe third column requires the same candour, because “Basis” is doing two different jobs in it. A minority\nof the entries are derived or conventional in a defensible external sense: the CUSUM critical value is the\nasymptotic 5% level, fmin is the conventional weak-instrument cutoff [Staiger and Stock, 1997], the block\nlength follows the stated formula, and α is a nominal miscoverage the committee chooses. The majority are\ndeclared conventions: round numbers chosen by the authors as starting points, with no derivation and no\nsource behind the level. This applies to the QA Gate’s R2 ≥ 0.65 and its coverage tolerance of 0.03, to the\nfour broken-week signatures (0.5% own move, 2.5% competitor jump, the 75th promotional percentile, −4%\nwith volume at 1.5× the median), to the pass-through regime cutoffs of 0.30 and 0.80, and to RVmin = 0.12,\nfor which Cinelli and Hazlett [2020] supply the statistic but no threshold on it.\nWorth calling out directly, the threshold δ = 0.30 serves as the tolerance for five separate checks: the regime\ngap of Guard 1, the IV/DML divergence of Guard 4, the mediator bracket of Guard 6, the exclusion-audit\nresidual correlation of Guard 7, and the quota bracket of Guard 8. These five bounds coincide not by\nstructural necessity, but because a single round figure was selected for all of them initially. Unified until\ncategory-level recalibration differentiates them, these thresholds act as governance switches whose initial\norigin must be declared separately from their adjustment rule.\nDeclared open gaps. Three limitations of this calibration are recorded as open rather than resolved.\nConformal validity is verified marginally and by rolling origins, but not conditionally on regime: a band can\nbe narrow and correct on average while failing in the regime that matters. Every threshold above is calibrated\nagainst the generator and none against a commercial category. And promotional incrementality is outside the\nsystem’s scope: promotion is treated as a confounder to be removed, never as a lever to be optimized.\n\n45\n\n\fB\n\nExtended Methodology\n\nSection 4 states each layer of the system in the space a journal article allows. This appendix carries the\nexposition it was condensed from: the generators in full, the argument for the temporal grain, the diagnostic\nand its cutoffs, the confounder architecture, the pooling layer, the demand system, the two intermediated\nretail extensions, the optimizer, the eight guards and the ten abstention causes they define, the architectural\nprinciples, and the validation protocol. Nothing here is new relative to the run reported in Section 6; it is the\ndetail a reader who wants to reproduce or to audit the design will need, and it is kept verbatim so that the\nmain text can be read as a summary of it rather than as a substitute.\n\nB.1\n\nData Generating Process\n\nThe data generating process (DGP; Cadahia [2026]) simulates a weekly panel (W = 120 weeks, W-MON)\nacross six regions and six product presentations (spanning three brands and two categories), replicating the\nportfolio structure in Section 3. To capture realistic confounding, volume is generated jointly with prices,\ncosts, promotional activity, calendar effects (holidays, working days), weather, national minimum wage, and\nan inflation index.\nFive distinct identification regimes are assigned across presentations, each defined by a specific own-price\nelasticity (ε) and structural challenge:\n• Clean (ε = −1.3, ρ = 0.85): Baseline regime featuring 8–12 list price changes of 3.0–7.5% per series.\n• round-point (ε = −1.1, ρ = 0): Shelf prices are pinned to psychological price points, so no list\nmovement reaches the consumer and the list-price elasticity is zero by construction.\n• Weak (ε = −1.0, ρ = 0.85): Minimal variation (two changes of 0.4–0.9%), serving as a diagnostic\nboundary case for non-identification.\n• Confounded (ε = −1.4, ρ = 0.85): Price changes systematically co-occur with promotions.\n• Collinear (ε = −1.2, ρ = 0.85): Own price closely follows competitor pricing, limiting channel\nseparability.\nFour additional empirical mechanisms are injected: (i) Pass-through: shelf prices are generated from list\nprices at the regime-specific rate ρ declared above, so the elasticity the causal layer targets is θ = ερ rather\nthan ε; (ii) Cross-price response: A +0.30 elasticity on log competitor price enters the demand equation;\n(iii) Forward buying: Sell-in inflates by 30% during the two weeks preceding list price increases > 2%, while\nsell-out remains unaffected (reproducing the target outcome variable’s structural bias); and (iv) Delisting:\nMargin-dependent product attrition induces endogenous sample selection.\nMethodological Caveats. Two structural features bound the empirical scope of the generator: first, retail\nmargins are nearly deterministic functions of list price and promotion, exercising the margin-state framework\n(Appendix B.7) architecturally rather than validating it empirically. Second, the cash-pressure index relies on\na smooth national income trend, lacking regional identifying variation.\nThe extended generator. A generator with known answers is also a generator with chosen answers, and\na design decision that happens to suit one configuration will validate on it. The run reported in Section 6\ntherefore uses a second generator alongside the first, and reports both wherever they disagree. The extended\ngenerator draws eight presentations across a wider set of brands, and differs from the five-regime one in three\nways that were chosen because they remove features the first generator makes artificially clean. Pass-through\nis drawn per region around a presentation-level mean rather than fixed by regime, so that ρ ranges from\n0.658 to 0.984 with a regional standard deviation between 0.096 and 0.214 and no presentation shares its\ntransmission with any other. The identification regimes are attached to individual presentations rather than\nto pairs, so that a regime is not confounded with a brand. And the injected elasticities are drawn over a\nwider range, from −0.9 to −1.4, so that recovery is not measured at a single magnitude.\nTwo structural features of the first generator, both of which the second retains by design, are declared here\nbecause they bound what either can establish: both are stationary apart from an inflationary drift, and in\nboth the confounding mechanisms are injected at magnitudes the designer chose. A result that holds on both\n46\n\n\fis more credible than one that holds on either; neither is a commercial panel, and Section 8.4 states what\nthat costs.\nThe two agree on the estimation results and disagree on the gating results, and the disagreement is among\nthe more informative outputs of the run: a property that holds on one generator and not on the other is\na property of a configuration and not of the method (Sections 6.1 and 6.7). The extended generator also\nsupplies the units the hierarchical experiments of Sections 6.3 and 6.4 require, since a between-unit variance\ncomponent cannot be estimated from a portfolio in which only two presentations are identifiable; those\nexperiments run at eight presentations, and the level-against-the-gate experiment of Section 6.5 widens the\nportfolio further, to twenty-four, so that the brand and category levels have enough units to estimate a\nheterogeneity component of their own.\n\nB.2\n\nWhy estimation is weekly and decision is monthly\n\nThe business runs a monthly pricing cycle, and it would be natural to build the panel on that cycle. The\nsystem does not, and the reason is not convenience: three components lose identification or power under\nmonthly aggregation, and none of them is recovered by any downstream sophistication. Setting the argument\nout explicitly is worth a subsection, because “model at the grain at which you decide” is a common and\ncostly default.\nWhat aggregation destroys. The quota-pressure guard (Appendix B.11, Guard 8) identifies the contamination of the commercial incentive cycle from variables defined on the day of the month: the distance\nto period close and an indicator for the closing window. On a weekly panel these take four or five distinct\nvalues within each month; on a monthly panel they are constant, the guard’s contrast is identically zero, and\na real confounder becomes undetectable rather than absent. The cash-pressure index of Appendix B.8 loses\nits calendar component for the same reason, retaining only a national real-income term with no regional\nvariation. And the structural-break tests of Guard 5 lose most of their power: with roughly twenty-eight\nmonthly observations in place of a hundred and twenty weekly ones, a CUSUM has little left to detect with,\nso an expired elasticity would keep authorizing decisions.\nWhat aggregation would buy, and why it is not needed. The one genuine argument for the monthly\ngrain is that it would cancel the forward-buying bias within the observation, since a purchase pulled forward\ntwo weeks stays inside the same month. The system already neutralizes that bias by a different route, taking\nsell-out rather than sell-in as the outcome: the consumer does not stockpile to meet a salesperson’s target.\nAppendix C.1 measures both the bias and the remedy on the generator, and the measurement returns a null:\nthe injected contamination does not displace the estimate materially in this DGP, so the choice of outcome\nvariable rests on the argument and on the independent one of Appendix B.12, not on a magnitude this panel\nsupplies. Reporting it that way is the point; the alternative would be to keep a quantitative justification the\nexperiment declined to provide.\nThe division adopted. Estimation, resampling, calibration, and break detection run on the week. The\ndecision layer aggregates to the monthly cycle at which the committee meets and the operational feed\npublishes. Nothing in the commercial process changes: the committee still decides monthly, on evidence that\nwas assembled at the grain where it is identifiable. The one component whose identification depends on the\npanel’s temporal resolution is Guard 8. Rather than a defect, that is a result worth reporting, since it locates\nprecisely where the grain of a panel becomes an identification question rather than a storage decision.\n\nB.3\n\nVariation Diagnostic and Feasibility Assessment\n\nPrice elasticity estimation requires not only sufficient price variation but also unconfounded variation.\nCollinearity between prices and simultaneous interventions (such as promotional campaigns or competitor\nprice changes) prevents statistical identification regardless of downstream estimation complexity. To address\nthese identification constraints, every presentation × region cell undergoes a diagnostic evaluation comprising\nfive quantitative criteria. Key failure modes include competitor collinearity (where own-price changes\n47\n\n\fmirror competitor movements) and price rigidity or \"frozen shelves\" (where consumer prices remain fixed at\npsychological price points while volume fluctuates due to unobserved levers, generating spurious elasticity\nestimates).\nSpecifically, a presentation × region cell is declared modelable if it satisfies five joint conditions:\n1. Deflated list price coefficient of variation (CV) > 0.03.\n2. At least four discrete price changes, defined as |∆ log p| > 0.005 to exclude rounding and deflation noise.\n3. Absolute price–promotion correlation < 0.6.\n4. Absolute price–competitor correlation < 0.9.\n5. Non-frozen shelf status, defined as a sell-out price coefficient of variation CV(P so ) ≥ 0.01 and ≤ 5%\nzero-volume weeks. The conjunction is deliberate: a shelf pinned to a round point carries a degenerate\nprice distribution while volume flows normally, so a disjunctive rule would fail to reject the frozen-shelf\nregime.\nAn additional sample size threshold requires at least 40 usable observations for point estimation. Section 6.6\nmaps these operating cutoffs onto the minimum detectable effect (MDE) frontier.\nCrucially, the diagnostic evaluates real (deflated) prices rather than nominal figures; persistent inflation\ngenerates spurious nominal price changes that would artificially inflate move counts. Units failing the\ndiagnostic bypass direct point estimation, inheriting a hierarchical elasticity pooled at the brand level\n(Appendix B.5) and marked as non-actionable.\nThe admissibility gate. While the variation diagnostic evaluates data feasibility, the admissibility gate\ndetermines whether an estimated elasticity is statistically fit for decision-making. A presentation is declared\nadmissible if three conditions hold jointly:\n1. The variation diagnostic is passed in at least half of the constituent regions (≥ 1/2, not a strict majority:\nthe implementation tests ≥ 0.5).\n2. The treatment partial R2 after residualization exceeds 0.10. This ensures sufficient residual treatment\nvariance to prevent variance explosion in the orthogonalized DML estimator [Chernozhukov et al., 2018,\nCinelli and Hazlett, 2020].\n3. The bootstrap confidence interval width for the raw elasticity is below 0.6. This operationalizes selective\nprediction under estimation uncertainty [El-Yaniv and Wiener, 2010], ensuring downstream optimization\nalgorithms receive actionable signal rather than wide, decision-neutral bounds.\nArchitecturally, admissibility is evaluated prior to the decision guards (Appendix B.11). Ineligible presentations\nare immediately routed to a WAIT status, bypassing downstream guard evaluations. Furthermore, admissibility\nconditions the input set for conformal calibration and QA Gate scoring. This sequential ordering structures the\nmulti-stage abstention funnel (Section 6.8), systematically isolating data signal feasibility from decision policy\nevaluation. Finally, the diagnostic estimates a reduced-form competitor reaction function using log-price\ncross-correlograms and a distributed-lag regression. This yields a parameter r̂ capturing the competitor’s\ncumulative price reaction and response latency. Although descriptive rather than causal (due to the absence\nof a competitor instrument), r̂ serves four functions: ...it makes explicit which of the three estimands of\nSection 4.1 the competitive bracket contrasts, enters the internal consistency identity θtotal − θcond ≈ θcross · r̂,\ninforms competitive scenario analysis (Section 4.4), and detects asymmetric price-following dynamics.\n\nB.4\n\nConfounder architecture, temporal status and the causal extensions\n\nWeekly-Grain Confounder Architecture. Three specific properties of W arise from the weekly panel\nstructure and require explicit architectural accounting:\n1. Non-parametric Seasonality: Week-of-year enters as a continuous integer rather than fifty-two\ncategorical indicators. Gradient-boosted nuisance estimators split on this feature non-parametrically,\n\n48\n\n\fabsorbing seasonal patterns without imposing explicit degrees-of-freedom penalties on short panels (e.g.,\n120 weeks).\n2. Collinearity Management: Weekly working days and holidays exhibit deterministic linear dependence\n(Working Days = Total Days − Holidays). Admitting both unadjusted destabilizes linear downstream\nsteps; the framework mandates explicit relation declarations and ingests either a single component or\nits orthogonalized residual.\n3. Exogenous Demand Drivers: Weather, operationalized as weekly regional mean temperature, drives\ndemand independently of list prices. Its role is therefore a precision covariate and not a confounder:\na confounder moves both the treatment and the outcome, and weather moves the outcome alone.\nAdmitting it to W is correct on efficiency grounds, since it absorbs outcome variance that would\notherwise inflate the residual, but it is not part of the identification argument and is not described as\nthough it were.\nTemporal Conditioning and Mediator Diagnostics. A central identification condition dictates that\nelements of W must be measured prior to the pricing decision. Conditioning on post-treatment realizations\nblocks causal pathways and alters the target estimand [Rosenbaum, 1984, VanderWeele, 2015]. Unlike\nstructurally bracketed competitor prices, commercial tactics (promotions, distribution, stockouts) adjust\nalongside weekly list prices, making them potential mediators. The architecture enforces an explicit declared\ntemporal status (pre or post) for every covariate in W , rejecting undeclared controls. Elasticities are\nreported both with and without post-treatment commercial controls. The resulting differential acts as an\nexplicit diagnostic rather than an automated pruning mechanism; large gaps signal over-conditioning on\nendogenous responses. Under the sign structure assumed for this channel (trade activity increasing when\nlist price rises), the indirect path through the mediator carries the opposite sign to the direct effect, so\nconditioning on post-treatment commercial controls returns the direct effect and inflates the estimate rather\nthan attenuating it (|θ̂post | > |θ0 |). The consequence for the recommendation follows from the optimizer’s\nown iso-elastic form, whose interior optimum P ∗ = Cθ/(1 + θ) carries a markup P ∗ /C = θ/(1 + θ) that\nfalls toward one as |θ| grows: over-conditioning therefore shrinks the recommended move and generates\nsystematically conservative recommendations that remain deceptively plausible. The direction is a function\nof the assumed sign structure and not of the estimator: were trade activity to contract when list price rises,\nthe same over-conditioning would attenuate |θ̂| and the failure mode would be an aggressive one.\nImplementation Decisions and Inference Rules. Four operational decisions govern the estimation\nsystem:\n1. Contiguous Block Cross-Fitting (K = 5): To avoid cross-sectional information leakage within the\nsame week, cross-fitting folds are partitioned into contiguous temporal blocks [Bergmeir and Benítez,\n2012], binding all regional observations of a given week to a single fold. This non-forward-chaining scheme\nrelies on DGP stationarity and weak temporal dependence [Chiang et al., 2022] rather than Neyman\northogonality, which pertains to moment conditions, not temporal information sets. Appendix C.4\nmeasures the cost when this assumption is violated. Division of labor delegates strict chronological\nrequirements to downstream modules (e.g., conformal prediction, backtesting).\n2. Shallow Nuisance Learners: Nuisance models utilize shallow gradient-boosted trees (depth 3, 200\nestimators, learning rate 0.05, subsample 0.8; expanded to 300 estimators for quantile regressors). A\nregression tree is piecewise constant and cannot extrapolate beyond the range of its training data, so it\nrepresents a monotone trend as a staircase and absorbs it only where splits are dense; low-frequency\ncomponents can therefore persist into the residuals rather than being partialled out; Appendix C.3\ncontrasts this choice against regularized trend-harmonic learners.\n3. Weekly Moving-Block Bootstrap: Inference is constructed using a moving-block bootstrap clustered by week [Künsch, 1989], accommodating both serial autocorrelation and regional cross-sectional\ndependence. Block length is parameterized as L = clip(round(1.5 W 1/3 ), 2, ⌊W/3⌋) (yielding L = 7 for\nW = 120, since 1.5 · 1201/3 = 7.40). Replication parameters and coverage properties are detailed in\nAppendix A.2 and Section 6.2.\n49\n\n\f4. Median-of-Folds Aggregation: Point estimates are reported as the median across fold estimates\n(θ̃ = mediank θ̂k ) rather than the pooled DML2 aggregate. On short panels with five temporal folds, a\nsingle atypical fold materially displaces pooled estimators; the median provides robust finite-sample\nprotection against that risk. Section 6.2 tests the choice against known truth for both learners and finds\nneither paired contrast significant: a paired t of 1.99 for the production learner (sieve) and −1.43 for\nthe boosted alternative, both short of the conventional threshold. The preference is therefore declared\nas a convention rather than as a validated result, and pooled forms remain restricted to diagnostic\nlayers.\nEffective Sample Size Principle. A foundational design principle underpins these choices (Appendix B.14):\nthe effective sample size is dictated by independent temporal periods, not raw row counts. Multiple regional\nobservations within a single week represent one draw from the underlying macro shock. All statistical inference\nfeeding the decision guards (Appendix B.11) evaluates uncertainty over the interval distribution rather than\npoint estimates.\nLimits of Identification and Unobserved Confounding. DML acknowledges three intrinsic boundaries:\nfunctional form choices shift to the outcome model, extrapolation beyond observed support remains unanchored,\nand residualization addresses only observed confounders. A fourth is specific to this channel and is not\na property of the estimator: the treatment is the list price, so what is identified is the manufacturer’s\nlever composed with the shopkeeper’s response, and separating the two requires the pass-through layer of\nAppendix B.7 rather than a better nuisance model. To evaluate unobserved endogeneity, two additional\nstructural contrasts are embedded:\n• Instrumental Contrast (PLIV) and Exclusion Audit:Identification relies on a partially linear\ninstrumental variables (PLIV) framework, using log variable costs (Z = log Cost) as an instrument\nto isolate supply-side shocks from raw materials, packaging substrates, exchange rates, and excise\ntaxes.[Chernozhukov et al., 2018]. Divergence between observational and instrumental estimates forms\nan operational Hausman test [Hausman, 1978]. However, category-wide cost shocks induce common\ncompetitor price shifts, violating the exclusion restriction and causing both estimators to displace\njointly, understating the Hausman contrast [Angrist et al., 1996]. The framework audits this restriction\ndirectly by examining the residual correlation between residualized instruments Z̃ and residualized\ncompetitor prices log^\nP comp (augmented with an explicit time index t to prevent unremoved trend\ninflation). Identification is restored by disaggregating variable costs across packaging format families\n(e.g., returnable glass vs. single-use containers), where format-specific input cost dynamics break\ncategory-wide reflection channels (Section A.5).\n• Competitive Bracket: The framework decomposes price response into three explicit estimands:\nconditional elasticity (competitor price in W ), historical-equilibrium elasticity (competitor price omitted\nfrom W ), and cross-elasticity (competitor price as treatment). The triad admits an internal consistency\ncheck: the gap between conditional and total elasticity should approximate the product of cross-elasticity\nand the competitor’s reaction function. The check binds only where the competitor moves and reacts,\nsince an inert competitor drives both sides to zero and satisfies the identity vacuously; Section 6.1\nestablishes on which panel it is contrastable before reporting it.\nCausal Extensions: Asymmetry and Heterogeneity. Two structural extensions complete the causal\nestimation layer:\n• Dual Beta (Asymmetric Response): Captures demand hysteresis and psychological threshold effects\n[Hardie et al., 1993, Levy et al., 2011], where price increases and cuts exhibit asymmetric elasticities.\nTo avoid selection bias from splitting samples by treatment sign, a Houck cumulative decomposition\n[Houck, 1977] is applied to the deflated real price within a single residualized joint regression. Deflation\nprevents cumulative upward components from collapsing into collinearity with macro inflation in W .\nIf directional event counts fall below the minimum identification threshold (nmin = 20 events per\ndirection), the estimator reverts to the symmetric baseline to avoid publishing unsupported asymmetries\n(Appendix A.3). This component is described here as specified and is withdrawn from the published\n50\n\n\foutput on the evidence of Appendix C.6, where its size and its power are measured and found to be\nindistinguishable; the architecture is retained for a panel that can support it.\n• Regional Heterogeneity Diagnostic: Evaluates variation across regional elasticities using a precisionweighted Cochran Q test [Cochran, 1954], verifying whether regional divergence exceeds estimation\nnoise prior to deploying regional parameterization.\n\nB.5\n\nHierarchical regularization: partial pooling with a decision-grade elasticity\n\nEstimating hundreds of independent elasticities guarantees overfitting, as most individual presentations carry\nlittle signal of their own. The hierarchy Category > Brand > Presentation allows each estimate to be\nshrunk toward its branch via a conjugate normal–normal update, yielding a posterior elasticity that is the\nprecision-weighted average of a prior and the cycle’s own estimate (see Appendix A.6 for formal equations).\nThis mechanism relies on three distinct origins for the prior:\n1. Hierarchical branch (cross-sectional): The prior mean is the leave-one-out brand mean (preventing\nself-shrinkage), with the brand shrunk toward its category. Between-presentation heterogeneity τ 2 is\nestimated via the Paule–Mandel estimator [Paule and Mandel, 1982]. To avoid mechanism degradation,\ntwo pitfalls are avoided: using sample variance instead of τ 2 (which inflates heterogeneity with noise)\nand summing raw precisions rather than incorporating τ̂ 2 into branch precisions [Efron and Morris,\n1975].\n2. Previous cycle (sequential): A local-level dynamic linear model with a discount factor δ = 0.90\n[West and Harrison, 1997]. The posterior of cycle t − 1, with variance inflated by δ, acts as the prior\nfor cycle t. At δ = 0.90, information weight halves in roughly seven cycles, establishing the system’s\noperational memory decay. Sequential states are versioned per cycle.\n3. Cold start and structural breaks: A presentation lacking an own estimate receives the branch\nprior with its full predictive variance as posterior. If a structural break is detected by Guard 5\n(Appendix B.11), the sequential state resets, expiring the prior with the dead regime and restarting\nfrom the branch. This path is specified and currently inert, since Appendix C.2 reports the break guard\nas not operative; a reset mechanism that depends on a detection the guard cannot make never fires,\nand saying so is preferable to leaving a dead branch documented as live.\nThe output is a decision-grade elasticity, θdec , traveling with its posterior variance, own-data weight, and\ndeclared origin. Its role is strictly bounded: the ACT/WAIT decision gate (Appendix B.11) votes solely with\nthe raw causal estimate θ̂ so that pooling never unlocks movements. Conversely, θdec sets the absolute level\nof published price and elasticity curves.\nThe heterogeneity component and design variance. The between-unit heterogeneity τ̂ 2 that this\nlayer estimates in order to set the shrinkage weight also measures something a within-panel bootstrap cannot:\ndispersion arising from the realized design itself rather\nthan from sampling noise around it (Section 6.3\n√\n2\nsketches why). An interval of the form θ̂ ± 1.96 s + τ̂ 2 , with s2 the within-unit variance and τ̂ 2 the\nPaule–Mandel estimate already computed for the shrinkage [Paule and Mandel, 1982], is one way to fold that\nsource back into the reported uncertainty; its coverage properties relative to the production bootstrap are the\nsubject of companion methodological work [Delgado, 2026] rather than of this paper. The change is identified\nand not yet adopted in the production configuration, and that status is declared wherever a figure produced\nunder the current construction is reported.\nOne consequence for this layer is unfavourable and is stated with the rest. Where the raw estimate is\nmis-centred, shrinkage can move the interval toward a displaced branch mean while narrowing it, so the\npooling posterior’s coverage is not guaranteed to improve on the bootstrap it shrinks. Partial pooling is not\nassumed to help the interval merely because it helps the point estimate, and a system that publishes both\nshould say so.\nThis framework concentrates estimation effort following a Pareto principle: high-revenue presentations receive\nfull causal estimation alongside live price tests, whereas the long tail relies on sequentially updated hierarchical\n51\n\n\fpriors rather than fabricated figures. Full mathematical updates, Paule–Mandel numerical solving, and\ndeclared technical approximations are detailed in Appendix A.6.\n\nB.6\n\nCross-elasticities: an identified demand system, not a correlation matrix\n\nPortfolio decisions require knowing how much presentations cannibalize one another. The system estimates a\ncross-elasticity matrix through an AIDS-type demand system, the Almost Ideal Demand System [Deaton and\nMuellbauer, 1980], in its linear approximation over revenue shares deflated by the Stone index [Stone, 1954].\nThe identification correction is the central contribution of this layer: the standard practice in the linear\napproximation of this system [Deaton and Muellbauer, 1980, Stone, 1954] regresses shares on raw prices by\nleast squares, which confounds the price effect with everything that moved at the same time and invents\nspurious substitutions: cannibalization where there was only co-movement with promotion. Here the system\nruns exclusively on the residuals of the same causal model of Section 4.1 (the Frisch–Waugh–Lovell\ntheorem guarantees that the coefficients coincide with those of the full system including the confounders,\nbut without the endogeneity of the observational price), reusing the single cross-fitting residualization kernel\nthat feeds every layer of the system. Because equation-by-equation nonlinear residualization makes Neyman\northogonality only approximate in a multi-equation system, the theoretical restrictions (homogeneity, Slutsky\nsymmetry, and adding-up) are not assumed: they are tested explicitly, and the matrix is published only if the\nviolations are of the order of estimation noise.\nThree qualifications keep the claim proportionate to what the layer actually does. The Stone deflator is\nbuilt from observed shares, which is the standard linear approximation and carries the simultaneity that\napproximation is known to introduce; the correction available for it [Moschini, 1995] is not applied, and\nAppendix A.9 states the reason. The confounders are collapsed to the date level by averaging across\npresentations before residualization, which is not the same as residualizing each share equation against its own\nW , and the simplification is declared rather than absorbed. The third qualification is addressed rather than\nmerely declared: the block bootstrap of Appendix A.9, applied jointly to the residualization and the demand\nstage, supplies standard errors that the standard linear-approximation practice does not, so the restrictions\nare contrasted against a null instead of against the dispersion of the estimated coefficients. Appendix C.8\nreports the result, and it is unfavourable: homogeneity and Slutsky symmetry are rejected at the 5% level in\nmost replications. The rule stated above, that the matrix is published only if the violations are of the order\nof estimation noise, is therefore testable, and does not clear its own test; the matrix is published with the\nrejections attached rather than withheld, and the reason is given in that section. One reading rule matters\nmore than any of the three for a committee: the Marshallian elasticity divides by the revenue share, so in\nsmall-share presentations the noise in the coefficients is amplified and the matrix can look visibly non-zero\nwhere the underlying coefficients are not. Cross-elasticities of small presentations are to be read as bands,\nand the artifact reports them as such.\n\nB.7\n\nPass-through as a process, not a constant\n\nThe elasticity estimated by the causal layer, which measures volume against the sell-in price, is not the\nstructural consumer elasticity. Instead, it is a convolution of consumer elasticity relative to the shelf price and\nshopkeeper pass-through, defined as the derivative of the shelf price with respect to the list price. Treating\nthat pass-through as an implicit constant, as standard practice does, is flawed: the empirical literature finds\npass-through rates that are neither unitary nor homogeneous across products and retailers [Besanko et al.,\n2005], and two presentations with identical observed elasticities under different pass-through regimes require\nfundamentally different pricing decisions.\nThe system estimates pass-through as a process with three components.\nFirst, a pass-through elasticity ρ per presentation, estimated from a single residualized regression of\nthe log shelf price on the log list price, whose magnitude classifies the shopkeeper’s regime against two\ndeclared cutoffs: below 0.30 the shelf is sticky, anchored at the round point, the shopkeeper absorbs the shock\nand the list lever loses traction on the consumer; at or above 0.80 the shopkeeper defends their percentage\nmargin almost fully and the shelf follows the list; between the two the shock is partially absorbed with\nmargin compression. On the generator, where the pass-through is injected at ρ = 0.85 in every regime but\n52\n\n\fround-point, the estimator is contrasted on the two presentations of the clean regime over 19 replications and\nrecovers ρ̂ = 0.911, which classifies the shopkeeper correctly as defending their margin almost fully. This is a\nvalidation result and should be read as one rather than as an observation about any commercial category.\nOne asymmetry in that estimation is deliberate and is declared, because an attentive reader will otherwise\nfind it: ρ is residualized against a reduced confounder set, excluding the competitor’s price and the inflation\nindex, while θ uses the full set. The justification is that the competitor’s list price is not a confounder of the\nshopkeeper’s decision about their own margin, which is a different decision by a different agent from the one\nθ describes. It is defensible, but “the same residualization kernel” does not mean “the same confounders,”\nand the distinction belongs in the text rather than in the code.\nThe two exclusions are not equally defensible, and separating them is the point of stating both. The\ncompetitor’s price is excluded on the substantive argument above. The inflation index is excluded as well,\nand that is a different case: it is a common factor of the list and the shelf price, so dropping it leaves a shared\nnominal drift on both sides of the regression and biases ρ̂ toward one, for exactly the reason the exclusion\naudit of Appendix A.5 adds an explicit time index to the residualization. The bias is measured rather than\nargued: the pass-through experiment estimates ρ with and without the common factor in the reduced set,\nand the displacement is 0.061, in the predicted direction and of the predicted kind. The reduced set returns\nρ̂ = 0.911, above the injected 0.85; admitting the inflation index to the reduced set returns 0.844, which\nrecovers the injected value to within six thousandths. The prediction was that dropping a common factor of\nboth prices would bias ρ̂ towards one, and it does, by an amount that is small in absolute terms but accounts\nfor essentially the whole of the error: restoring the common factor moves the estimate by 0.067 and reduces\nthe miss from 0.061 to 0.006. Two consequences follow. The reduced-set ρ̂ should be read as an upper bound\non the pass-through, as stated, and the bound is now quantified rather than declared. And the specification\nthis layer should adopt is the augmented one: the substantive argument for excluding the competitor’s price\ndoes not extend to the inflation index, and the experiment shows what the conflation costs. That change\nis a one-line one and is recorded here rather than presented as already made, since the figures above were\nproduced under the reduced set the implementation currently runs.\nSecond, a Markov chain over the shopkeeper’s margin state, conditioned on the sign of the list shock\nand stratified by region, which adds to the pass-through level its dispersion and state dependence: toward\nwhich margin band the shopkeeper gravitates and how long it takes to return after absorbing a shock. On\nthe five-regime generator the margin is close to a deterministic function of list price and promotion, so this\ncomponent was exercised as architecture rather than validated as a measurement. The extended generator\nsupplies the missing variation, drawing ρ per region and, in one scenario, conditioning it on the margin\nstate, and the estimator recovers it: a bias of −0.015 under regional heterogeneity and −0.048 under state\ndependence, with a regional dispersion of ρ̂ of 0.172 against a bootstrap standard error of 0.016, so the\nheterogeneity is an order of magnitude above the noise the estimator carries. What does not follow is a\nclaim on the response band: Appendix C.7 shows that even with that heterogeneity injected, pass-through\ncontributes at most 0.6% of the variance of the published band, so the layer is validated as a measurement\nand declared as immaterial to the decision on this panel.\nA methodological note belongs with that pair, because the two results are easy to read as contradictory\nand are not. That an object is estimable and that it matters for a decision are separate questions, and a\nsystem that reports the first as though it settled the second is the failure mode this paper exists to document.\nPass-through is estimable here, well recovered, and heterogeneous; it is also, on this panel, not a material\nsource of uncertainty in the quantity a committee acts on. Both statements are published.\nThird, the deconvolution of θ̂ into consumer elasticity ε̂ = θ̂/ρ̂ and pass-through ρ̂. Because θ̂ is directly\nidentified by Double Machine Learning, all response bands and decision thresholds rely directly on Var(θ̂)\nvia the weekly block-bootstrap. Decoupling θ̂ introduces a negative structural covariance (Cov(ε̂, ρ̂) < 0);\nomitting this term in a Delta-method expansion would artificially inflate uncertainty bounds. Deconvolution\nis thus reported solely as an interpretive diagnostic, while optimization operates directly on θ̂. If ρ̂ < 0.30, ε̂\nis reported as undefined.\nThe system’s honesty requires stating what is not identified in the region×week aggregate: the discrete\nrigidity of the shopkeeper’s repricing, that is, the (S, s)-band behavior [Sheshinski and Weiss, 1977, Nakamura\n53\n\n\fand Steinsson, 2008] in which the shelf jump depends on the untransferred cost accumulated since the\nlast change, averages out and disappears, producing a falsely gradual pass-through [the Caballero–Engel\naggregation bias; Caballero and Engel, 2007]. For when a store-level transactional panel exists, the system\nleaves ready a three-layer structural/Bayesian architecture: a data-quality scoring layer that classifies stores\ninto Gold/Silver/Bronze according to the continuity of shelf-price capture, with repricing detection confirmed\nby persistence so as not to confuse capture noise with real jumps; a layer that estimates the (S, s) physics,\nthat is, the repricing trigger, exclusively on the Gold segment, where individual change events are observable\nwith dates; and a Bayesian transfer layer that carries that physics, as a prior with a learned hypervariance,\ntoward the noisy-data segments. The key conceptual correction of this last layer is that reliability is not\nsampling precision: shrinkage weights anchor to measurement reliability λ rather than nominal standard\nc to prevent corrupt data from appearing spuriously precise. Although attenuation is linear in λ, the\nerrors (SE)\ndisattenuated variance Var(ε̂)/λ2 causes effective precision to scale with λ2 [Fuller, 1987]. Final coverage is\nguaranteed by construction via quality-stratified conformal prediction: the structural model delivers efficiency,\nwhile the conformal layer maintains validity through wider intervals for low-quality data.\n\nB.8\n\nThe budget constraint of the intermediate buyer\n\nThe shopkeeper is not only a price setter; they are a liquidity-constrained buyer. Working capital in the\nintermediated retail channel is finite and its tightness is not constant across the month, so a store may decline\nor truncate an order for reasons that have nothing to do with how well the product sells. This is a distinct\nmechanism from pass-through, and it is worth stating precisely why the obvious way of measuring it is worse\nthan leaving it unmeasured.\nThe intuitive construction (infer the budget from what the store buys from us) is built from the treatment and\nthe outcome simultaneously. Introduced as a control it converts the elasticity into within-basket substitution\nat constant expenditure: if a list increase on one presentation is absorbed by reallocating toward another, the\nmodel observes little aggregate volume loss and recommends a larger increase precisely where substitution\nroom exists. There is, moreover, a measurement problem that precedes any econometric one: the manufacturer\ndoes not observe the shopkeeper’s wallet, only its own share of it. A decline in observed purchases is equally\nconsistent with a contraction of liquidity and with a loss of share to a competitor, and the two call for\nopposite responses: the first for patience, the second for a price reaction.\nThe system therefore decomposes the construct into three objects with three different entry points. A\ncash-pressure index, built exclusively from sources exogenous to the firm’s own sales (the semimonthly\npayroll cycle taken to govern retail liquidity in the market under study, month-end proximity, staple-basket\ninflation, and the real erosion of the minimum wage), is a legitimate pre-treatment control and enters W\nwith that status declared. Two components of that sentence should not be conflated. That expenditure\nresponds to the arrival of a paycheck is documented, though for a different population and institutional\nsetting [Stephens Jr, 2003]; that the relevant cycle here is semimonthly and that it governs shopkeeper\nliquidity in this channel is a property of the market under study, asserted on operational grounds and not\ntransferred from that evidence. The screening test described below is what keeps the assertion from being\nload-bearing: the index is admitted to W only if it clears it. Its composition is published together with a\nvariance decomposition, because the two terms of the index are not equally informative and the aggregate\nconceals that. The calendar term carries intra-month variation and is the component that identifies; the\nreal-income term is, absent a regional price index, a smooth national series that is nearly collinear with the\ninflation index already present in W and therefore identifies nothing on its own. On the generator the split is\n51.0% of the index’s variance from the calendar and 51.0% from real income, the two summing above unity\nbecause the cross term is negligible and each standardized component contributes its own unit variance.\nThat split does not, on its own, support the asymmetry just asserted, and saying so is the point of publishing\nthe decomposition rather than the conclusion drawn from it. By variance the two terms are interchangeable.\nWhat separates them is the second measurement: the real-income term correlates with the inflation index\nalready in W at 0.884, so almost all of its variance is variance the confounder set already carries, while the\ncalendar term is orthogonal to it by construction. The correct statement is therefore about incremental\nrather than total variation: the two terms contribute equally to the index and unequally to its identifying\n\n54\n\n\fcontent, and an index reported as a single number would conceal both facts. The repair is a data one and\nmirrors the quota hierarchy of Appendix B.12: regional sources (a regional price index, a harvest calendar, a\nmeasure of informality) enter automatically where the panel supplies them. The construct is admitted to the\nfull frontier only after a screening test clears it, described below. Total store expenditure belongs in the\ndemand system of Appendix B.6, where budget shares are defined on it by construction and where it is the\nallocation variable rather than an illegitimate control; it must be instrumented, since the error of any single\nequation enters the total [Blundell and Robin, 1999], and it yields an expenditure elasticity that the system\ndoes not currently produce and that answers a question the business asks constantly: which references get\nrestocked first when cash arrives. The binding state of the constraint (whether liquidity binds, rather\nthan its level) is an effect modifier on both the elasticity and the pass-through, not a control.\nFinally, order refusal is not elasticity. The behavioural premise of this whole subsection is that a liquidityconstrained shopkeeper narrows the breadth of the portfolio before reducing depth, and it is stated as a\nhypothesis rather than as an established regularity: it is not documented in the literature this paper draws\non, and the screening test below exists precisely to refuse investment in the layer on a panel where it does\nnot hold. The econometric citations that follow concern the treatment of such an event, not the existence of\nthe ordering. If the premise holds, the event is an extensive-margin one with an empirical signature distinct\nfrom a smooth volume decline, and it would require a two-part treatment [Cragg, 1971]: the probability that\nthe order is accepted at the offered price, and the volume conditional on acceptance. If the constraint causes\nnon-purchase, the observed volume sample is selected on the state of the constraint [Heckman, 1979], and no\nadjustment of the observable confounders repairs it.\nThe status of this frontier in the present system should be stated exactly, because it is an instance of the\npaper’s governing principle rather than a gap. One component is implemented: a screening test that regresses\nportfolio breadth (the count of presentations with a positive order per region×week) on the cash-pressure\nindex, controlling for own log price, and requires |t| ≥ 2 before any further construct is built. It is deliberately\ncheap, and its purpose is to refuse investment in the full frontier on a panel that shows no cash-constraint\nsignal at all. On this generator the test returns no statistic: it executes without error in all thirteen replications\nand the t is non-finite in every one. That outcome is reported as it is and not rounded into a verdict, because\nthe two are different. A finite |t| < 2 would be a screening failure and would say that the panel carries no\ncash-constraint signal; a non-finite t says the regressor carries no variation against which a signal could be\nmeasured.\nThe cause of the non-finite statistic is now established rather than conjectured, and it lies on the left-hand\nside rather than the right. Portfolio breadth is a count of presentations with a positive order, and on a\ngenerator where every presentation is ordered every week that count is constant: its standard deviation is\nexactly zero, there is nothing to regress, and a non-finite t is the correct output of a well-behaved routine\nrather than the symptom of a defect. The diagnosis is confirmed by construction. Injecting order refusal gives\nthe dependent variable variation and the screen then operates: with a weak refusal mechanism the breadth\ncarries a standard deviation of 1.755 and the screen passes in every one of 5 replications. That |t| runs into\nthe tens is not evidence of power and is not presented as such, since breadth is generated as a direct function\nof the index in those scenarios and the test recovers a relation that was imposed. The informative cell is the\none with regional income variation and no refusal, where breadth is again constant across weeks, the statistic\nis finite at t = −0.5, and the screen correctly declines to admit the index. The screen is therefore operative\nand correctly calibrated, and what the panel lacked was the phenomenon rather than the instrument. The\nfrontier remains neither cleared nor refused on this generator, and it is now clear which of the two the\nmissing ingredient is: an order-refusal signature in the data, not a better regressor. Three components\nare specified and not estimated: the two-part selection model, the instrumented total expenditure, and the\nbinding-state effect modifier. Each is documented in Appendix A.10 as a proposed design with its grain\ndependence declared, and each is withheld for the same reason: a region×week panel cannot support them,\nand estimating them badly at this grain would smooth a lumpy discrete process into a mild and false elasticity.\nThis subsection would not be honest if it presented four estimated objects where the system runs one test;\npresented correctly, this frontier is a worked example of not estimating what the grain does not support. Two\ndependencies are declared rather than worked around. Store-level grain is a prerequisite: the constraint is a\ndiscrete, lumpy phenomenon at the store, and averaging it across a region smooths it into a mild and false\n\n55\n\n\felasticity, the same aggregation argument that governs the (S, s) layer of Appendix B.7 [Caballero and Engel,\n2007]. And the panel’s imputation policy is a live risk to this measurement: forward-filling gaps can convert\norder refusals into apparent continuity, so a concentration of the imputation flag at month-end and among\nsmall stores is evidence of the phenomenon rather than of a capture defect.\n\nB.9\n\nOptimization: contribution as the objective, the Score as the language\n\nThe optimization layer is designed on two levels with deliberately separated roles. The optimizer maximizes\ncontribution in value, (P − C) · Q̂(P ), with minimum margin, volume, market share, and price ladders as\nconstraints, and with the competitive corridor (competitor price ±10%, the strategic RGM cap) as a hard\nconstraint. The Score(P), a weighted, normalized combination of margin and volume, is the communication\nlayer: it ranks scenarios for the committee with readable verdicts (“sweet spot,” “valid,” “marginal”), but\nit never defines the optimal price, because maximizing a normalized score is not equivalent to maximizing\ncontribution and would make the optimum dependent on the normalization window.\nThe architecture of the decision curve is deliberately simpler than a reader of this literature might expect,\nand the simplicity is the point. One hard constraint on the price derives from the competitive environment:\nthe corridor [P comp (1 − κ), P comp (1 + κ)] with κ = 0.10. It is not the only bound the optimizer respects,\nand stating otherwise would misdescribe the feasible set: the operating limit of ±20% on the list price, the\nminimum margin, the volume and share bounds, the price ladders, and the absolute range [Pmin , Pmax ] of\nAppendix A.11 all bind as well. The precedence between them is explicit, because the ±20% limit can bite\nbefore the corridor does: the feasible set is the intersection of the operating range around the base price with\nthe competitive corridor, and where the two conflict the tighter of the two governs and the artifact records\nwhich one was binding. The corridor is distinctive only in what it encodes, a strategic RGM position relative\nto the competitor rather than an internal accounting floor. The conformal band of Section 4.2 does not\nbound it. That band is a predictive interval on the volume response, and it travels with the recommendation\nand with each row of the lookup table as the uncertainty attached to the consequence of moving; using it\nto trim the corridor would silently mix an interval on a response with a bound on a decision variable, two\nobjects with different units and different meanings.\nThe separation is what makes each of them interpretable: the corridor answers where the price is allowed to\ngo, the band answers how confident the system is about what follows. A third candidate cut, a kink at the\ntranche where the elasticity accelerates, is not implemented: the demand model carries no threshold terms,\nand introducing them is recorded as future work alongside the multi-item optimizer rather than described as\nthough it were running.\nThe choice of optimization method responds to the structure of the problem and not the other way around,\nand the argument is worth stating correctly because the intuitive version of it is wrong. For the decision on a\nsingle presentation the feasible space is one-dimensional, bounded by the corridor, and discrete in practice,\nsince prices are set in whole units of the smallest circulating denomination. That last property is an operating\nconvention of the channel declared here rather than a documented fact, and the argument does not lean on\nthe particular denomination: it requires only that the admissible prices form a finite set small enough to\nenumerate, which any discrete pricing convention delivers. Exact enumeration of that grid with an arg max of\ncontribution therefore delivers the global optimum deterministically and without a seed, and the grid is the\nfeasible set rather than a discretization of it. A metaheuristic would add stochastic dependence to a problem\nthat has none to spare and would buy nothing in return. What it would not do is rescue the optimizer from a\ndiscontinuous landscape, because the objective is not discontinuous: under Q = Q0 (P/P 0 )ε the contribution\nis smooth on each side of the base price, with a single kink at P 0 where the Dual Beta switches slope (a\nkink that disappears with the withdrawal of that estimator in Appendix C.6, leaving the objective smooth\nthroughout, which strengthens rather than weakens the argument for enumeration). The real non-convexity\nappears only when optimizing the joint portfolio vector, where cross-cannibalization couples all decisions;\nthat high-dimensional multimodal landscape is the intended domain of global differential evolution [Storn\nand Price, 1997] followed by L-BFGS-B polishing [Byrd et al., 1995], which is specified and reserved for the\nmulti-item scale-up phase rather than implemented here. The demand curve the optimizer consumes is driven\nby the decision-grade elasticity of Appendix B.5: θdec sets the level, whereas the permission to move at all is\ngranted by the decision layer voting on the raw estimate. In the configuration this paper validates the curve is\n56\n\n\fsymmetric, since the asymmetric estimator is withdrawn (Appendix C.6); the scaling of a directional pair by\nk = θdec /θ̂ is retained in Appendix A.11 as the form the layer takes on a panel that supports asymmetry. The\nShopkeeper Elasticity (Section 4.3) acts as a traffic light after optimization, not as a term in the objective.\nA final translation step closes the gap between what the optimizer computes and what a committee can\nadjudicate. The break-even is already implicit in the objective (contribution is maximized against observed\ncost), but implicit is not auditable. Every recommendation therefore travels with three additional quantities:\nthe tolerable volume loss the move can absorb before it stops paying, the critical elasticity at which\nthe contribution of moving equals that of holding, and a flag stating whether the entire uncertainty interval\nfalls on one side of that threshold. The status of the first two is worth stating precisely, and in the system’s\nfavour. The tolerable volume loss is algebraically exact and free of any functional-form assumption: it follows\nfrom the definition of contribution alone, which is what allows a committee to verify it mentally and is the\nreason it is published. The critical elasticity is exact conditional on the log-log specification the optimizer\nitself uses, so it inherits that assumption and no other. Neither is an approximation, and presenting them as\nsuch would understate the one part of the output a non-technical reader can fully audit. The motivation is\nempirical as much as rhetorical: the canonical meta-analysis of price elasticities reports a mean of −1.76 with\na dispersion as wide as the mean itself across 367 estimates [Tellis, 1988], and a later synthesis over 1,851\nestimates does not agree with it in the first digit [Bijmolt et al., 2005]. Against that background, publishing\nan elasticity to two decimals promises a precision the method cannot deliver; reporting which side of the\nbreak-even the whole range falls on delivers exactly what the decision requires. When the interval straddles\nthe threshold, the honest output is not a smaller decimal but a smaller move, a store-level test, or abstention.\n\nB.10\n\nThe decision layer: ACT, or which kind of WAIT\n\nAll the machinery above estimates the causal slope as well as possible with the data at hand. The decision\nlayer adds no further analysis: it converts what has been estimated into a single verdict per presentation,\ndrawn from the four states of Definition 1, and makes the optimizer act only where identification holds. The\nthree WAIT states are not decoration: they separate a block an experiment can lift from one it cannot and\nfrom one that time alone lifts. It is, to the best of our knowledge, the least common component in enterprise\npricing systems, and the one on which organizational trust turns: reluctance to use a model intensifies once it\nhas been seen to err [Dietvorst et al., 2015], and it is recovered by giving the user visible control over an\nimperfect system rather than by improving the model [Dietvorst et al., 2018]. An engine that knows how to\nabstain is an engine one can delegate to.\n\nB.11\n\nThe eight guards and the ten abstention causes\n\nThe ACT decision requires passing every operative guard simultaneously, each tied to a real failure mode and\nnot to a report metric. The qualifier is load-bearing rather than defensive: Guard 8 is declared non-operative\nwhen the panel carries neither the true incentive fields nor sub-monthly grain, and a guard that cannot be\nevaluated is reported as such instead of being counted as passed. Each verdict therefore travels with the\noperative status of all eight, so that a presentation reaching ACT on seven evaluable guards is distinguishable\nfrom one reaching ACT on eight. The first five address the quality and the currency of the estimate; the\nlast three, added after an external audit of the system, address the ways an estimate can be well measured\nand still be measuring something other than what it claims. Table 5 summarizes them in compact form (the\nquestion each answers, its mechanism, whether it vetoes, and the verdict type it maps to); the paragraphs\nbelow develop them in turn. The table has ten rows and not nine because Guard 1 evaluates two conditions\non separate thresholds, a regime shift and a coverage share, and Definition 1 classifies causes rather than\nguards.\nThe eight are preceded by the admissibility gate of Appendix B.3, and the order is not incidental: a\npresentation that fails admissibility reaches WAIT without a single guard being evaluated, so the guards\nare properly understood as the second of two filters rather than the whole of the decision layer. Section 6.8\nreports how much of the portfolio each stage removes.\nThe first guard flags the broken weeks, the contaminated natural experiments, through four observable\nproxies whose operating definitions are published because the guard is only as credible as its criteria. A\n57\n\n\fstockout is a week whose volume falls below the presentation’s own fifth percentile without a price fall that\nwould explain it: volume responded to availability, not to price. Price-matching is a week in which the own\nprice moved by at least 0.5% in the same direction as a competitor jump of at least 2.5%; that variation\nidentifies a gap elasticity, not an own-price one. Intense promotion with social diffusion is flagged only above\nthe 75th percentile of positive promotional intensity, and the restriction is deliberate rather than conservative:\naverage promotion is already controlled as a confounder, and flagging all of it would change the estimand\ninstead of protecting it. Clearance is a list fall of at least 4% accompanied by volume at or above one and a\nhalf times the median.\nThe elasticity is then re-estimated excluding those weeks, subject to a floor of forty usable observations, and\nthe guard vetoes when the two estimates differ by more than 0.30 or when more than 45% of weeks were\nflagged. One limitation of that contrast is declared rather than left implicit: when the clean re-estimation\nis left with few observations, the guard compares two estimates that are both noisy, and a large gap may\nreflect the loss of precision rather than dependence on the excluded weeks. The artifact therefore publishes\nthe interval width of the clean estimate alongside the gap, so that a reader can see whether the contrast\ndiscriminates.\nThe second guard is the Cinelli–Hazlett robustness value [Cinelli and Hazlett, 2020]: a single auditable\nnumber answering how strong an unmeasured confounder (one moving price and volume at the same time)\nwould have to be to drive the elasticity to zero. A low value means the sign of the effect is fragile with respect\nto what is not observed, and no price is moved on that basis. The operating threshold is 0.12.\nTwo refinements make this guard say what it is supposed to say. The first is the unit of inference, and\nit is a correction rather than an extension. The robustness value depends on the degrees of freedom, and\nfeeding it a count of panel rows inflates those degrees of freedom by approximately the number of regions,\nsince six regions observed in one week are not six independent observations. The consequence is systematic:\nan inflated count shrinks the statistic that enters the calculation and displaces the robustness value, so a\nfixed threshold is miscalibrated against the very unit of inference the rest of the system treats as honest. The\nguard therefore consumes the effective number of independent periods, consistent with the principle stated in\nAppendix B.14, and the threshold is recalibrated against the generator. Section 6.8 reports the veto rate\nbefore and after the correction, since a change in the veto rate of a guard is exactly the kind of consequence\na methodological paper exists to document.\nThe second refinement is presentational and matters for adoption. A bare robustness value is close to\nunreadable for a committee, and the device that makes it interpretable is the benchmark against an observed\ncovariate that Cinelli and Hazlett propose. Since every column of W is already declared, promotional intensity\nis the natural reference, and the guard reports the robustness value alongside a sentence of the form: an\nunmeasured confounder would have to be some multiple of the strength of promotional intensity to nullify\nthe estimate. That formulation is adjudicable in a meeting; a bare value is not.\nThe third guard evaluates the stability of the recommendation across the width of the uncertainty:\nthe optimizer is run at the endpoints of the elasticity’s confidence interval, and if the recommended price\nswings between them by more than a tolerance (half the corridor width), the decision is not robust to the\nestimate’s own uncertainty.\nAs implemented, that test interrogates the raw estimate while the price actually emitted is computed at the\nregularized level, which means the guard verifies the stability of a price different from the one published.\nThe division of roles behind this is sound and is retained (the gate votes with the raw evidence, the pooled\nestimate sets the level), but the gap it leaves is closed by evaluating stability twice and publishing both.\nThe first evaluation, on the raw interval,\np answers whether the evidence supports moving at all. The second,\nrunning the optimizer at θdec ± 1.96 s2dec over the posterior, answers whether the price about to be emitted\nis stable within its own uncertainty. These are different questions, and a system that publishes a regularized\nprice should answer the second. A related exposure is bounded at the same point: the rescaling factor\nk = θdec /θ̂ that carries a directional pair to the published level diverges as the raw estimate approaches\nzero, so k is capped and the cap is declared in Appendix A.6. With the asymmetric estimator withdrawn\n(Appendix C.6) that exposure is not live in the validated configuration, and the cap is retained for the case in\nwhich it returns.\n58\n\n\fThe fourth guard is the instrumental contrast described in Section 4.1: if the cost instrument is strong\nand the instrumental estimate diverges materially from the observational one, there is residual endogeneity\nthat the observable confounders did not clean. If the instrument is weak, the contrast informs but does not\nveto: a decision machine does not paralyze itself because an instrument lacks power; it records the state and\ndecides with the remaining guards.\nThe fifth guard monitors not the quality of the estimate but its validity over time: two structural-break\ntests on the residual of the causal model: an OLS-CUSUM [Ploberger and Krämer, 1992] that detects\nbreaks without knowing the date [Andrews, 1993], and Chow tests [Chow, 1960] with Bonferroni correction\nat candidate dates known a priori (own list changes and competitor jumps above 2.5%), both on weekly\naggregates and with a Bartlett long-run variance estimator [Newey and West, 1987], which gives three layers\nof protection against false positives. The full operating parameters are given in Appendix A.14; two of them\ncarry an argument that belongs in the main text. The aggregation to weekly totals before testing is not a\nconvenience: the Brownian-bridge limit of the CUSUM and the F distribution of the Chow statistic both\nassume approximately independent observations, and running them on a panel with six regions per week\ninflates the sample size and makes both tests liberal. And “recent” is defined as twelve weeks, which is the\nwindow that determines whether a detected break vetoes or is merely recorded.\nThree layers of protection against false positives is a statement about the size of these tests and says nothing\nabout their power, which is the property that matters when the guard’s job is to expire an estimate. A\ntest that never fires spuriously and never detects a real break would satisfy the description and be useless.\nAppendix C.2 therefore reports the detection rate against the magnitude of an injected break. That section\nalso verifies the size claim itself rather than assuming it: the three devices control multiplicity and dependence,\nbut none of them protects against a non-stationary residual, and the realized false-positive rate on residuals\nof a boosted nuisance model does not match the nominal one: with no break injected the pair rejects at 0.94,\nand neither device is innocent, the CUSUM alone firing at 0.71 and the Bonferroni-corrected Chow family at\n0.84 (Appendix C.2). The sentence above should therefore be read strictly as a description of what the three\ndevices are designed to do, and as a measured refutation of the claim that they achieve it here. Calibrating\nthe critical value by simulation rather than taking it from the asymptotic distribution restores the size on\nevery residual path tested and leaves the guard with no power at the largest break the generator can inject,\nso the outcome of that section is that Guard 5 is not operative and both its veto and its reset are withdrawn\nfrom the decision layer. The description that follows is therefore of the design as specified, and the measured\nstatus of each element is given in Appendix C.2. As specified, a recent break vetoes action: the elasticity was\nestimated mostly in the previous regime and has expired. The same break also expires the sequential prior of\nthe pooling layer: the state is marked as reset, so the next cycle restarts from the hierarchical branch instead\nof a posterior learned in the dead regime. An old break does not veto, because the re-estimation already lives\nin the new regime, but it is recorded.\nThe sixth guard tests over-conditioning on mediators. Following the declared temporal status of every\ncontrol in Section 4.1, the elasticity is re-estimated with the post-treatment commercial controls removed,\nand the two estimates are compared. A material gap means the production estimate is partly conditioned on\nactivity that itself responds to price. The competitor’s price is deliberately excluded from this pruning, since\nits ambiguity is already resolved by the competitive bracket and merging the two would confound distinct\nquestions.\nThe seventh guard tests the exclusion restriction of the cost instrument by the residual-correlation\nprocedure of Section 4.1. Like the fourth guard, it vetoes only where the instrument is strong: with a weak\ninstrument it records the state and defers to the remaining guards. Its practical effect is to prevent the\ninstrumental contrast from being read as an endorsement when both estimators are displaced by the same\ncommon shock.\nThe eighth guard addresses cross-system interference and is developed in Appendix B.12: the elasticity is\nre-estimated with controls for the commercial incentive cycle, and a material change indicates that part of\nwhat was being read as price sensitivity was salesforce effort.\nIts inputs follow a declared hierarchy of sources, and the hierarchy is part of the guard rather than an\nimplementation note. Where the panel carries the true incentive fields (quota, attainment against target,\n59\n\n\feffective discount), they enter directly and the guard runs on them. Where it does not, the guard falls back\nto a calendar proxy built from the day of the month, which requires sub-monthly grain to have any variation\nat all. And where neither is available, the guard is declared non-operative rather than run on a constant\nand reported as passing, since a contrast between two identical specifications returns zero and would be\nindistinguishable from a clean verdict. This is the one component of the system whose identification depends\non the temporal resolution of the panel, which Appendix B.2 treats as a result worth reporting rather than a\nlimitation to be apologized for.\nTwo mechanisms complement the guards. First, the elasticity is treated as a snapshot with an expiration\ndate, not a structural truth: it is re-estimated on expanding windows at quarterly cuts, a drift alert fires\nwhen the estimate of one cut falls outside the interval of the previous cut, and an estimate with active drift or\nnot revalidated in three cycles does not feed decisions until re-estimated. The same discounted sequential filter\nof Appendix B.5, run over this history, quantifies for the committee that the decision-grade elasticity does\nnot bounce between cycles with pure estimation noise while the raw estimate does, without changing a single\nACT/WAIT verdict. Second, every ACT-class recommendation is re-evaluated under three competitive\nscenarios built on the bracket of Section 4.1: the competitor ignores the move, reacts with their historical\nslope, or matches it fully. The verdict distinguishes moves that are robust to the game (the contribution gain\nsurvives all three scenarios, typical of increases: if the competitor matches, the whole category moves up),\nconditioned moves (they pay only if the competitor does not match, typical of cuts that degenerate into a\nprice war), and cases where holding dominates. This is not structural game theory: the reaction is taken as\nconstant and one round, and it is declared as such: it is the deliberate refusal to optimize with false precision.\nOne risk remains that no econometrics resolves: performativity [MacKenzie, 2006], the pricing instance of\nthe general point that a relation estimated under one policy regime need not survive the adoption of a new\none [Lucas, 1976]. Once the engine starts moving prices, future variation will be the child of its own estimates\nand observational identification will degrade on its own. The defense is not statistical but experimental,\nand this system instruments it rather than merely recommending it: Appendix B.19 describes the holdout\nprotocol, the synthetic control contrast, and the contamination test that operationalize it. It remains the\nsingle investment worth more than any additional sophistication of the estimator.\n\nB.12\n\nCross-system interference and the endogeneity of commercial effort\n\nNo decision model in an enterprise operates alone, and treating adjacent systems as inert background is\nan unstated identifying assumption. In this setting, a primary structural neighbor is a commercial orderrecommendation system that functions as a quota allocator rather than a demand forecast, predicting what\nthe enterprise aims to place in the channel rather than what final demand will absorb. When downstream\nmodels mistake these push-driven placement targets for true consumer demand, historical sales become\nartificially capped by past allocation policies. Conflating supply allocation with unconstrained point-of-sale\ndemand induces structural endogeneity. As a result, optimization models inadvertently capture past enterprise\npolicies rather than true underlying market responsiveness.\nThe consequence for causal identification runs through three channels, none of which was instrumented\nbefore. First, quota pressure is an unobserved confounder with a temporal signature. When a\nsalesperson runs behind target, discounts, free-goods allowances, and portfolio pushes appear; the effective\nprice falls and volume rises. A model without a quota variable records that co-movement as elasticity when it\nis quota compliance, and because the incentive cycle is monthly the contamination concentrates in the closing\nweeks, precisely where a weekly panel carries the most price movement [Oyer, 1998, Larkin, 2014]. Second,\nforward buying reverses. A month-end push leaves the store holding inventory above its rotation, and the\nfollowing week it purchases less for reasons of stock rather than price [Hendel and Nevo, 2006], producing an\noverstated sell-in elasticity in the push week and a negative one after it, a pattern a model without the cycle\nvariable reads as noise or as a structural break. This constitutes an additional and independent argument\nfor the system’s choice of sell-out rather than sell-in as the outcome: the consumer does not stockpile to\nmeet a salesperson’s target. Third, and most consequentially for this system, over-inventoried stores are\nconjectured to discount on the shelf to rotate, so the shelf price would fall for inventory reasons and\nthe pass-through layer would record the resulting volume response as consumer behavior. The asymmetry\nwith the two channels above is deliberate and is declared: those rest on documented evidence [Oyer, 1998,\n60\n\n\fLarkin, 2014, Hendel and Nevo, 2006], whereas this one is an operational conjecture from the channel with\nno source behind it, and it is the channel on which the most consequential of the three claims rests. The\nbroken-weeks guard already flags the terminal symptom (clearance), but only after the fact and without the\ncause; with the quota variable the cause becomes observable in advance.\nThe loop closes uncomfortably: the push responds to the target, the target is set by looking at history, and\nthe pricing engine learns from that same series. Part of what the system measures is the consequence of its\nown company’s commercial policy. The correction is to admit the incentive cycle into the confounder set and\nto test its influence explicitly, which is the eighth guard.\nThere is also a direct operational conflict, and it is not resolved by econometrics. The two systems optimize\nobjectives that cannot be maximized jointly, and adherence to the suggested order will fall in response to\nthe very price increases this engine recommends. Arbitrating each collision bilaterally lets the outcome be\ndecided by the relative standing of the areas involved and leaves nothing learned for the next one, and there\nwill be a next one, since production planning, territory targets, and trade investment optimize objectives that\nare equally legitimate and equally unaware of each other. The mechanism adopted instead is a reporting\nconvention: every alternative that reaches the committee declares its cross-system cost: the\nexpected volume loss, and hence the expected adherence loss, implied by the proposed move, on a three-level\nscale. The committee stops choosing between two systems and chooses an option that already carries the\ncross-cost inside it, and the same format extends to the next collision. A secondary benefit is at least as\nvaluable: the convention forces each model to state in writing what it predicts. Much of the friction did not\narise from the objectives differing (that is normal and healthy) but from both figures being presented as\nthough they were the same kind of object.\n\nB.13\n\nSystem architecture and artifacts\n\nThe layers above are a specification; this subsection states how the specification is enforced in code and what\nthe cycle leaves behind. Three elements complete the methodology: the architectural principles that prevent\ntwo layers from diverging on the same operation, the single quality checkpoint that halts the system rather\nthan degrading it, and the versioned artifacts that make a cycle auditable ex post. The validation protocol\nthat measures whether all of this works is deliberately separated into Section 5.\n\nB.14\n\nArchitecture principles\n\nThe system is implemented as a modular, auditable computational architecture, with the more opaque\nproduction components for gradient boosting, automated causal estimation, and Bayesian updating replaceable\nby transparent implementations that are contrasted against the production estimator where available. Five\nprinciples organize the design, and each is enforced in code rather than recommended in documentation.\nFirst, a single residualization kernel: the same cross-fitting function feeds the causal estimator, the\ncross-elasticity system, and the pass-through deconvolution, so that no layer can diverge in folds, estimators,\nor resampling policy. Second, a single temporal resampling policy across the entire system (contiguous\nweekly blocks, moving-block bootstrap, chronological splits), because a single component that shuffles rows\ncontaminates with optimism everything that consumes it downstream. Third, labeled placeholders, never\ndisguised ones: where a quantity is provisional (for example, the long-run elasticity derived by a factor from\nthe short-run one), the artifact carries it with an explicit label of its computational basis, visible all the way\nto the committee dashboard. Fourth, no undeclared control: every column admitted to the confounder set\ncarries its temporal status relative to the pricing decision, and the estimation refuses to run on an undeclared\none; this is the same discipline of declaring estimands in Section 4.1, applied at the level of the individual\nvariable.\nFifth, stated as its own principle because its violation was found in a place where the rest of the system had\nalready got it right, one effective unit of inference: the number of observations that enters any statistic\nwhose distribution depends on a sample size is the number of independent periods, never the number of panel\nrows. Six regions observed in the same week are one draw from the weekly shock, and treating them as\nsix inflates degrees of freedom, deflates standard errors, and makes tests liberal. The principle was already\nrespected by the bootstrap, which clusters on the week, and by the break tests, which aggregate to the week\n61\n\n\fbefore testing. However, it was violated by both the robustness value and the first-stage F statistic of the\ninstrumental contrast, each of which consumed a raw row count and thereby rendered the weak-instrument\nscreens (Guards 4 and 7) effectively non-binding (Appendix B.11). That both instances survived a build in\nwhich every other component got the unit right is the strongest evidence for stating the requirement as a\nprinciple rather than an ad-hoc, per-component correction. It is the concrete form that the paper’s general\nthesis takes inside its own implementation: the failure that survived a careful build was a tacit assumption\nabout the unit of analysis, not an error of estimation.\n\nB.15\n\nThe QA Gate: the system halts, it does not degrade\n\nA single checkpoint operationalizes the first layer of the validation protocol before the decision layer and\nthe artifacts consume the model outputs. The system halts with an explicit exception (it does not degrade\nsilently) if the out-of-fold fit of the demand model falls below 0.65 on the modelable set, or if mean conformal\ncoverage falls below the nominal level minus a tolerance of 0.03, which at α = 0.10 places the floor at 0.87.\nThe fit is computed out of fold on temporal blocks, since evaluating it on shuffled folds would exploit the\nserial leakage the gate exists to police. The nuance matters: the gate is evaluated on the modelable set; that\npresentations diagnosed as non-modelable fail thresholds is the desired behavior of the routing, not a failure.\nThe same control protects, as a decorator, the artifact-construction stage: no curves are published from a\nmodel that did not pass health.\nOne deliberate correction to that design is proposed here and stated as such, because the gate as built\nanswers one question and is asked to guarantee two. The out-of-fold fit of a log-volume model is dominated\nby seasonality and level, not by price: a demand model can clear 0.65 comfortably while carrying almost\nno identifying price variation, so a fit threshold protects the conformal band (which predicts volume) but\ndoes not protect the elasticity (which requires the treatment to be identified). The quantity that protects\nthe second is already computed and already used, in the admissibility gate of Appendix B.3: the partial R2\nof the treatment after residualization, with its cutoff at 0.10. The gate is therefore best presented as two\nthresholds answering two questions: predictive health for the band, and effective identification for the\nslope. The system currently reports the first and applies the second without naming it, and naming it costs\nnothing and closes a real gap in the argument.\n\nB.16\n\nVersioned artifacts\n\nThe deliverable is not a table: it is an auditable repository per cycle. The lookup table discretizes the\n±20% operating range into 1% steps (41 rows) with three non-negotiable rules: the percentage is applied once\non the base price (in log-log, tranches compose, they do not add, and a tranche-by-tranche chaining would\nbe misleading), each row stores the volume effect against the base as the single decision column, and each\nrow declares whether it falls within the observed support, computed per region and taking the intersection,\nbecause pooled support across regions with different levels declares “inside” tranches that no region ever\ntraversed. When an asymmetric estimator is in use the table exposes the effect band per direction; in the\nconfiguration validated here it is not, so each row carries a single band (Appendix C.6). It is accompanied by\nthe elasticity curves by tranche, whose level is driven by the decision-grade elasticity of Appendix B.5\nand whose confidence score is continuous in the weight of the cycle’s own data, the cross-elasticity matrix\nin long format, and the dashboard feed under schema validation with rollback; the data contract is sacred:\na change of key or unit silently corrupts the downstream reporting. Everything is persisted versioned by cycle\nand presentation, with quality flags, confidence intervals, and conformal intervals. To these are added the\nversioned elasticity history per cycle and the versioned sequential state of the pooling layer, which together\nmaterialize the validity governance of Appendix B.11, and a versioned report payload persisted centrally\nthat composes the already-computed outputs (it re-estimates nothing) into two summary views, one for data\nscience and one for the pricing committee.\n\n62\n\n\fB.17\n\nValidation design in full\n\nB.18\n\nFour-layer validation protocol\n\nValidation follows four layers of robustness. The statistical layer requires significance, a negative sign on the\nown elasticity, dominance of the own elasticity over the cross ones, and the QA Gate fit threshold. The sign\nrequirement carries a selection cost that the system measures rather than assumes away: retaining estimates\nconditional on their own realization truncates the published distribution, and that truncation composes with\nthe admissibility gate and with the shrinkage, so the elasticities a committee sees are filtered at three points.\nAppendix C.5 quantifies the displacement against known truth and states the condition under which the\nfilter should be replaced by an a priori criterion. The commercial sanity layer confronts the results\nwith category managers: the estimated substitution hierarchy must match the consumer decision tree the\nbusiness knows. The back-testing layer introduces a deliberate methodological correction: it decouples\nprediction (base volume, evaluated via conformalized quantile regression with out-of-time coverage and a\ntarget percentage error below 15%) from inference (the stability of the elasticity between the training window\nand the full panel), because a single direct predictive model conflates both questions and answers both poorly.\nThe stress layer verifies the system’s behavior at the extremes: competitor tending to infinity (the corridor\nshifts but the optimal price stays bounded by margin and volume), a 20% cost shock, and a price war with\nno destructive recommendations.\n\nB.19\n\nThe experimental layer: holdouts, synthetic controls, and contamination\n\nThe four layers above interrogate the estimate; none of them can establish that an executed price change\ncaused the outcome that followed it. That requires a comparison group, which the system implements directly\nrather than merely prescribing. Three design decisions define the protocol. First, the retained regions are\ndrawn by a deterministic function of the cycle identifier, not resampled at each run: the same cycle\nalways yields the same holdout, and the assignment remains auditable months later, a property that matters\nmore for governance than for statistics. Second, the counterfactual is not a raw group average but a synthetic\ncontrol with unit weights: the retained regions are reweighted, with non-negative weights summing to one,\nto reproduce the pre-treatment trajectory of the treated group, following Abadie et al. [2010], and the quality\nof that pre-treatment fit is reported alongside the effect, without which the estimate is not interpretable. The\nname is chosen precisely: the estimator carries unit weights only, without the time weights and the intercept\nterm that define the synthetic difference-in-differences of Arkhangelsky et al. [2021], which is specified as the\nnatural upgrade of this layer rather than claimed as its current content.\nInference for that estimator is supplied by permutation, which the design makes natural. With six regions\nthe sampling distribution of the effect is not available analytically, but the assignment mechanism is a\ndeterministic hash of the cycle identifier, so re-drawing it under alternative seeds enumerates the assignments\nthat could have occurred and yields the distribution of the placebo effect under the null of no intervention. The\nprotocol reports the observed effect against that distribution as a permutation p-value, which is what converts\na placebo departure from zero into evidence of an unbalanced assignment rather than an impression of one.\nThird, the protocol tests its own key assumption. Synthetic controls presume the donor pool is unaffected\nby the intervention (the no-interference half of SUTVA; Rubin, 1980), an assumption a competitor who\nreads regional price movements will violate by construction; the system therefore compares the competitor’s\nprice movement across treated and retained regions after the intervention. The threshold for declaring\ncontamination is not chosen from outside: it is the placebo of the panel itself, the same computation\nperformed on false cuts inside the pre-period, which supplies the natural noise scale of these data, augmented\nwith a minimum economic magnitude of 0.005 in log price, without which the test fires on differences of\nthousandths.\nThe construction of that noise scale is upgraded here from a single comparison to a test. Contrasting the\nobserved discrepancy against one false cut and a multiplicative factor makes the verdict depend on where\nthe single cut happened to fall; sweeping the pre-period yields a distribution of placebo discrepancies and\ntherefore a permutation p-value, with the economic floor retained as a declared minimum magnitude below\nwhich no discrepancy is acted on regardless of significance. The two conditions remain complementary for\n\n63\n\n\fthe reason they were both introduced: the placebo supplies a noise scale but no notion of materiality, and a\nfixed threshold supplies materiality but is arbitrary across panels.\nTwo properties of this layer deserve emphasis because they are easy to lose. Absent a real intervention the\neffect this block returns is a placebo and should be near zero; a departure from zero indicates that the holdout\nassignment is not balanced for the panel at hand, which is a design defect worth discovering before a pilot\nrather than during one. At six regions, over 300 replications, the placebo effect averages 0.081 in log volume\n(Appendix C.9), and the assignment is exhaustively enumerated rather than drawn at a single seed, so this\nis a rate rather than one realization. The assignment is balanced on this panel. One quantity published\nalongside it bounds what that reassurance is worth: the pre-treatment fit of the synthetic control has a root\nmean squared error of 0.189 in log volume, roughly 2.34 times the placebo effect it is being used to certify. A\ncomparison group that cannot reproduce the treated trajectory before the intervention cannot license an\ninference about what follows it, so the correct reading of this block is that the assignment shows no imbalance\nthe layer is powerful enough to see, rather than that it shows balance.\nThe resolution of that p-value is bounded by the panel and the bound is declared, since a reader is entitled to\nknow the smallest value the test can return before reading the value it did return. With |R| = 6 regions\n\u0001 and\na retention fraction f = 0.25, the holdout is ⌈1.5⌉ = 2 regions, so the assignment mechanism admits 62 = 15\ndistinct draws and the attainable floor on the permutation p-value is 1/15 = 0.067. No effect measured on\nthis panel can reach significance at the 5% level, whatever its magnitude, and the level actually applied is the\ngovernance parameter αperm of Table D.14 rather than a conventional 0.05. One consequence is recorded with\nit: at six regions the donor pool of four leaves the synthetic control with little to optimize (Appendix C.9\nreports 3.85 donors carrying non-zero weight on average, an effective count of 3.16), so the fitted weights\nsit close to a simple average of the retained regions and the “synthetic” qualifier is thinner here than the\nmethod’s name suggests, a property of a six-region panel rather than of the estimator.\nBecause that is a property of the panel width, it is testable by varying it, and the run does. Appendix C.9\nreports the layer at six, twelve, twenty and thirty regions, holding the number of treated regions fixed at\ntwo and always enumerating the assignment exhaustively, over 300 replications each: the attainable floor\nfalls from 0.067 to 0.0023, the pre-treatment fit of the synthetic control improves from a root mean squared\nerror of 0.189 to 0.133, the number of donors carrying non-zero weight rises from 3.85 to 12.09, and the\nfalse-positive rate at the nominal 5% level moves from an arithmetically guaranteed zero at six regions to\n0.060 at thirty, evidence that the layer’s inference is calibrated at all once the floor stops binding. The layer\ntherefore stops being an instrumented protocol awaiting a wider panel and becomes a sized prescription: the\nscale at which it licenses an inference is measured rather than asserted, which is what Section 6.5 needs from\nit. And the value of instrumenting the protocol in advance of the need is precisely that both of its calibration\ndecisions (the balance of the assignment and the economic floor of the contamination test) surface only on\nexecution, not on design.\n\nB.20\n\nReplications per experiment\n\nOne point of bookkeeping is settled before any figure is reported, because it governs how every number in\nSection 6 should be read: there is no single R for the paper. The replication count is set per experiment, from\n300 seeds for the panel-width and randomized-shock experiments down to 5 for the re-run liquidity-screen\nand pass-through-heterogeneity scenarios, and the counts are collected in Table D.1 at Section D rather\nthan folded into one number that would overstate the evidential weight of the thinner blocks. The table\ndistinguishes seeds from estimated units, since several experiments cross a seed with a grid of configurations,\nlearners or aggregation levels and their unit counts are correspondingly larger.\nTwo rows constrain a conclusion rather than merely describing a setting. The synthetic-control block runs at\n300 seeds per panel width, at six, twelve, twenty and thirty regions (Appendix C.9), so even its six-region\ncell – the width most exposed to the combinatorial floor – is a rate rather than a single realization. And\nthe conditional-coverage experiment runs at four designs, with forty noise draws taken within each, because\nits object is the dispersion across designs rather than within one; that structure, and not a raw replication\ncount, is what the experiment requires.\n\n64\n\n\fC\n\nExtended Validation Results\n\nTable 3 states the ablation block in one line per contrast. This appendix reports each of them in full, in\nthe order in which they are summarized there, together with the implementation and governance material\ncondensed in Section 8.3. Each contrast removes or replaces one design decision and measures what that\ncosts against the same generators; where a contrast returns a null, the null is the reported outcome rather\nthan a step toward a positive one.\n\nC.1\n\nSell-in against sell-out under injected forward buying\n\nThe generator inflates sell-in by 30% in the two weeks before a list increase while leaving sell-out untouched,\nwhich makes the cost of the wrong outcome variable directly measurable rather than merely arguable. Over\n50 replications, estimating θ on sell-in volume returns −1.025 against the injected list-price elasticity of\n−1.105 in the clean regime; estimating it on sell-out returns −0.937, and the gap between them is −0.088.\nThe gap is not merely small, it carries the sign opposite to the one the mechanism predicts: the contaminated\nseries is the closer of the two to the truth, and not by a small margin: its bias is 0.080 against 0.168 for the\nclean one, less than half as large.\nThis experiment is reported as a null result, and the distinction between the mechanism and its magnitude\nis the whole of what it establishes. The mechanism is not in doubt: a purchase pulled forward into the weeks\nbefore an increase inflates sell-in without any consumer having responded. What the generator shows is that\nthe injected contamination does not displace θ̂ materially on this panel, and the reason is structural: the\ninflation is confined to a two-week window before increases above 2%, those events are few in a hundred\nand twenty weeks, and the residualization against W absorbs a share of a contamination that is itself\na deterministic function of an observable price movement. The choice of sell-out over sell-in retains its\nargumentative support, through the reversal of forward buying after a month-end push (Appendix B.12), and\nloses its quantitative one here.\n\nC.2\n\nThe structural-break guard: size, critical values, and power\n\nA test’s size says nothing about its ability to expire an estimate, and the three layers of protection against\nfalse positives described in Appendix B.11 are statements about size alone. The run measures both, and it\ndoes so twice, because the first measurement diagnosed a configuration defect and the second asks whether\nrepairing it recovers a usable guard.\nThe size failure, and its cause. With the asymptotic critical value of 1.358 on residuals from the boosted\nnuisance learner, the union of the two tests rejects at 0.94 with no break injected, over 31 replications:\nthe CUSUM alone at 0.71 and the Bonferroni-corrected Chow family at 0.84. The condition was declared\nin advance, the guard does not pass it, and the planned power figure is withdrawn: a detection rate that\nbegins at 0.94 under the null cannot be crossed by a power criterion, and the aggregator’s value of zero for\nthe smallest detected break is the arithmetic of a threshold already exceeded at the origin rather than a\nmeasurement. The failure traces to a low-frequency component surviving residualization: the learner matrix\nof Appendix C.3 confirms the mechanism directly, with the false-positive rate of the CUSUM under no\nbreak at 0.810 on residuals from the boosted learner, 0.619 from a histogram-based one, 0.476 from a radial\nbasis kernel, and exactly 0.000 from the lasso, ridge and sieve learners, whose residuals pass an augmented\nDickey–Fuller test at t ≈ −4.81. The size failure is a property of the residual the guard is fed, not of the tests.\nRepairing the critical value does not repair the guard. Table D.7 reports the second measurement,\nover four residual paths crossing two learners with and without explicit detrending, at 14 replications per cell\nand 672 evaluations in total. Calibrating the critical value by simulation under the null, rather than taking it\nfrom the asymptotic distribution, moves the boosted learner’s size from 0.607 to 0.035 at a calibrated critical\nof 1.720, itself above the asymptotic 1.358; the other three paths already sit at zero size under the asymptotic\ncritical, and calibration leaves them between 0.000 and 0.107, at calibrated criticals of 0.733 to 1.123, all\nbelow the asymptotic value. The asymptotic critical is therefore mis-specified in both directions: too low for\nthe boosted learner’s residuals and too high for the other three.\n65\n\n\fThe power that survives the calibration is the finding. At the largest break injected, a ∆ε = 1.3 that moves θ\nby 1.105, the detection rate is 0.000 on the boosted path, 0.161 with detrending, 0.089 on the sieve path and\n0.160 on the detrended sieve path, against calibrated sizes of 0.035, 0.107, 0.035 and 0.000 respectively. Only\nthe detrended sieve path clears its own size by a margin large enough to call a discrimination, an excess of\n0.160 over a size of exactly zero; the other three paths return an excess of at most 0.054 and the boosted\npath returns a negative one. A single path distinguishing the largest injected break from its absence, out of\nfour, is not a guard the paper can rely on. The property the guard’s veto actually consumes fares no better:\nrestricted to week 112, the break date inside the twelve-week recency window that determines whether a\ndetected break vetoes, the best detection rate across all paths and both residual types is 0.286, against 0.214\nat week 80, outside the window. The window does not cost the guard power it had; it simply confirms that\nno configuration tested here reaches a detection rate a veto could be built on, inside the window or outside it.\nWhat is retired and what remains. The planned power figure is withdrawn and the figure that was to\ncarry it is not published. Guard 5 is reported as not operative rather than merely not validated: either the\nsize is uncontrolled, as with the asymptotic critical, or it is controlled by a test with no power, as with the\ncalibrated one. The two failures are different in kind and the second is the more serious, because a size failure\nis a calibration problem and an absence of power at the largest break the generator can inject is not. The\ncurrency of the elasticity therefore rests on the expanding-window drift alert and the revalidation cadence of\nAppendix B.11 alone, and the veto on recency, which consumes a detection property the guard does not have,\nis withdrawn with it. One consequence reaches the estimation layer: the policy of re-estimating only within\nthe regime that follows the most recent detected break, which Appendix C.4 identifies as the natural next\nrefinement, depends on locating a boundary that no configuration tested here can locate.\n\nC.3\n\nNuisance learners: a trade-off, not a dominance\n\nTwo panels test whether the production nuisance learner, a gradient-boosted tree, is dominated by a regularized\nalternative carrying seasonal harmonics and an explicit trend, and the two panels do not agree with each\nother, which is itself the finding.\nOn the five-regime generator, over 38 replications, the regularized learner dominates: bias 0.080 against\n−0.003, root mean squared error 0.173 against 0.091, and partial R2 of the treatment 0.446 against 0.652,\nwith the fit of the treatment nuisance itself nearly equal at 0.349 against 0.361. That near-equality is the\ninformative part: the two learners explain almost the same share of the treatment and differ almost entirely in\nwhich share, the boosted tree leaving a low-frequency component in the residual that the regularized learner\nremoves by construction.\nOn the extended generator, over 42 replications, the ordering does not carry over. Table D.6 reports six\nlearners on that panel. The paired contrast in absolute error between the best-performing rival, a radial\nbasis kernel, and the production learner is −0.0185 with a standard error of 0.0615 (t = −0.30, n = 42): the\nrival’s error is nominally smaller but the contrast is not distinguishable from noise. No rival dominates the\nproduction learner on this panel, and the production learner does not dominate either.\nThe table settles a question the five-regime panel could not raise on its own. No single learner wins on\nall three quantities that matter. The radial basis kernel has both the lowest bias, at −0.009, and the best\ncoverage, at 0.690; the boosted learner has the lowest root mean squared error, at 0.457. The choice is a\ntrade-off between two learners rather than a search for a dominant one, and the paper declares it as such.\nThe column that connects this table to the rest of the paper is the last one: the false-positive rate of the\nbreak test under each learner, which is exactly 0.000 for the three linear learners (lasso, ridge, sieve), 0.476\nfor the radial basis kernel, 0.619 for the histogram-based learner, and 0.810 for the boosted one, and which\nlocates the size failure of Appendix C.2 in the choice of learner rather than in the tests. A reader weighing\nbias, coverage and error against each other should weigh that column too: the learner with the lowest root\nmean squared error is the one that breaks the break test.\nThe paper’s organizing claim survives both panels and is sharpened by the pair. The claim is that the value\nof the system does not lie in the sophistication of its estimator. What the two contrasts show together is\nstronger: which estimator is better is not stable across panels that differ only in how pass-through is drawn,\n66\n\n\fso a system whose value rested on the estimator would have no defensible way to choose one. What is stable\nis the discipline of measuring, and it is the measurement, not the learner, that transfers.\n\nC.4\n\nWhat the cross-fitting scheme costs\n\nBlock cross-fitting predicts each block from other blocks, past and future, which relies on weak temporal\ndependence under stationarity. Injecting a break makes the cost of that tolerance measurable: under a\nbreak at week 80 that moves θ from −1.105 to −0.510, the scheme returns θ̂ = −0.705 against −0.769 under\nforward-chaining, over 38 replications. The difference is 0.063.\nThis is the second null of the block, and it is a narrower null than it looks. What the contrast establishes is\nthat using future blocks to fit the nuisance models costs nothing detectable here; what it does not establish\nis that either estimator is doing well. Both return roughly −0.74 against a panel whose true θ is −1.105\nbefore week 80 and −0.510 after it, so both are recovering a blend of two regimes rather than either one,\nand they blend it almost identically. The cost of the tolerance is nil because the tolerance is not what binds:\nthe binding problem under a break is that a single elasticity is being fitted to two, and no partition of the\nfolds repairs that. The implied policy, re-estimating within the regime that follows the most recent break, is\nunchanged and blocked at a different point: Appendix C.2 reports that no configuration tested locates the\nboundary.\n\nC.5\n\nThe sign filter and its replacement\n\nThe statistical validation layer of Appendix B.18 retains only estimates that are significant and negative,\nand that filter composes with the admissibility gate and with the shrinkage, so the published distribution of\nelasticities is truncated at more than one point. The measurement is made on a low-variation configuration\nwhere the filter bites, with four movements of 1.0 to 2.5% at a consumer elasticity of −1.0, yielding θ = −0.85.\nOver 125 replications the filter retains 0.51 of the estimates and the distribution of retained θ̂ sits at −0.682\nagainst a mean of −0.452 over all estimates, so the published mean is displaced from the truth by 0.168 while\nthe unfiltered mean is displaced by 0.398.\nThe underlying distribution is attenuated before any filtering occurs: the filter discards the estimates nearest\nzero, and two errors partially cancel, which is why the filtered displacement looks smaller than the unfiltered\none despite the filter selecting on the estimate’s own realization. A second experiment contrasts the sign\nfilter against a priori criteria that do not condition on the estimate’s own realization, over 25 replications on\nthe same configuration. Under the boosted learner, retaining on the partial R2 keeps 0.96 of estimates and\nmoves the published mean by 0.022 against the unfiltered mean; adding a variation criterion changes nothing\nfurther; the sign filter keeps 0.20 and moves it by −1.293. The selection bias of the a priori criterion is smaller\nthan that of the sign filter by a factor of roughly sixty, and it is small for a structural reason rather than by\nluck: a criterion evaluated before the estimate is realized cannot truncate the distribution of that estimate.\nReplacing the sign filter with the a priori criterion is the change this section makes, and what the a priori\ncriterion exposes is the attenuation the sign filter had been concealing: with the a priori criterion in place\nthe published mean sits at 0.037 against a truth of −0.857, a displacement of 0.894, against an unfiltered\ndisplacement of 0.872 on this configuration – nearly the same, because a criterion evaluated before the\nestimate is realized retains almost all of it (0.96) rather than the sign filter’s 0.20. Removing a selection rule\nthat flattered the output is the correct change and it makes the output look worse, which is the expected\nconsequence of removing a selection rule that flattered the output.\n\nC.6\n\nThe Dual Beta is retired\n\nThe asymmetric-response estimator carries an unresolved marker in its specification: Appendix A.3 specifies\na decomposition on the deflated price while the validation below exercises it on the nominal one as well, so\nthe choice of which the production implementation should run is not yet settled by specification alone. This\nsection settles it by retiring the estimator rather than by choosing between the two specifications.\nOver 28 replications per cell the experiment injects an asymmetry of zero and then of 0.25, and runs both\n\n67\n\n\fspecifications against each. With no asymmetry injected the detection rate is the estimator’s size: 0.321 on\nthe deflated specification and 0.286 on the nominal. With an asymmetry injected that implies a true gap of\n0.557, the detection rate is its power: 0.357 deflated and 0.321 nominal. Power exceeds size by 0.036 and\n0.035 respectively. The estimator declares an asymmetry in roughly a third of panels that contain none, and it\nis barely more likely to declare one when an asymmetry is present than when it is absent. The estimated gap\nunder the null is 0.262 on the deflated specification and 0.237 on the nominal, against mean interval widths\nof 1.311 and 1.389; the event counts and collinearity are not what limit it on the deflated specification, at\n24.5 upward and 25.4 downward events on average against a declared minimum of 20 and a variance inflation\nfactor of 1.26, though the nominal specification’s event counts split far more unevenly between directions, at\n99.1 upward against 32.4 downward, and still returns the same verdict.\nThat the two specifications return the same verdict is what settles the question the specification left open.\nWhich of the two the implementation runs is not a question the paper needs to resolve, because resolving it\nwould not rescue an estimator whose power and size are indistinguishable on this panel. The Dual Beta is\ntherefore withdrawn from the published output on this evidence. The lookup table loses its per-direction\neffect band and carries the symmetric response only; the asymmetry ratio that scaled it in the pooling layer\nis removed with it; and a noise floor of roughly 0.25 is recorded as the smallest gap a panel of this length and\nevent count could establish, which is larger than any asymmetry a committee would act on. The architecture\nremains specified in Appendix A.3 for a panel that can support it, and it is not run on one that cannot.\nA further experiment asks what kind of panel would support the estimator rather than retire it outright.\nOver 300 replications, running the identical decomposition against a design where shocks are blocked and\nof fixed magnitude, half increases and half decreases, one per temporal block, rather than drawn from the\nobservational panel, changes the picture entirely: the size falls from 0.353 under the observational design\nto 0.027 under the blocked design, and at an injected asymmetry the blocked design recovers 99.6% of the\ntrue gap against 53.9% for the observational one, with detection power of 0.797 against 0.297. The variance\ninflation factor moves the other way, from 1.14 to 3.10, because blocking the shock’s timing correlates the upand down-movement regressors more tightly; the recovery gain dominates that cost. The estimator’s problem\nwas therefore never the decomposition itself, which recovers an injected asymmetry cleanly once the design\nassigns shocks rather than merely observing them; it is the observational panel’s shock timing, which the\nretirement in this section leaves to a genuinely randomized or blocked price experiment to supply.\n\nC.7\n\nTwo nulls that were re-run under conditions designed to break them\n\nTwo of the system’s layers return a null result on the production generator, and a null whose only support\nis that nothing happened on one generator is weak evidence on its own. Both are therefore also run on\ngenerators built specifically to produce the effect they otherwise fail to find.\nPass-through as a source of decision-relevant variance. The system reports pass-through as a process;\non the production generator its contribution to the variance of the published response band never rises above\na fraction of a percent, which makes the elaborate treatment hard to justify on that panel alone. Injecting\nthe heterogeneity whose absence is the obvious explanation, a ρ that varies across regions with a standard\ndeviation of 0.1715, an order of magnitude above the bootstrap standard error of 0.0156, and in one scenario a\nρ that depends on the shopkeeper’s margin state, does not move the attribution. At a 20% list movement the\npass-through share of the response band’s variance is 0.0059 under state-dependent heterogeneity and 0.0007\nunder regional heterogeneity alone; at 10% it is 0.0016 and 0.0002. The null holds under conditions designed\nto break it, a considerably stronger result than a null measured on the production panel alone, and the\nimplication for the artifact is to publish a single pass-through number with its regime classification rather than\na variance decomposition that attributes nothing. The estimator’s own recovery is worse under the injected\nheterogeneity than on the unperturbed panel: a bias of −0.048 under state-dependent heterogeneity and\n−0.015 under regional heterogeneity alone, against −0.007 on the homogeneous baseline, over 35 replications\nper scenario – still small relative to a true ρ near 0.85, but not the flat recovery a single-scenario report would\nsuggest.\n\n68\n\n\fThe cash-pressure screen. The screening test for a liquidity constraint returns no statistic on the\nproduction panel: it executes in all thirteen replications and the t is non-finite in every one. This is not a\ndefect of the test. The dependent variable is portfolio breadth, the count of presentations with a positive\norder in a region-week, and on a generator where every presentation is ordered every week that count is\nconstant: its standard deviation is exactly 0, the regression has nothing to explain, and a non-finite t is the\ncorrect output. Re-running with order refusal injected gives the variable variation, and the test then works:\nin the weak-refusal scenario, over 5 replications, the breadth has a standard deviation of 1.755 and the screen\npasses in every replication at a mean |t| of 45.58. That figure is not evidence of power and is not presented as\nsuch, because breadth is generated as a direct function of the index in these scenarios, so the test recovers a\nrelation that was imposed. The informative cell is the one with regional income variation and no refusal:\nthere the breadth is again constant, the t is −0.5, and the screen correctly declines to admit the index. The\nscreen is therefore operative and correctly calibrated, and what the production panel lacks is the phenomenon\nrather than the instrument.\n\nC.8\n\nThe demand system, with inference\n\nThe cross-elasticity layer travels with standard errors from a block bootstrap specified in Appendix A.9,\nrun jointly over the residualization and the demand stage, over 8 replications on eight presentations, so its\ntheoretical restrictions are tested against a null rather than merely contrasted against the dispersion of the\nestimated coefficients, and the layer can be reported as a demand system rather than as a matrix.\nThe matrix carries statistical content, but less than the validation layer requires: only 18.5% of the cross\ncoefficients have |t| > 1.96 against bootstrap standard errors, short of the declared minimum of 20%. The\nmean own-price coefficient is −4.167 against a mean absolute cross coefficient of 0.586, so the own effects\nstill dominate in magnitude, and the smallest revenue share is 0.122, which bounds the amplification the\nMarshallian conversion applies to the noisiest row.\nTable D.12 reports the three restrictions against their null distributions. Adding-up is satisfied almost exactly,\nat a mean violation of 0.0000 and a Wald statistic of 0.160 on one degree of freedom (p = 0.759), rejected\nin none of the replications: this is the restriction the estimator imposes by construction, and its behaviour\nconfirms the bootstrap is doing what it claims. Homogeneity carries a mean violation of 0.856 with a Wald\nstatistic of 21.39 on seven degrees of freedom (p = 0.079), rejected at the 5% level in 0.625 of replications.\nSymmetry carries a mean violation of 0.241, a Wald statistic of 59.60 on twenty-eight degrees of freedom\n(p = 0.133), rejected in 0.625. Both are rejected in most of the eight replications, and the honest reading is\nthat on this panel the demand system does not satisfy the restrictions economic theory places on it. The\npublished matrix therefore travels with its bootstrap standard errors, with the three Wald statistics, and with\nthe statement that two of the three restrictions are rejected in most replications and that the cross-coefficient\nsignificance rate itself falls short of the validation layer’s own bar.\n\nC.9\n\nThe experimental layer against panel width\n\n\u0001\nThe experimental layer of Appendix B.19 faces a combinatorial floor at six regions: only 62 = 15 ways to\nassign two treated regions exist, so the exact permutation p-value cannot fall below 1/15 = 0.067 regardless\nof the data, and the synthetic control has few donors to draw on. The run measures the layer at four panel\nwidths, over 300 replications each, always enumerating the assignment exhaustively rather than sampling it,\nand the scaling is the clearest positive result in the paper.\nTable D.13 reports six, twelve, twenty and thirty regions, holding the number of treated regions fixed at two\nthroughout. The combinatorial floor on the exact p-value falls from 0.0667 through\n0.0152 and 0.0053 to\n\u0001\n0.0023, as the number of exhaustively enumerated assignments rises from 62 = 15 through 66 and 190 to\n\u0001\n30\n2 = 435. The pre-treatment fit of the synthetic control improves from a root mean squared error of 0.1888\nto 0.1332, and the ratio of that fit to the placebo effect it is being used to certify falls from 2.343 to 2.182.\nThe number of donors carrying non-zero weight rises from 3.85 to 12.09 (an effective, Herfindahl-based count\nof 3.16 to 7.65), so the “synthetic” qualifier becomes more substantive as the panel widens. The false-positive\nrate at the nominal 5% level moves from 0.000 at six regions, an arithmetic consequence of the combinatorial\nfloor rather than a property of the test, to 0.060 at twelve and thirty and 0.033 at twenty, within sampling\n69\n\n\frange of the nominal 0.05 at this replication count and the first evidence that the layer’s inference is calibrated\nat all once the floor stops binding. Detection power against a 0.12 log-point shock injected after treatment\nrises from exactly 0.000 at six regions, where the floor mechanically forecloses it, to 0.203 at twelve, 0.207 at\ntwenty and 0.227 at thirty.\nThe placebo estimate itself – the synthetic control’s average treatment effect under no true treatment – is\n0.081, 0.070, 0.064 and 0.061 in log volume at the four widths respectively, falling as the panel widens and\nthe donor pool improves, which is the behaviour a well-specified placebo should show.\nThis is a validity check on the system’s own experimental-comparison machinery, not a general solution to\nthe limits on the observational panel that Section 6.5 reports. The holdout and synthetic-control construction\nsupplies an independent way to confirm the effect of an already-executed price change, and its resolution is\nsensitive to how many independently assigned units back it, improving across the six, twelve, twenty and\nthirty regions Table D.13 reports. A committee planning a holdout of this kind should size it against the\nmeasured values in that table rather than assume a construction usable at thirty regions is equally usable at\nsix; the general relationship between panel width and attainable resolution, and what it implies at widths\nsmaller than tested here, is developed in companion methodological work.\n\nC.10\n\nImplementation challenges, governance and the pilot\n\nThree challenges dominated the implementation. The first is the internal coherence of the system itself: in\na system with resampling at multiple points, a single component that shuffles rows (an i.i.d. bootstrap, a\nrandom K-fold, a conformal bridge that mixes residuals) suffices for optimism to leak into everything that\nconsumes its outputs; the audit that unified the temporal resampling policy across all components was as\nvaluable as any estimator improvement. The second is the discipline of declaring estimands: the difference\nbetween the competitor-conditional elasticity and the equilibrium response, or between the list elasticity\nand the consumer elasticity, is invisible in a report that only shows “the elasticity,” and making it explicit\n(with labels traveling all the way to the dashboard) changed committee conversations that were previously\ninconclusive.\nOne finding emerged only from executing the newly instrumented controls, and it argues for building such\ncontrols before they are needed rather than when they are. The false-positive rate of the exact permutation\ntest is mechanically zero at six regions, an arithmetic consequence of the combinatorial floor rather than\nevidence the test is well calibrated, and only becomes informative once the panel widens enough for the floor\nto stop binding (Appendix C.9); a team that read the six-region number at face value could mistake the\ntest for conservative when it is merely unable to reject anything. Sizing a holdout against the measured\nresolution in Table D.13, rather than assuming a construction usable at thirty regions is equally usable at six,\nis the operational lesson, and it is one that only surfaces by running the width sweep rather than a single\nconfiguration.\nThe third challenge is organizational, and the validation run has changed what it requires. Planners accustomed\nto experience-based decisions rightly distrust recommendations they cannot interrogate, a reluctance that\nintensifies once the model has been seen to err [Dietvorst et al., 2015]. The design answer was the same one\nthe enterprise-systems literature reports [Llenas et al., 2026]: transparency and interactivity (scenarios, visible\nbands, verdicts with readable reasons, and the ability to override while seeing the quantified consequences),\nwhich is precisely the lever experimental work identifies as restoring willingness to use an imperfect model\n[Dietvorst et al., 2018], and, distinctively here, explicit abstention. The conversation a committee must\nbe prepared for is harder than a simple decline-to-recommend framing suggests. It is not “the system will\nsometimes decline to recommend” but “the system cannot, on the evidence this channel supplies, publish a\nper-presentation elasticity with an interval narrow enough to act on, and here is the level at which it can.” A\nsystem that says the first earns trust; a system that says the second earns it and changes the operating unit,\nwhich is a larger organizational ask and the honest one.\n\n70\n\n\fC.11\n\nFrom artifact to process: governance, adoption, and expected impact\n\nA critical design factor is that the artifacts feed a human-in-the-loop operating process, not an autopilot.\nThe target flow chains a periodic trigger at fixed cadence, the data system, the recommendation layer with\nits corridor, commercial validation with a documented override, the committee with minutes linked to the\nanalysis, and execution with ex-post measurement. Governance assigns explicit roles (the RGM analyst\noperates the application and documents exceptions; the pricing manager approves and owns the cycle; the\ncommittee decides) and typifies valid overrides (strategic, commercial, model-based, always documented)\nagainst the single invalid one: “we have always done it this way.” To these is added the cross-system convention\nof Appendix B.12: each alternative submitted to the committee declares its expected cost on the adjacent\ndecision systems, so that conflicts between models are adjudicated on the same sheet as the price.\nOne governance risk is created by the system’s own discipline and should be anticipated rather than discovered.\nAn engine designed to halt rather than degrade will return WAIT for a substantial share of a real portfolio,\nwhich is the intended behavior, and which reads to a committee expecting a price list as a malfunction. The\npressure that follows is predictable in any organization: relax a threshold for one category, waive a guard\nfor a strategic presentation, publish the midpoint because a number is required. The countermeasure is not\ntechnical. It is to publish an explicit degraded mode (“no recommendation, reason, date of next review”)\nrather than silence, and to accompany every abstention with the decision arithmetic of Appendix B.9, so that\nholding is legible as discipline rather than as incapacity.\nA second governance item follows directly from this validation, and it is the more consequential of the two.\nTwo of the system’s calibrations, the admissibility width threshold of 0.6 and the construction of the interval\nit reads, were set independently, and Section 6.5 reports that, across the levels of aggregation tested, intervals\nwide enough to cover tend to run one and a half to three and a half times wider than the threshold admits.\nA committee cannot be asked to adjudicate that as two separate switches. It is one decision, and it has\nthree defensible resolutions, each of which the committee should see costed: publish at a coarser unit, where\nthe interval is narrowest; publish per presentation with an interval that does not cover, and say so on the\nartifact; or supplement the observational panel with experimental variation, the path Appendix B.19 develops\nempirically and companion methodological work takes further. The first is available now and the second is\nwhat the system does today without declaring it. Presenting those options with numbers attached is what\nthis validation makes possible and what a recalibration exercise should start from.\nThe ex-post loop remains the most valuable asset of the system. Every real price change is a natural\nexperiment: measuring with discipline the actual versus predicted volume at 6–12 weeks and feeding that\ndata back into the next cycle’s training set is what makes the system improve on its own; if that loop is\nnot closed, everything else degrades silently. The residual uncertainty this validation isolates behaves like\ndispersion across designs rather than sampling noise within one, a distinction companion methodological\nwork [Delgado, 2026] develops formally; if that description is right, better estimation of the same panel would\nnarrow it only partially, and generating new designs would narrow it further.\nFor the pilot that would follow this work, a set of proposed success criteria is stated in advance rather\nthan reported as results, since none of them has been observed and the distinction between a target and\na measurement is exactly the kind of slippage this paper argues against. They are staged across process\n(full coverage of priority items with a recommended price by month six; fewer than five days from trigger\nto deliverable by month four), decision (100% documented overrides by month three; concordance between\nthe approved and recommended price above 0.80 by month twelve), model (forecast error below 15% by\nmonth nine, together with the QA Gate’s statistical thresholds), and business (a revenue increase of 2–5%\nby month nine with no share deterioration). The provenance of that range is stated exactly, because it is a\ntarget and not an estimate: the classic evidence establishes that price carries high leverage on profit [Marn\nand Rosiello, 1992] and the documented deployments establish that analytics-enabled pricing has delivered\nmaterial gains at scale [Deng et al., 2023, Llenas et al., 2026], but neither supplies an expected uplift for\nthis system in this channel. The 2–5% figure is a commercial ambition set by the sponsoring organization,\nand it is recorded here as the bar the pilot will be judged against rather than as a forecast the evidence\nsupports. One further criterion is added by the run: rather than assume a holdout of any size is adequate,\nsize it against the experimental layer’s own measured resolution, for which Table D.13 gives a concrete range\n\n71\n\n\fto plan against, between twelve and thirty independently assigned units in this panel. The roadmap is\notherwise incremental by design: a directional phase of contribution and communication, the phase this work\ndocuments, the scale-up of the multi-item portfolio optimizer, and only at the end dynamic pricing.\n\n72\n\n\fD\n\nSupplementary Tables and Empirical Diagnostics\n\nThis section collects the replication counts of the run, the pre-registered verdicts and their measured values,\nthe parameter-recovery metrics on both generators, the inference and hierarchy experiments, the guard\ndiagnostics, the portfolio abstention funnel, and the governance thresholds referenced in the main text.\nTable D.1: Replications per experiment in the run reported here,\nwith the identifier each experiment carries in the replication package.\nReps is the number of independent seeds; units is the numb