tag each: QB-BEHAVIOR, COACHING, OL, TRUST-SIGNAL, SCHEME, OTHER)\n- File is repo-infrastructure and governance; no QB, coaching, OL, scheme, or player/coach quote content → tag: OTHER (evidence-ladder and trust-gate infrastructure only, relevant only as the scaffolding that governs which engine claims are treated as supported).\n\n## Engine-actionable? (yes/no + one-line what)\nNo — governance/infra handoff; the only intake value is the reminder that unsupported-claim tracking (`UNSUPPORTED_CLAIMS.md`, `CLAIM_EVIDENCE_LEDGER.json`) and the \"do not trust yet\" list are the house standard for engine claims.\n"}
{"id": "corpus-intelligence/briefs/c01/reader-44/LABELING_WORKFLOWS.brief.md", "corpus": "corpus-intelligence", "kind": "brief", "chars": 2080, "equations": [], "text": "# fable/LABELING_WORKFLOWS.md\n\n## What it is (1-2 sentences)\nShort spec of the local-only human-labeling workflow for FABLE: a Zod-backed local manifest schema plus a local cost simulator, explicitly flagging that no AWS Ground Truth job, no labeling vendor, and no paid resources exist.\n\n## Key metrics/methods (formulas where given, else \"not specified\")\n- Allowed local pattern: (1) generate a manifest from uncertainty-ranked or rights-review candidates; (2) review locally; (3) store reviewed labels only after source rights and data-retention policy allow; (4) record cost assumptions separately from actual spend.\n- Manifest flags: `provider: local`, `priced: false`. No formulas.\n\n## Data sources named\n- New local workflow file: `apps/web/lib/fable/labeling.ts`. No data sources named.\n\n## Findings (numbers and facts, not vibes)\n- What exists: Zod-backed local manifest schema; local cost simulator for manual labeling/review; explicit local/priced-false flags.\n- What does not exist: no AWS Ground Truth job; no labeling-vendor integration; no paid resource activation; no AWS account mutation.\n- Connects directly to the second-pass gap-fills (Q2) finding that labeled data for football-specific classifiers (formations, routes, play boundaries) is the moat — this workflow is the local mechanism by which such labels would be generated and costed, with rights review gated in.\n\n## Intelligence connections (tag each: QB-BEHAVIOR, COACHING, OL, TRUST-SIGNAL, SCHEME, OTHER)\n- Labeling infrastructure is the prerequisite for building the formation/route/play-boundary dataset that would feed coaching/scheme tendency profiles → tag: SCHEME (labeling pipeline as future scheme-intelligence producer).\n- No QB, OL, coaching, or trust-signal content present → tag: OTHER otherwise.\n\n## Engine-actionable? (yes/no + one-line what)\nYes — pair this manifest + cost-simulator pattern with the Q2 CV feasibility read: uncertainty-ranked local labeling is exactly how the \"labeled data as a byproduct of the content pipeline\" moat gets built for formation/route classification.\n"}
{"id": "corpus-intelligence/briefs/c01/reader-44/second_pass_gap_fills_2026_09_29.brief.md", "corpus": "corpus-intelligence", "kind": "brief", "chars": 7932, "equations": ["199–", "199, Essential", "499, Premium", "299–", "299, Growth", "449+); Podium", "599/mo; GatherUp from ~", "40 for 1,", "45/mo, Tukios tribute videos", "350/mo; funeral website packages: generic designer", "5,500, full branding + obituary site"], "text": "# engine/research/2026-09-29/second-pass-gap-fills-2026-09-29.md\n\n## What it is (1-2 sentences)\nEmpirical gap-fills for a leverage synthesis, answering five questions with sourced numbers (funeral printables, automated sports charting from broadcast video 2025–2026, QR/NFC live signage, local-business presence management pricing, tamper-evident pick tracking in the tout space). Q2 (sports CV state of the art) and Q5 (tout pick-tracking trust) are the engine-relevant sections.\n\n## Key metrics/methods (formulas where given, else \"not specified\")\n- Soccer CV pipeline pattern (2025–2026 open-source repos): YOLOv8/v11 detection → ByteTrack tracking → jersey-color K-means team assignment → pitch homography → formation/shape analysis. Reported **~27–32 FPS end-to-end on an RTX 4060** with per-stage benchmarks: detection ~14ms, pose ~11.5ms, tracking ~1.8ms. Not specified in the file which repo produced each exact benchmark; treat the stage breakdown as a reported pattern across the cited repos.\n- YOLOv8-based possession estimation from broadcast: **RMSE 4.87 on 20 full matches** (Sensors, Feb 2026).\n- Event/game-state detection patterns: replay start–end detection at **0.95 confidence**; VideoMAE game-state classification (service/play/no-play); SoccerNet as open broadcast-footage training substrate.\n- nflgsplat (active Sept 2026, most serious open NFL-broadcast CV project found): NFL All-22 footage → camera calibration from field paint → detection + BoT-SORT tracking → SMPLest-X pose → placement in field coordinates → free-viewpoint avatar-twin renders, all on a local RTX 4080. Independently cross-validated calibration with two methods: SMPL-X vs. YOLOv8-pose with vertical-body constraint agreeing at **p90 1.58–1.59 m**. One operator, \"genuine engineering.\"\n- Architecture feasibility read: vanishing-point/field-line calibration → homography is load-bearing in every cited 2026 repo (\"PitchHomography\" module); field-color masking and camera-cut/replay discrimination are the cheap prefilter stage before expensive detectors; Sharp Labs play-segmentation family maps 1:1 onto cutting the game into plays before GPU spend. Genuinely hard and unsolved openly: formation + route classification from broadcast (needs strategy labels) and robust play-boundary detection under broadcast editing (replays, cutaways, graphics wipes). Pipeline is prototype-able on consumer hardware today; the expensive part is labeled data for football-specific classifiers, not geometry.\n- Tout-space trust landscape: Betstamp's click-to-track workflow is permanently logged, \"can't be edited or deleted\" (verified-record feature, Sept 2026 tracker comparison); Action Network BetSync auto-imports sportsbook wagers; Pikkit/SlipSync do sportsbook-linked tracking; MolTrust uses SHA-256 commitments anchored on Base L2 targeting AI prediction agents. VSiN's tout guide documents double-siding (opposite sides to halves of list) and doctored records; a sportsbook-review forum documents monitoring sites taking bribes to pad results.\n- Presence-management incumbent pricing table (per location): Yext $199–$999/yr (Emerging $199, Essential $449, Complete $499, Premium $999, annual rent-model — listings can revert on cancel); Moz Local $199–$399/yr; Birdeye $299–$449/mo (Starter $299, Growth $399, Dominate $449+); Podium $249–$599/mo; GatherUp from ~$99/mo.\n- QR/NFC signage prices: 5,000+ Etsy \"QR code table sign\" listings at $4–$48; ZappyCards NFC Google-review stands $40 for 1, $20/ea at 10, 1,257 reviews at 4.97/5; Tagglu NFC tags (NXP NTAG213) with QR fallback (pricing unverified); funeralOne ~$45/mo, Tukios tribute videos $20/video, 1Director ~$350/mo; funeral website packages: generic designer $1,500–$5,500, full branding + obituary site $5,000–$15,000.\n\n## Data sources named\n- Soccer repos: smithaker10/football_analytics_cv, aviasoletechnologies/football-event-detection, yohannesth/pitch-control-cv (GitHub); SoccerNet (broadcast footage + tracking + jersey labels); Sensors (Feb 2026) YOLOv8 possession paper; asif-ikbal-protik/trainmatricx blog on NFL CV difficulty; sumedhk0/nflgsplat (RESULTS_first_end_to_end.md, HANDOFF.md); xclsvmedia.com Pikkit-vs-SlipSync-vs-Betstamp tracker (Sept 2026); Action Network BetSync; VSiN tout guide; sportsbookreview.com forum; moltycel/moltrust-api; wisernotify.com Moz-vs-Yext; vendr.com Yext marketplace; emitrr.com Birdeye pricing; G2 Yext pricing; Etsy/RFID Journal/ZappyCards. TAM for memorial printables explicitly NOT verified (sell-through rates unknown).\n\n## Findings (numbers and facts, not vibes)\n- American football is \"structurally the hardest mainstream sport for CV\": 22 players, coordinated movement from standing start, LOS pileups (\"densest occlusion scenario in mainstream sport\"), helmets defeating face-based re-ID, route/formation classification requiring strategy understanding. No open equivalent of SoccerNet exists for NFL broadcast (labeled routes/formations/play boundaries from TV footage); NFL's own tracking is RFID/Zebra (Next Gen Stats) — positional, not tactical. CV is the layer that adds formation/route/blocking classification, and that labeled data is the moat.\n- Expired patent ideas G1/G2/G4 (field-anchored telestration, classical prefilter, vanishing-point calibration) describe precisely the architecture the 2026 field converged on — the patents' value is as a design spec, not IP.\n- Automated 2–4 s field-anchored clips and play segmentation are feasible internal tooling; full automated charting (formations, routes, personnel) from broadcast remains research-grade without a labeled dataset — and that dataset, built as a byproduct of the content pipeline, is the actual long-term asset.\n- Bettors care about verified records (Betstamp's verified-capper social layer used by handicappers on Whop; Action's expert tracking), NOT demonstrably about the cryptographic mechanism — no evidence bettors choose a capper over hash chains vs. app-enforced immutability.\n- Trust play for GSE: \"tamper-evident public record\" differentiates against self-reporting touts, but Betstamp already occupies \"can't be edited or deleted\" as a shipping feature; hash-chained daily digests are cheap to build and make the record provably unedited — brand infrastructure, not a marketing claim; market it as \"independently verifiable record,\" not \"blockchain.\"\n- Public/private doctrine check: nothing in the doc contradicts it — all GSE items remain internal capability; the pick-trail is the one legitimate public artifact (it publishes outcomes, which the doctrine explicitly allows).\n\n## Intelligence connections (tag each: QB-BEHAVIOR, COACHING, OL, TRUST-SIGNAL, SCHEME, OTHER)\n- The entire CV architecture section feeds future automated charting of formations/routes/personnel from broadcast — that pipeline, once the labeled dataset exists, is what would eventually produce formation, play-action, motion, and blitz rate data at scale → tag: COACHING, SCHEME (as future intake infrastructure, not current metrics).\n- No open NFL dataset exists for routes/formations from TV footage; labeled-data moat claim → tag: SCHEME (data-gap intel).\n- Betstamp/Action/BetSync/VSiN trust findings → tag: TRUST-SIGNAL (evidence for why an independently verifiable GSE pick record matters and how competitors already occupy parts of it).\n- Funeral-printable, QR/NFC signage, and presence-management pricing sections → tag: OTHER (revenue-lane research, not engine intelligence).\n- No QB behavioral, OL, or player/coach trust-quote content present in this file.\n\n## Engine-actionable? (yes/no + one-line what)\nYes — the Q2 CV stack (YOLOv8/v11 + ByteTrack/BoT-SORT + SMPLest-X + homography, prototyped on consumer GPUs) is the concrete blueprint for GSE's own broadcast-charting pipeline; the hard gap to resource is labeled formation/route data from broadcast, which the doc explicitly flags as the moat to build as a byproduct of the content pipeline.\n"}
{"id": "corpus-intelligence/briefs/c01/reader-45/EDGE_CANDIDATE_BACKLOG.brief.md", "corpus": "corpus-intelligence", "kind": "brief", "chars": 1561, "equations": [], "text": "# fable/edge-lab/EDGE_CANDIDATE_BACKLOG.md\n## What it is (1-2 sentences)\nTemplate/spec for the edge-candidate backlog: each candidate must define hypothesis, why incumbents may miss it, source type, data required, legal/TOS risk, cost to test, metric affected, expected improvement, minimum detectable effect, sample size needed, overfit risk, measurement method, falsification rule, decision; backlog source is MICRO_EDGE_HARVEST_LEDGER.md.\n## Key metrics/methods (formulas where given, else \"not specified\")\nnot specified — fields are templates, no formulas. Defaults: expected improvement \"Unknown until replayed\"; cost \"Zero for fixture/local tests\", owner decision required for paid feeds.\n## Data sources named\nBacklog source: MICRO_EDGE_HARVEST_LEDGER.md (source type recorded per candidate, not specified here).\n## Findings (numbers and facts, not vibes)\nNo findings; spec doc only. The required fields (minimum detectable effect, sample size, falsification rule, overfit risk) are the right rigor template for QB-behavior or coaching-tendency hypotheses before they enter any profile model.\n## Intelligence connections (tag each: QB-BEHAVIOR, COACHING, OL, TRUST-SIGNAL, SCHEME, OTHER)\nOTHER — methodology template; INFERENCE: the required-fields format (hypothesis → falsification rule → decision) should be adopted for every QB-behavioral and coaching-tendency candidate added to the intelligence program.\n## Engine-actionable? (yes/no + one-line what)\nno — template doc; adopt its candidate schema when logging QB/coaching/OL hypothesis candidates.\n"}
