{"id": "corpus-intelligence/briefs/c01/reader-55/ODDSPAPI_DEEP_DIVE.brief.md", "corpus": "corpus-intelligence", "kind": "brief", "chars": 7281, "equations": [], "text": "# docs/props/research/2026-09-18/firecrawl/ODDSPAPI-DEEP-DIVE.md\n## What it is (1-2 sentences)\nDeep-dive vendor research report (2026-09-18, Motif subagent, public sources only, no API key) on OddsPapi (oddspapi.io) — a sports-odds aggregation API by 55 Tech — covering pricing, endpoint inventory, NFL coverage depth, the NFL player-prop board structure, historical-odds mechanics/retention, WebSocket, latency, and terms. Every factual claim carries a CONFIRMED / INFERRED / UNVERIFIED label with source URL and access date.\n## Key metrics/methods (formulas where given, else \"not specified\")\nBilling: flat per-request (1 call = 1 request regardless of response size/filters); free tier 250 requests/month, all books incl. Pinnacle/Singbet, historical odds included at every tier; `/v4/historical-odds` is unmetered (does not increment usage); errors on billable endpoints (4xx/5xx) count as 1 request; after plan exhaustion, all endpoints except `/account` blocked with 429. Per-endpoint cooldowns (CONFIRMED): /v4/odds 500ms, /v4/fixtures 2000ms, /v4/markets 1000ms, /v4/settlements 2000ms, /v4/historical-odds 5000ms. 429 body is valid JSON with `error.retryMs` — honor it.\nClose-line semantics (CONFIRMED, no `is_closing` flag): close = last snapshot with `active=true` AND `createdAt < startTime` (snapshots continue in-play, e.g. Pinnacle); dedupe consecutive identical prices (heartbeats, not moves) before measuring movement. One Bet365 fixture example: 266 markets / 230,515 snapshots / 29,569 deduped moves. ETag/`If-None-Match` on finished fixtures (3-day cache, max-age=259200).\nProp-board engineering rules: key yardage props on (playerName, handicap), never market ID (ladder rungs); de-vig only rungs with both sides active; dedupe clone feeds by full price tuple (`cloneOf` metadata incomplete — NFL props 99 books → 39 independent groups, 60.6% reduction; largest group 21 slugs); filter `active=true` at price level; skip internal feeds (`pinnacle+*`, `demo`) and suspended books; resolve market IDs by name via /markets (same bet ships under multiple IDs — anytime TD under market IDs 14388 and 144637).\n## Data sources named\n- OddsPapi direct API: base `https://api.oddspapi.io/v4`, auth = `apiKey` query param (never a header). Endpoints: /v4/fixtures, /v4/fixture, /v4/odds, /v4/odds-by-tournaments, /v4/historical-odds, /v4/markets, /v4/settlements, /v4/sports, /v4/tournaments, /v4/bookmakers, /v4/participants, /v4/players, /v4/languages, /v4/scores, /v4/account; WebSocket `wss://api.oddspapi.io/v4/ws` (paid/B2B only).\n- Fixture `externalProviders` crosswalk fields: betradarId, mollybetId, opticoddsId, lsportsId, txoddsId, sofascoreId, betgeniusId, flashscoreId, pinnacleId, oddinId.\n- Settlements: `result` ∈ WIN | LOSE | HALFWIN | HALFLOSS | PUSH | CANCELLED | UNDECIDED.\n- All sources enumerated in §16 (official docs/blog URLs + 11 third-party GitHub repos).\n## Findings (numbers and facts, not vibes)\n- NFL coverage: sportId 14, NFL tournamentId 31 with 255 scheduled fixtures; NCAA tournamentId 27653 (2,745 fixtures); CFL 790 (23); 100 market families on sportId 14. 237 bookmakers quoted Falcons vs Panthers (3 days pre-kickoff) → 79 independent prices after dedupe. Match-market ID counts: Total (incl. OT) 201, Handicap (incl. OT) 281, quarters 80 each (1st/2nd/3rd/4th incl. OT variants).\n- Week 1 2026 opener (Seahawks vs Patriots) prop board: 259 books quoted, 99 priced props, 8,508 prop prices, 5,391 active; 21 prop families (largest: receiving yards 2,176 prices, 62.5% active, 91 books, 8 players, 41 market IDs; rush yards 1,147, 53.9%; pass yards 888, 41.1%; anytime TD 830, 91.1% active, 32 books, 23 players, 2 IDs). Kupp receiving-yards ladder: 15 distinct active rungs (9.5 → 39.5+), one market ID per rung. `mainLine` flag unreliable for NFL alt lines (agreed with consensus spread only 46% of flags). `Over Under Rush Yards` (two-sided, de-viggable) vs bare `Rush Yards` (one-sided threshold ladder, not de-viggable) are distinct families.\n- Prop book depth (Week 1): FanDuel 321 prop prices (91.6% active) > Caesars/WilliamHill 250 > ESPNBet/theScore 233 > DraftKings 200 > Kalshi 110 (100% active — unusual for a prediction market). Pinnacle, Bet365, BetMGM, Betfair Exchange, Polymarket, SBOBet priced ZERO props — game lines only; no sharp reference price exists on the NFL prop board. Anytime-TD payout gap on Kupp: 3.00 FanDuel → 4.37 betfirst.be (+45.7%).\n- Timing: FanDuel game lines entered feed Aug 9; props entered Aug 31 00:27:16 UTC — 10 days before kickoff, 22 days after game lines. DraftKings 00:31:48, Caesars/ESPNBet 00:34:19, Kalshi 00:57:40 UTC the same morning (5 independent operators opened props within ~30 min = scheduled jobs). DK had posted an anytime-TD board June 2–4 then pulled it (60 snapshots). Rule: schedule the prop scanner against kickoff, not the calendar.\n- Retention conflict (resolve live before backfill engineering): official docs claim \"all historical odds since January 2026\" (~8–9 months); independent May 2026 probe (cleatiq, soccer) measured ~90-day rolling window; provider's own Sept 16 blog confirmed 8 months for EPL and stated \"rolling window, not a permanent archive.\"\n- Terms (CONFIRMED): \"You may not resell, repackage, or redistribute our data as a standalone product\"; abuse monitoring covers scraping; payments non-refundable; terms change anytime. GSE implication: internal analytics safe lane; any public display of odds needs a legal read.\n- Latency (provider methodology): sharps (Pinnacle) and major softs (Bet365) targeted as close to 1 second as possible over WebSocket; soft books with anti-bot protection 4–10s; NFL explicitly named as high-profile pregame polling priority. Their 60-second-delay claim about The Odds API is vendor marketing, UNVERIFIED independently.\n- Recommended GSE lane split: The Odds API = primary current-line feed + failover anchor; OddsPapi = (1) historical CLV reconstruction, (2) NFL prop-market discovery + cross-book comparison, (3) Pinnacle-limit-anchored fair probabilities, (4) book-breadth disagreement checks. Current GSE `picks` table supports only SPREAD/MONEYLINE/TOTAL — props require a schema extension before any prop lane goes live.\n## Intelligence connections (tag each: QB-BEHAVIOR, COACHING, OL, TRUST-SIGNAL, SCHEME, OTHER)\n- QB-BEHAVIOR — prop-board structure for pass yards, TD passes, completions, attempts, interceptions families; QB prop ladders keyed on (playerName, handicap).\n- TRUST-SIGNAL — CLV reconstruction method (unmetered historical-odds + close-line derivation rule); Pinnacle limits for true-probability anchoring; sharp-vs-soft book segregation (no sharp props); cross-book disagreement checks.\n- SCHEME — prop-market family taxonomy (21 families) as a scheme-aware prop-discovery surface; half/quarter market ladders.\n- OTHER — vendor identity, billing/cooldown mechanics, terms, retention, latency.\n## Engine-actionable? (yes/no + one-line what)\nYes — the unmetered `/v4/historical-odds` endpoint plus the close-line derivation rule (last active snapshot with createdAt < startTime, dedupe heartbeats) is a free CLV-reconstruction pipeline, and the prop-ladder keying rules ((playerName, handicap) not market ID; price-tuple dedupe) are directly usable scheduler/integration engineering.\n"}
{"id": "corpus-intelligence/briefs/c01/reader-55/galaxy_studio_spec.brief.md", "corpus": "corpus-intelligence", "kind": "brief", "chars": 3232, "equations": [], "text": "# docs/product/galaxy-studio-spec.md\n## What it is (1-2 sentences)\nPhase 3 product spec for \"Galaxy Studio\" — a content-generation workflow that turns one unit of intelligence (one game or slate) into eight monetizable creator assets (fan explainer, fantasy angle, betting education, X thread, TikTok script, newsletter block, sponsor-safe blurb, YouTube title/thumbnails). It runs on the Claude API over the Intelligence Graph with a three-layer compliance scanner, mandatory human review, and hard refusals on auto-posting and unsupported claims.\n## Key metrics/methods (formulas where given, else \"not specified\")\nNot specified as formulas. Compliance scanner returns `ComplianceScanResult` = {status: 'green'|'yellow'|'red', flags[], publicReady (true only when green)}; each flag = {layer 1|2|3, severity block|warn|info, span, message, suggestion}. Three compliance layers: (1) platform-wide banned vocabulary, (2) unsupported-claim detection (regex-tagged statistics/win-streak/percentage patterns trigger citation requirement), (3) template-specific rules. Word-count budgets per asset: fan explainer 250–400, fantasy angle 200–350, betting education 300–500, X thread 5–7 posts, TikTok script 45–90 seconds, newsletter block 400–700, sponsor-safe blurb 100–200, YouTube 8–12 titles + 3–5 thumbnail concepts. Eval coverage: 3 evals per template (happy path, thin-evidence refusal, planted banned term). Acceptance criterion #9: banned-vocabulary scan returns zero hits across a 50-game sample.\n## Data sources named\n- Intelligence Graph: `GameIntelligenceNode`, `SlateWeather`, `PickSignalSnapshot`, `GameSignal` rows (every asset must carry ≥1 citation to these).\n- Claude API (sole LLM vendor per DEC-020).\n- `docs/positioning.md` banned vocabulary list (scanner Layer 1 reference).\n## Findings (numbers and facts, not vibes)\n- 8 asset templates shipped in Phase 3 v0; Studio lives at `/cockpit/studio`, operator-only, no public route.\n- Betting education template always closes with the exact line: *\"Whether to bet this is your call. What it teaches you about the market is the point.\"*\n- Five hard refusals: (1) no auto-post endpoint (no \"Send to Twitter\" button), (2) no generation without ≥1 citation (\"Evidence is thin — no asset generated\"), (3) no generation against bootstrap-only games, (4) no recommendations even when a published pick exists, (5) gated games must open with \"We considered this game and did not publish — here's why.\"\n- 9 acceptance criteria gate Studio v0 green; open items OPEN-STUDIO-1..3 (batch generation default yes; persist CreatorAsset rows with 90-day TTL; re-runnable compliance scan on edited content).\n- Post-v0 roadmap: Canva, HyperFrames/TikTok video drafts, Slack/Gmail draft routing, Beehiiv API — all deferred to Phase 4+.\n- Code ownership: Codex owns runtime (`apps/web/app/cockpit/studio/`, `apps/web/lib/studio/`); Claude owns templates + voice.\n## Intelligence connections (tag each: QB-BEHAVIOR, COACHING, OL, TRUST-SIGNAL, SCHEME, OTHER)\n- OTHER — content-generation product spec and compliance pipeline; no player, coach, line, or scheme data.\n## Engine-actionable? (yes/no + one-line what)\nNo — product/compliance spec for creator content, not a data or modeling feed.\n"}
{"id": "corpus-intelligence/briefs/c01/reader-55/our_metric_stack.brief.md", "corpus": "corpus-intelligence", "kind": "brief", "chars": 6143, "equations": [], "text": "# docs/props/research/2026-09-17/props-consensus/our-metric-stack.md\n## What it is (1-2 sentences)\nThe GSE lab's plain-language inventory of every NFL number it computes — data sources, sample, filters, the full metric family list, known limitations, and how raw numbers become projections. Written 2026-09-17; computed data only, no mock data. This is the methodological bible for Garrett's props/metric pipeline.\n## Key metrics/methods (formulas where given, else \"not specified\")\nFilters (exact sieve): (1) regular season only; (2) pass/run plays only; (3) no kneels (453 removed in 2025) or spikes (82 removed); (4) garbage time excluded = 4th-quarter plays with possession-team win probability >0.95 or <0.05 — removed 11.2% of 2025 plays; overtime kept; (5) Success = EPA > 0 (nflverse `success` flag matched `epa > 0` on 100% of pass/run plays, zero mismatches); (6) Dropback = pass attempt OR scramble (sacks count as attempts; scrambles count as dropbacks per 4for4); (7) Explosive = 15+ yards on dropback or 10+ yards on designed rush (4for4 convention).\nKey formulas: Expected INTs = league-average INT rate per dropback × team dropbacks; 2025 baseline 1.867%. Expected fumbles lost = league-average fumble-lost rate per play × team plays; 2025 baseline 0.640%. Luck meter = actual minus expected (negative = lucky, positive = unlucky). League percentiles: 100 = best, 0 = worst; lower-is-better metrics inverted. Turnover-worthy-play rate = FTN `is_interception_worthy` per dropback (2025 only): ~3.0% of dropbacks flagged; 52.3% of flagged throws became actual INTs. Pressure-to-sack conversion is near-pure luck: R² < 0.005 → explicit veto on individual sack props. Fumble recovery year-to-year correlation ~0.00.\nProjection recipe: base prior = 2025 full-season per-game means; Week 1 2026 = role-check only, 0% efficiency weight; volume script-adjusted via measured 2025 dropback rates by win-probability bucket; garbage-time corrected via measured unfiltered/filtered per-game ratio multiplier per stat; every projection carries uncertainty bands; unsupported rows NULL with a one-line reason; H1/H2/H3 pre-registration bar (three hypotheses killed on honest nulls in the props lab).\n## Data sources named\n- nflverse play-by-play (CC-BY 4.0; EPA columns from nflfastR Expected Points multinomial logistic regression, taken as published, not re-estimated).\n- FTN charting subset via nflverse — `is_interception_worthy` (2025 only, joined on game_id+play_id at 100% coverage; CC-BY-SA 4.0, credit \"FTN Data via nflverse\").\n- Scripts: `compute_team_metrics.py`, `compute_advanced_metrics.py` in `~/workspace/gse-research/nfl-2026/`.\n## Findings (numbers and facts, not vibes)\n- Sample: 2025 full regular season = 46,452 plays → 29,239 after filters (285 games in file including postseason, excluded). 2026 Week 1 = 2,756 raw → 1,673 after filters (~45–65 plays per team).\n- Metric families computed per team per season: EPA/play (per dropback, per designed rush, defensive sign-flipped); success rate; explosive-play rate; EPA distributions (median, 10/25/75/90th percentiles, share negative-EPA, share chunk EPA>1.0); league percentiles; down splits (early 1st–2nd vs late 3rd–4th); drive stats; stuff rate; air-vs-YAC EPA splits (+ air yards per dropback); late-and-close EPA/success (4Q, WP 0.20–0.80, ~50–80 plays per team per season, always reported with play count); unit matchups; weekly trends (team × week × 18 weeks 2025 = 544 rows); turnover components; TWP rate; pressure proxies (QB-hit rate, sack rate per dropback — floors only, hurries invisible).\n- League 2025 norms: 2.10 points per drive, 24.0% TD rate, 20.4% three-and-out. Three-and-out defined as exactly 3 plays ending in a punt. Drive stats computed on unfiltered sample (includes punts/FGs/garbage-time drives), not comparable to filtered EPA sample.\n- 2025 Dallas textbook luck case: 4th in EPA/play, 7-9-1 record, defense 4.6 INTs below expected.\n- No opponent adjustment: unadjusted EPA had Jacksonville #1 after Week 1 2026 (beat Cleveland) while FTN's DVOA ranked San Francisco #1 after adjustment — DVOA-style adjustment is the documented next step. Cross-unit EPA used as qualitative modifier only, never smuggled into numbers.\n- Week 1 2026 caught real structural breaks: Montgomery now playing for Houston, DJ Moore for Buffalo.\n- Proprietary posture: PFF grades, DVOA tables, Next Gen Stats used to learn/sanity-check only (e.g., own Seattle defensive EPA matched The Athletic's TruMedia report; Buffalo EPA rank within filter noise of PFF) — never republished.\n- Legal constraint on video: Richardson v. Townsquare Media, 2d Cir. 2026-04-23 → real-footage clips stay 2–4 seconds, commentary-led, never the whole play.\n## Intelligence connections (tag each: QB-BEHAVIOR, COACHING, OL, TRUST-SIGNAL, SCHEME, OTHER)\n- QB-BEHAVIOR — turnover-worthy-play rate (~3.0% of dropbacks flagged, 52.3% conversion to INTs) as the INT-projection signal, not raw INT count; dropback convention (scramble vs designed rush); air-vs-YAC EPA splits per dropback.\n- COACHING — volume script-adjusted by 2025 dropback rates per win-probability bucket; early-vs-late down splits; late-and-close sample (4Q WP 0.20–0.80).\n- OL — stuff rate (designed rushes gaining zero or negative yards); pressure proxies as QB-hit/sack rate per dropback (floors; R²<0.005 conversion veto).\n- SCHEME — air-vs-YAC EPA splits, EPA distributions (boom-bust vs consistency), explosive-play thresholds (15+ dropback / 10+ rush).\n- TRUST-SIGNAL — H1/H2/H3 pre-registration bar (three hypotheses killed on honest nulls); \"a projection that disagrees with the market is a hypothesis, not an edge, until validated\"; unmodeled risks named in writing.\n- OTHER — licensing (CC-BY / CC-BY-SA), no-opponent-adjustment limitation, legal video constraint.\n## Engine-actionable? (yes/no + one-line what)\nYes — this is the engine's metric bible: baselines (1.867% INT/dropback, 0.640% fumble-lost/play), TWP rate (~3.0% flagged, 52.3%→INT), filter conventions (11.2% garbage-time removal, Success=EPA>0), and the R²<0.005 pressure-to-sack veto all feed engine calibration and prop pricing directly.\n"}
{"id": "corpus-intelligence/briefs/c01/reader-55/phase_6_plus_planning.brief.md", "corpus": "corpus-intelligence", "kind": "brief", "chars": 2782, "equations": [], "text": "# docs/product/phase-6-plus-planning.md\n## What it is (1-2 sentences)\nLightweight planning frame for Phase 6+ (network effects, federation, adjacent product lines) — 10 items sketched but not spec'd: white-label licensing, multi-contributor house picks, native mobile app, Apple Watch complication, WhatsApp alerts, year-in-review data viz, survival pool, calibration leaderboard, local/youth sports, free widgets. Full specs deferred until Phase 4 verification gate passes.\n## Key metrics/methods (formulas where given, else \"not specified\")\nNo formulas. Decision thresholds stated: Apple Watch complication only worth building at 10k+ mobile users; WhatsApp alerts deferred until Elite tier has 1k+ active users; year-in-review requires 12 months of per-user data; white-label licensing requires Phase 5 B2B API with ≥3 Enterprise customers. Calibration leaderboard concept: rank users by calibration accuracy, NOT win rate (explicit anti-tout metric); user calibration curves vs the model's curve; pre-show confidence prompts as the training mechanic.\n## Data sources named\nNone — planning doc, no data sources.\n## Findings (numbers and facts, not vibes)\n- 10 Phase 6+ items listed; 7 open decisions (OPEN-P6-1..7); verification gate requires Phase 5 gate passed + decision-log retrospective + real product metrics + owner-resolved decisions.\n- Six items explicitly NOT in scope for any phase as currently planned: paid social at scale, crypto/events markets, AI-assisted bet placement (auto-betting), aggressive sportsbook affiliate placement, white-labeling the Galaxy consumer brand, multi-asset prediction marketplace.\n- Compliance-relevant: survival pool cash prize requires gaming-law review; native mobile app must frame carefully for App Store sportsbook-adjacency policy; multi-contributor model keeps individual track records public and algorithmic picks as a separate vertical, with a \"Sharp Consensus\" view when contributors agree.\n- Trust-toolkit adjacent: calibration training surfaces (pre-show confidence prompt), calibration leaderboard as anti-tout metric, opt-in-only year-in-review with privacy default.\n## Intelligence connections (tag each: QB-BEHAVIOR, COACHING, OL, TRUST-SIGNAL, SCHEME, OTHER)\n- TRUST-SIGNAL — calibration leaderboard ranked by calibration accuracy not win rate; per-user calibration curves vs model; pre-show confidence prompts; individual contributor track records with Sharp Consensus aggregation.\n- OTHER — all remaining items (licensing, mobile, watch, WhatsApp, survival pool, youth sports, widgets).\n## Engine-actionable? (yes/no + one-line what)\nYes — calibration-leaderboard concept (rank users by calibration accuracy, not win rate) and per-user calibration curves are direct design inputs for the engine's calibration module.\n"}
{"id": "corpus-intelligence/briefs/c01/reader-55/sfdata9ers.brief.md", "corpus": "corpus-intelligence", "kind": "brief", "chars": 3422, "equations": [], "text": "# docs/props/research/2026-09-18/notes/sfdata9ers.md\n## What it is (1-2 sentences)\nSource notes (index read 2026-09-18) on @sfdata9ers — a popular X account posting NFL data-analytics charts, identity confirmed via SI articles embedding his posts — plus notes on the data sources behind his charts (FTN StatsHub charting, nflverse PBP, Mock Draft Database probability matrices, consensus-big-board reach factors).\n## Key metrics/methods (formulas where given, else \"not specified\")\nNot specified in the file except: \"relative reach factor\" (e.g. 2.76; reach = actual pick − consensus rank) and mock-draft probability matrices (e.g. Downs 55% available at Pick 10 → 7% at Pick 11 across 79 mock drafts April 9–16, each team's probabilities sum to 1). Aaron Schatz via FTN interview: route-denominator metrics (yards per route run) are more stable/predictive than target-denominator metrics. Kickoff-coverage chart methodology not found — INFERENCE: likely nflverse kickoff PBP (kickoff/return/touchback plays + next drive start yardline) or FTN ST charting; denominators/exclusions (penalties, onside kicks, return TDs, end-of-half) unknown. Josh Allen \"Career EPA/Play\" heatmap: axis labels not found — INFERENCE: most plausibly nflverse PBP Allen dropbacks/rushes with cell = mean EPA/play.\n## Data sources named\n- FTN StatsHub (ftnfantasy.com/nfl/stats): human-charted by in-house team; every play includes route run, motion, coverage, concept, pressure, stacked box, play situation, offense/defense/ST splits; filters: team, player, play concept, formation, play situation, coverage, stacked box, pressure. Proprietary, subscription.\n- Mock Draft Database (NFL Mock Draft Database) — availability gauges / full team-player probability matrices.\n- nflverse (https://github.com/nflverse) — probable basis of the kickoff-coverage and Allen EPA charts.\n- SI (si.com) articles embedding his X posts; NBC Sports fantasy draft-grades article citing his reach-factor list.\n## Findings (numbers and facts, not vibes)\n- Identity: \"SFdata9ers, a popular account on X that posts data insights and analytics on the NFL\" (SI, May 3 2026 cap-space chart).\n- FTN's per-play charting inventory includes route run, motion, coverage, concept, pressure, and stacked box — i.e., the same charted dimensions underpin the Week 1 playcalling-tendencies chart.\n- The reach-factor concept (actual pick − consensus rank) was cited by NBC Sports in 2026 NFL draft grades; 49ers got an F on his list.\n- Route-denominator stability rule (Schatz): yards per route run beats yards per target on stability/predictiveness — a standing receiver-metric design principle.\n## Intelligence connections (tag each: QB-BEHAVIOR, COACHING, OL, TRUST-SIGNAL, SCHEME, OTHER)\n- QB-BEHAVIOR — Josh Allen career EPA/play heatmap (nflverse-sourced dropbacks/rushes, cell = mean EPA/play) as a QB-behavioral visualization archetype.\n- SCHEME — Schatz's route-denominator rule (route-denominator metrics more stable/predictive); FTN charting dimensions (route, motion, coverage, concept, stacked box, pressure).\n- OTHER — source provenance for a third-party analytics creator; mock-draft probability matrices; kickoff-coverage methodology gap.\n## Engine-actionable? (yes/no + one-line what)\nYes — the Schatz route-denominator rule (prefer yards per route run over yards per target) is an actionable receiver-metric design principle for the engine's stability weighting.\n"}
{"id": "corpus-intelligence/briefs/c01/reader-56/advanced_matchups_deep_dive_2026_09_19.brief.md", "corpus": "corpus-intelligence", "kind": "brief", "chars": 8034, "equations": ["3,600; Schultz 8 targets W1 at", "3,700, Gesicki", "3,200, M. Washington", "3,600; top traps R. Rice", "6,500, Ja'Marr Chase", "5,600 (69th/72 separation, 1.33 YPRR), McLaurin", "40M, Cousins→Raiders QB, Brissett→ARI, Rush→ATL starter, Pittman→PIT, Kirk→SF, Deebo→SF; Nabers faces LAR zone (85.3%) not McDuffie; Darnold out/Lock starts SEA; Purdy out/Jones starts; Myles Garrett traded to LAR, IR 9/17; Donald reduced situational snaps).\n- Injury resolutions: Mason IR 9/16 (thumb, out 4+ games); Bowers doubtful → Mayer 6/7 W1 pivot; Z. Flowers doubtful; Nico Collins OUT (Grade 1 hamstring) → Schultz ELITE (", "6,400 RB8); Mayer PLAY ("], "text": "# research/2026-09-19-dk-week2/deep/advanced-matchups-deep-dive-2026-09-19.md\n## What it is (1-2 sentences)\nExhaustive 301-line DK Week-2 (2026-09-20/21, 15-game \"Ray's in Aruba\" slate, 75-player winner-take-all) advanced-matchup dossier: verdicts on all of Garrett's liked plays plus coverage tables, zone/gap RB scheme mismatches, DC-tendency corrections, and eight integrated web-research lanes (injury, coverage, advanced metrics, Farrell/Andrews participation, QB/WR-TE/RB full pools, defensive scheme).\n## Key metrics/methods (formulas where given, else \"not specified\")\n- Concentration/role metrics (2026 Week 1, StatRankings 2026-09-18 unless noted): Jefferson 37.5% target share, 52.9% first-read share, 100% route participation, 46.5% air-yard share, WOR 88.11, 22.1 xFP (#5, Sharp), 39.1% share on 97% routes; Bijan 45.5% target share (#2 behind JSN), 50.0% first-read, 84% backfield XFP (FantasyPoints bellcow), 81.5% dominator, 110 YAC, 57% zone; JSN 96% routes, 45.8% target share (led NFL), 42.3% TPRR, 4.69 YPRR; Puka 32.1% target share, 50% first-read, 0.47 TPRR, PFF #1 WR W1 (3.74 YPRR), 19.3 xFP vs 12.4 actual (+6.9 TD-regression candidate)